        ],
        "uncertain_name": "comebacker",
        "uncertain_mes": "In an earlier post, this blog examined malware from a DPRK-affiliated campaign targeting security researchers. Since the initial public post about this activity from Google, multiple vendors have corroborated and supplemented the technical details in this attack.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.440948361783487,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]ComeBacker is a malware family attributed to the Lazarus Group. It employs a multi-stage loader that decrypts and decompresses a final payload, which is executed via a call to its GetWindowSizedW export with the argument \"1282\". For command and control, it uses encrypted channels, specifically leveraging the HC256 stream cipher with keys like \"LH*x239udC<*sd_Sej%lOa0$&ujHl(.R\", and connects to infrastructure including domains like pypi.online, jdkgradle.com, and angeldoationblog.com, and the IP 91.206.178.125 on port 80. Its associated techniques include Timestomp (T1099) for defense evasion and Encrypted Channel (T1573) for C2. It has been delivered via supply chain compromise, exploiting vulnerabilities such as CVE-2023-42793.[/profile]"
    },
