        "gid": 101,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "bitter",
            "tropic trooper",
            "magic hound",
            "apt29"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1573",
        "uncertain_name": "encrypted channel",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may employ an encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Despite the use of a secure algorithm, these implementations may be vulnerable to reverse engineering if secret keys are encoded and/or generated within malware samples/configuration files.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.033299671223457,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Encrypted Channel is a Command and Control technique (Tactic: Command and Control, MITRE ID: T1573) applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, ESXi, and Network Devices. It involves adversaries using encryption algorithms like symmetric or asymmetric cryptography to conceal C2 traffic, independent of any inherent protocol security. This implementation can be vulnerable if secret keys are hardcoded within malware or configuration files, enabling potential reverse engineering. It is distinct from data encryption for exfiltration or impact and is directly related to foundational cryptographic methods."
    },
    "16226": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "command-and-control"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Proxy"
        ],
        "unique_id": "cb2c3b7f-4b9a-4eb7-a3a3-0a8fd197dda6",
        "name": "Proxy",
        "description": "Adversaries may use a connection proxy to direct network traffic between systems or act as an intermediary for network communications to a command and control server to avoid direct connections to their infrastructure. Many tools exist that enable traffic redirection through proxies or port redirection, including [HTRAN](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0040), ZXProxy, and ZXPortMap. (Citation: Trend Micro APT Attack Tools) Adversaries use these types of proxies to manage command and control communications, reduce the number of simultaneous outbound network connections, provide resiliency in the face of connection loss, or to ride over existing trusted communications paths between victims to avoid suspicion. Adversaries may chain together multiple proxies to further disguise the source of malicious traffic.\n\nAdversaries can also take advantage of routing schemes in Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) to proxy command and control traffic.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1090",
        "standard_attck_name": "Proxy",
        "gid": 102,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "polonium",
            "earth lusca",
            "gamaredon group",
            "contagious interview",
            "blue mockingbird",
            "magic hound",
            "copykittens",
            "fox kitten",
            "scattered spider",
            "sandworm team",
            "turla",
            "lapsus$",
            "cinnamon tempest",
            "moustachedbouncer",
            "windigo",
            "apt41",
            "volt typhoon"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1090",
        "uncertain_name": "proxy",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use a connection proxy to direct network traffic between systems or act as an intermediary for network communications to a command and control server to avoid direct connections to their infrastructure. Many tools exist that enable traffic redirection through proxies or port redirection, including [HTRAN](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0040), ZXProxy, and ZXPortMap. (Citation: Trend Micro APT Attack Tools) Adversaries use these types of proxies to manage command and control communications, reduce the number of simultaneous outbound network connections, provide resiliency in the face of connection loss, or to ride over existing trusted communications paths between victims to avoid suspicion. Adversaries may chain together multiple proxies to further disguise the source of malicious traffic.\n\nAdversaries can also take advantage of routing schemes in Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) to proxy command and control traffic.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 8.910100773402856,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity \"Proxy\" aligns with MITRE ATT&CK technique T1090 (Proxy) under the Command and Control tactic, applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, ESXi, and Network Devices. It involves using an intermediary, such as tools like HTRAN, ZXProxy, or ZXPortMap, to redirect network traffic and conceal direct connections to command and control servers. This can be implemented externally, internally within a compromised network, or as a multi-hop chain to obscure the traffic source and leverage trusted communication paths for evasion."
    },
    "16227": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion",
            "execution"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Scripting"
        ],
        "unique_id": "8a819513-1c1b-41a5-86e3-dbb073787234",
        "name": "Scripting",
        "description": "**This technique has been deprecated. Please use [Command and Scripting Interpreter](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059) where appropriate.**\n\nAdversaries may use scripts to aid in operations and perform multiple actions that would otherwise be manual. Scripting is useful for speeding up operational tasks and reducing the time required to gain access to critical resources. Some scripting languages may be used to bypass process monitoring mechanisms by directly interacting with the operating system at an API level instead of calling other programs. Common scripting languages for Windows include VBScript and [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1086) but could also be in the form of command-line batch scripts.\n\nScripts can be embedded inside Office documents as macros that can be set to execute when files used in [Spearphishing Attachment](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1193) and other types of spearphishing are opened. Malicious embedded macros are an alternative means of execution than software exploitation through [Exploitation for Client Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1203), where adversaries will rely on macros being allowed or that the user will accept to activate them.\n\nMany popular offensive frameworks exist which use forms of scripting for security testers and adversaries alike. Metasploit (Citation: Metasploit_Ref), Veil (Citation: Veil_Ref), and PowerSploit (Citation: Powersploit) are three examples that are popular among penetration testers for exploit and post-compromise operations and include many features for evading defenses. Some adversaries are known to use PowerShell. (Citation: Alperovitch 2014)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1064",
        "standard_attck_name": "Scripting",
        "gid": 103,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1064",
        "uncertain_name": "scripting",
        "uncertain_mes": "**This technique has been deprecated. Please use [Command and Scripting Interpreter](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059) where appropriate.**\n\nAdversaries may use scripts to aid in operations and perform multiple actions that would otherwise be manual. Scripting is useful for speeding up operational tasks and reducing the time required to gain access to critical resources. Some scripting languages may be used to bypass process monitoring mechanisms by directly interacting with the operating system at an API level instead of calling other programs. Common scripting languages for Windows include VBScript and [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1086) but could also be in the form of command-line batch scripts.\n\nScripts can be embedded inside Office documents as macros that can be set to execute when files used in [Spearphishing Attachment](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1193) and other types of spearphishing are opened. Malicious embedded macros are an alternative means of execution than software exploitation through [Exploitation for Client Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1203), where adversaries will rely on macros being allowed or that the user will accept to activate them.\n\nMany popular offensive frameworks exist which use forms of scripting for security testers and adversaries alike. Metasploit (Citation: Metasploit_Ref), Veil (Citation: Veil_Ref), and PowerSploit (Citation: Powersploit) are three examples that are popular among penetration testers for exploit and post-compromise operations and include many features for evading defenses. Some adversaries are known to use PowerShell. (Citation: Alperovitch 2014)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 4.761200115693562,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity \"Scripting\" is a deprecated MITRE ATT&CK technique, with its functionality now encompassed by the parent technique Command and Scripting Interpreter (T1059). It falls under the Defense Evasion and Execution tactics and is applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. The technique involves using scripts like VBScript, PowerShell, or batch files to automate malicious operations, potentially bypassing monitoring by interacting directly with OS APIs. Scripts can be embedded in documents as macros for execution upon opening, providing an alternative to software exploitation for initial access."
    },
    "16228": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion",
            "privilege-escalation"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "21bac197-bc50-4c90-849d-8f7f5e599e1e",
        "aliases": [
            "Process Injection"
        ],
        "name": "Process Injection",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may inject code into processes in order to evade process-based defenses as well as possibly elevate privileges. Process injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process. Running code in the context of another process may allow access to the process's memory, system/network resources, and possibly elevated privileges. Execution via process injection may also evade detection from security products since the execution is masked under a legitimate process. \n\nThere are many different ways to inject code into a process, many of which abuse legitimate functionalities. These implementations exist for every major OS but are typically platform specific. \n\nMore sophisticated samples may perform multiple process injections to segment modules and further evade detection, utilizing named pipes or other inter-process communication (IPC) mechanisms as a communication channel.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1631",
        "standard_attck_name": "Process Injection",
        "gid": 104,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "apt37",
            "apt38",
            "platinum",
            "gamaredon group",
            "kimsuky",
            "blackbyte",
            "apt5",
            "velvet ant",
            "apt32",
            "wizard spider",
            "cobalt group",
            "apt41",
