            "ta2541"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1055",
        "uncertain_name": "process injection",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may inject code into processes in order to evade process-based defenses as well as possibly elevate privileges. Process injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process. Running code in the context of another process may allow access to the process's memory, system/network resources, and possibly elevated privileges. Execution via process injection may also evade detection from security products since the execution is masked under a legitimate process. \n\nThere are many different ways to inject code into a process, many of which abuse legitimate functionalities. These implementations exist for every major OS but are typically platform specific. \n\nMore sophisticated samples may perform multiple process injections to segment modules and further evade detection, utilizing named pipes or other inter-process communication (IPC) mechanisms as a communication channel. ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 8.743165219966373,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Process Injection, identified as MITRE ATT&CK T1055, is a defense evasion and privilege escalation technique used across Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves executing arbitrary code within the address space of a legitimate live process to evade detection and potentially gain elevated privileges. Specific sub-techniques include DLL injection using APIs like `VirtualAllocEx` and `CreateRemoteThread`, PE injection, and proc memory injection via the `/proc` filesystem on Linux. This method allows access to the host process's memory and resources while masking malicious activity."
    },
    "16229": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "command-and-control"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Application Layer Protocol"
        ],
        "unique_id": "01200ec0-a176-4561-bd14-db4e25d6b4c8",
        "name": "T1071",
        "description": "Adversaries may communicate using OSI application layer protocols to avoid detection/network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to the remote system, and often the results of those commands, will be embedded within the protocol traffic between the client and server. \n\nAdversaries may utilize many different protocols, including those used for web browsing, transferring files, electronic mail, DNS, or publishing/subscribing. For connections that occur internally within an enclave (such as those between a proxy or pivot node and other nodes), commonly used protocols are SMB, SSH, or RDP.(Citation: Mandiant APT29 Eye Spy Email Nov 22)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1071.003",
        "standard_attck_name": "Mail Protocols",
        "gid": 105,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "inc ransom",
            "teamtnt",
            "magic hound",
            "velvet ant",
            "rocke"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1071",
        "uncertain_name": "application layer protocol",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may communicate using OSI application layer protocols to avoid detection/network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to the remote system, and often the results of those commands, will be embedded within the protocol traffic between the client and server. \n\nAdversaries may utilize many different protocols, including those used for web browsing, transferring files, electronic mail, DNS, or publishing/subscribing. For connections that occur internally within an enclave (such as those between a proxy or pivot node and other nodes), commonly used protocols are SMB, SSH, or RDP.(Citation: Mandiant APT29 Eye Spy Email Nov 22) ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.576916365108222,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1071 (Application Layer Protocol) is a Command and Control technique for macOS, Linux, Windows, Network Devices, and ESXi. Adversaries use standard OSI application layer protocols like HTTP(S), DNS, SMB, SSH, or RDP to blend their C2 traffic with normal network activity, embedding commands and data within legitimate protocol communications to avoid detection and filtering.[/profile]"
    },
    "16230": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion",
