            "7b21b95c4256308e8089bff38d5d20845f2dc28fa9e536de979ceab9b7962afa",
            "708722bafe35a9fdc94ac33b1970776c464f1bb4e9c2ea1c1dba3a9e1ba03ab3",
            "57b64a1ef1b04819ca9473e1bb74e1cf4be76b89b144e030dc1ef48f446ff95b",
            "362b9f497fce52a3f14ad9de2a027d974cc810473c929fed7c37526d2f13f83a",
            "e848355359de1e59901aa387f2d208889c368663438909fd3bb0a97566de2b2d",
            "42095521622c055db8d79441317952c0899c34d7b776f6f45855581fb86522dc",
            "cd737ac8d66a47d341dd4a3c98ab0d2c77c7558d9a0161f7d08a4ab310d440ba"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 0.0,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]This intrusion set, likely associated with the APT group BackdoorDiplomacy (also known as BackDip, Quarian, and CloudComputating), primarily employs Cobalt Strike Beacon for command and control and lateral movement, specifically leveraging Windows Remote Management (WinRM). Its key TTPs include compromising the software supply chain and relying on user execution for initial access. The group has been observed targeting entities in the Central African Republic and South Africa, utilizing infrastructure such as the domains schemas.openxmlformats.shop and dueog.xyz. Its operations show overlaps with other groups and it is believed to be motivated by espionage objectives.[/profile]"
    },
