        "uncertain_id": "t1036.005",
        "uncertain_name": "match legitimate resource name or location",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may match or approximate the name or location of legitimate files, Registry keys, or other resources when naming/placing them. This is done for the sake of evading defenses and observation. \n\nThis may be done by placing an executable in a commonly trusted directory (ex: under System32) or giving it the name of a legitimate, trusted program (ex: `svchost.exe`). Alternatively, a Windows Registry key may be given a close approximation to a key used by a legitimate program. In containerized environments, a threat actor may create a resource in a trusted namespace or one that matches the naming convention of a container pod or cluster.(Citation: Aquasec Kubernetes Backdoor 2023)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.014388316862885,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1036.005, also known as \"Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location,\" is a Defense Evasion technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, ESXi, and Containers. Its technical mechanics involve adversaries naming or placing malicious resources to closely approximate legitimate ones, such as placing an executable in a trusted directory like System32, naming it after a trusted process like `svchost.exe`, creating similarly named Windows Registry keys, or in containerized environments, creating resources in trusted namespaces or using legitimate pod naming conventions to evade detection. It is a sub-technique of T1036 (Masquerading) and has been associated with numerous threat groups including APT28, APT29, and Lazarus Group."
    },
