            "silence",
            "oilrig",
            "apt38",
            "machete",
            "kimsuky",
            "tropic trooper",
            "apt5",
            "higaisa",
            "menupass",
            "nomadic octopus",
            "lazyscripter",
            "wizard spider"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1059.003",
        "uncertain_name": "windows command shell",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may abuse the Windows command shell for execution. The Windows command shell ([cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106)) is the primary command prompt on Windows systems. The Windows command prompt can be used to control almost any aspect of a system, with various permission levels required for different subsets of commands. The command prompt can be invoked remotely via [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021) such as [SSH](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/004).(Citation: SSH in Windows)\n\nBatch files (ex: .bat or .cmd) also provide the shell with a list of sequential commands to run, as well as normal scripting operations such as conditionals and loops. Common uses of batch files include long or repetitive tasks, or the need to run the same set of commands on multiple systems.\n\nAdversaries may leverage [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106) to execute various commands and payloads. Common uses include [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106) to execute a single command, or abusing [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106) interactively with input and output forwarded over a command and control channel.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 8.724220633440773,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1059.003, known as Windows Command Shell, is a sub-technique of T1059 (Command and Scripting Interpreter) within the Execution tactic on Windows platforms. It involves adversaries abusing the cmd.exe shell to execute commands, scripts, or payloads, either locally or remotely via services like SSH. This shell can be invoked interactively over a command and control channel or through batch files (.bat, .cmd) for automated, repetitive tasks, providing extensive system control. It is commonly associated with numerous threat groups, including APT28, Lazarus, and FIN7.[/profile]"
    },
    "16302": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "resource-development"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Obtain Capabilities"
        ],
        "unique_id": "adf08806-155f-401b-9afc-06ae0b5cdfca",
        "name": "T1588",
        "description": "Adversaries may buy and/or steal capabilities that can be used during targeting. Rather than developing their own capabilities in-house, adversaries may purchase, freely download, or steal them. Activities may include the acquisition of malware, software (including licenses), exploits, certificates, and information relating to vulnerabilities. Adversaries may obtain capabilities to support their operations throughout numerous phases of the adversary lifecycle.\n\nIn addition to downloading free malware, software, and exploits from the internet, adversaries may purchase these capabilities from third-party entities. Third-party entities can include technology companies that specialize in malware and exploits, criminal marketplaces, or from individuals.(Citation: NationsBuying)(Citation: PegasusCitizenLab)\n\nIn addition to purchasing capabilities, adversaries may steal capabilities from third-party entities (including other adversaries). This can include stealing software licenses, malware, SSL/TLS and code-signing certificates, or raiding closed databases of vulnerabilities or exploits.(Citation: DiginotarCompromise)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "PRE"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1588",
        "standard_attck_name": "Obtain Capabilities",
        "gid": 178,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1588",
        "uncertain_name": "obtain capabilities",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may buy and/or steal capabilities that can be used during targeting. Rather than developing their own capabilities in-house, adversaries may purchase, freely download, or steal them. Activities may include the acquisition of malware, software (including licenses), exploits, certificates, and information relating to vulnerabilities. Adversaries may obtain capabilities to support their operations throughout numerous phases of the adversary lifecycle.\n\nIn addition to downloading free malware, software, and exploits from the internet, adversaries may purchase these capabilities from third-party entities. Third-party entities can include technology companies that specialize in malware and exploits, criminal marketplaces, or from individuals.(Citation: NationsBuying)(Citation: PegasusCitizenLab)\n\nIn addition to purchasing capabilities, adversaries may steal capabilities from third-party entities (including other adversaries). This can include stealing software licenses, malware, SSL/TLS and code-signing certificates, or raiding closed databases of vulnerabilities or exploits.(Citation: DiginotarCompromise)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 3.3903716645550244,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1588, Obtain Capabilities, is a Resource Development tactic on the PRE platform. Adversaries acquire capabilities like malware, exploits, tools, and vulnerability information (e.g., CVE-2023-0669) to support operations, rather than developing them in-house. This is done by purchasing from criminal marketplaces, freely downloading, or stealing from third parties, including other adversaries."
    },
    "16303": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "initial-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Compromise Software Supply Chain"
        ],
        "unique_id": "5cc089b5-757f-4c7d-b8a5-fc658c61568f",
        "name": "T1195.002",
        "description": "Adversaries may manipulate application software prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise. Supply chain compromise of software can take place in a number of ways, including manipulation of the application source code, manipulation of the update/distribution mechanism for that software, or replacing compiled releases with a modified version.\n\nTargeting may be specific to a desired victim set or may be distributed to a broad set of consumers but only move on to additional tactics on specific victims.(Citation: Avast CCleaner3 2018)(Citation: Command Five SK 2011)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "dragonfly",
            "fin7",
            "gold southfield",
            "apt41",
            "sandworm team",
            "cobalt group",
            "daggerfly",
            "moonstone sleet",
            "threat group-3390"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1195.002",
        "uncertain_name": "compromise software supply chain",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may manipulate application software prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise. Supply chain compromise of software can take place in a number of ways, including manipulation of the application source code, manipulation of the update/distribution mechanism for that software, or replacing compiled releases with a modified version.\n\nTargeting may be specific to a desired victim set or may be distributed to a broad set of consumers but only move on to additional tactics on specific victims.(Citation: Avast CCleaner3 2018)(Citation: Command Five SK 2011)  ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.241793811610098,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1195.002, Compromise Software Supply Chain, is an Initial Access technique applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS. Adversaries manipulate software prior to consumer receipt by altering source code, compromising update mechanisms, or replacing legitimate releases with malicious versions, as seen in campaigns like CCleaner. This can be broadly distributed or targeted, and has been associated with groups including APT41, Sandworm Team, and FIN7. A related vulnerability is CVE-2023-0669."
    },
    "16304": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "initial-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Phishing"
        ],
        "unique_id": "575d462c-d95a-44ff-9f3b-78ed1263d740",
        "name": "T1566",
        "description": "Adversaries may send phishing messages to gain access to victim systems. All forms of phishing are electronically delivered social engineering. Phishing can be targeted, known as spearphishing. In spearphishing, a specific individual, company, or industry will be targeted by the adversary. More generally, adversaries can conduct non-targeted phishing, such as in mass malware spam campaigns.\n\nAdversaries may send victims emails containing malicious attachments or links, typically to execute malicious code on victim systems. Phishing may also be conducted via third-party services, like social media platforms. Phishing may also involve social engineering techniques, such as posing as a trusted source, as well as evasive techniques such as removing or manipulating emails or metadata/headers from compromised accounts being abused to send messages (e.g., [Email Hiding Rules](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/008)).(Citation: Microsoft OAuth Spam 2022)(Citation: Palo Alto Unit 42 VBA Infostealer 2014) Another way to accomplish this is by [Email Spoofing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1672)(Citation: Proofpoint-spoof) the identity of the sender, which can be used to fool both the human recipient as well as automated security tools,(Citation: cyberproof-double-bounce) or by including the intended target as a party to an existing email thread that includes malicious files or links (i.e., \"thread hijacking\").(Citation: phishing-krebs)\n\nVictims may also receive phishing messages that instruct them to call a phone number where they are directed to visit a malicious URL, download malware,(Citation: sygnia Luna Month)(Citation: CISA Remote Monitoring and Management Software) or install adversary-accessible remote management tools onto their computer (i.e., [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204)).(Citation: Unit42 Luna Moth)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Office Suite",
            "Identity Provider",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1566.004",
        "standard_attck_name": "Spearphishing Voice",
        "gid": 180,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "sea turtle",
            "applejeus",
            "inc ransom",
            "kimsuky",
            "axiom",
            "gold southfield"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1566",
        "uncertain_name": "phishing",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may send phishing messages to gain access to victim systems. All forms of phishing are electronically delivered social engineering. Phishing can be targeted, known as spearphishing. In spearphishing, a specific individual, company, or industry will be targeted by the adversary. More generally, adversaries can conduct non-targeted phishing, such as in mass malware spam campaigns.\n\nAdversaries may send victims emails containing malicious attachments or links, typically to execute malicious code on victim systems. Phishing may also be conducted via third-party services, like social media platforms. Phishing may also involve social engineering techniques, such as posing as a trusted source, as well as evasive techniques such as removing or manipulating emails or metadata/headers from compromised accounts being abused to send messages (e.g., [Email Hiding Rules](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/008)).(Citation: Microsoft OAuth Spam 2022)(Citation: Palo Alto Unit 42 VBA Infostealer 2014) Another way to accomplish this is by [Email Spoofing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1672)(Citation: Proofpoint-spoof) the identity of the sender, which can be used to fool both the human recipient as well as automated security tools,(Citation: cyberproof-double-bounce) or by including the intended target as a party to an existing email thread that includes malicious files or links (i.e., \"thread hijacking\").(Citation: phishing-krebs)\n\nVictims may also receive phishing messages that instruct them to call a phone number where they are directed to visit a malicious URL, download malware,(Citation: sygnia Luna Month)(Citation: CISA Remote Monitoring and Management Software) or install adversary-accessible remote management tools onto their computer (i.e., [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204)).(Citation: Unit42 Luna Moth)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.294756946480268,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity is Phishing, identified as MITRE ATT&CK technique T1566 under the Initial Access tactic, targeting platforms including Windows, Linux, macOS, Office Suite, Identity Provider, and SaaS. It involves sending electronic messages, such as emails, to trick recipients into opening malicious attachments or links to execute code, often using social engineering like impersonating trusted sources. The technique encompasses sub-techniques like Spearphishing Attachment (T1566.001) and can be facilitated by compromising email accounts (T1586.002) to enhance credibility and evade filters."
    },
    "16305": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "resource-development"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Malware"
        ],
        "unique_id": "099dcae7-02c3-40b4-b2ec-878435cee88e",
        "name": "T1588.001",
        "description": "Adversaries may buy, steal, or download malware that can be used during targeting. Malicious software can include payloads, droppers, post-compromise tools, backdoors, packers, and C2 protocols. Adversaries may acquire malware to support their operations, obtaining a means for maintaining control of remote machines, evading defenses, and executing post-compromise behaviors.\n\nIn addition to downloading free malware from the internet, adversaries may purchase these capabilities from third-party entities. Third-party entities can include technology companies that specialize in malware development, criminal marketplaces (including Malware-as-a-Service, or MaaS), or from individuals. In addition to purchasing malware, adversaries may steal and repurpose malware from third-party entities (including other adversaries).",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "PRE"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1588.001",
        "standard_attck_name": "Malware",
        "gid": 181,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "unc3886",
            "metador",
            "earth lusca",
            "lazyscripter",
            "backdoordiplomacy",
            "apt1",
            "ta505",
            "scattered spider",
            "turla",
            "lapsus$",
            "luminousmoth",
            "andariel",
            "aquatic panda",
            "ember bear",
            "ta2541"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1588.001",
        "uncertain_name": "malware",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may buy, steal, or download malware that can be used during targeting. Malicious software can include payloads, droppers, post-compromise tools, backdoors, packers, and C2 protocols. Adversaries may acquire malware to support their operations, obtaining a means for maintaining control of remote machines, evading defenses, and executing post-compromise behaviors.\n\nIn addition to downloading free malware from the internet, adversaries may purchase these capabilities from third-party entities. Third-party entities can include technology companies that specialize in malware development, criminal marketplaces (including Malware-as-a-Service, or MaaS), or from individuals. In addition to purchasing malware, adversaries may steal and repurpose malware from third-party entities (including other adversaries).",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.742910933246987,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1588.001, also known as Malware, is a Resource Development technique (Tactic) within the MITRE ATT&CK framework, applicable to the PRE platform. Adversaries acquire malware—including payloads, droppers, backdoors, and C2 protocols—by purchasing it from criminal marketplaces (Malware-as-a-Service), stealing it, or downloading it from the internet to support their operations. This technique is associated with groups like UNC3886, Metador, and APT1, and has been linked to vulnerabilities such as CVE-2023-0669.[/profile]"
    },
    "16306": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "lateral-movement"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Windows Remote Management"
        ],
        "unique_id": "83ec7e58-6664-41c1-ad2c-0e88f2199a21",
        "name": "T1021.006",
        "description": "Adversaries may use [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) to interact with remote systems using Windows Remote Management (WinRM). The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user.\n\nWinRM is the name of both a Windows service and a protocol that allows a user to interact with a remote system (e.g., run an executable, modify the Registry, modify services).(Citation: Microsoft WinRM) It may be called with the `winrm` command or by any number of programs such as PowerShell.(Citation: Jacobsen 2014) WinRM  can be used as a method of remotely interacting with [Windows Management Instrumentation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1047).(Citation: MSDN WMI)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "windows"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "fin13",
            "chimera",
            "threat group-3390",
            "storm-0501",
            "wizard spider"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1021.006",
        "uncertain_name": "windows remote management",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) to interact with remote systems using Windows Remote Management (WinRM). The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user.\n\nWinRM is the name of both a Windows service and a protocol that allows a user to interact with a remote system (e.g., run an executable, modify the Registry, modify services).(Citation: Microsoft WinRM) It may be called with the `winrm` command or by any number of programs such as PowerShell.(Citation: Jacobsen 2014) WinRM  can be used as a method of remotely interacting with [Windows Management Instrumentation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1047).(Citation: MSDN WMI)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.830628846806356,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1021.006: Windows Remote Management is a Lateral Movement technique for Windows platforms. Adversaries use Valid Accounts to leverage the WinRM service and protocol for remote interaction, enabling actions like executing commands, modifying the Registry or services, and interacting with Windows Management Instrumentation (WMI) on target systems, often via the `winrm` command or PowerShell. Associated vulnerabilities include CVE-2023-0669 and CVE-2024-30088, and it has been used by groups like FIN13 and Wizard Spider."
    },
    "16307": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "command-and-control"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Remote Access Tools"
        ],
        "unique_id": "d9edb260-5aa2-40ab-86bf-857cd1e3f1d5",
        "name": "T1219",
        "description": "An adversary may use legitimate remote access tools to establish an interactive command and control channel within a network. Remote access tools create a session between two trusted hosts through a graphical interface, a command line interaction, a protocol tunnel via development or management software, or hardware-level access such as KVM (Keyboard, Video, Mouse) over IP solutions. Desktop support software (usually graphical interface) and remote management software (typically command line interface) allow a user to control a computer remotely as if they are a local user inheriting the user or software permissions. This software is commonly used for troubleshooting, software installation, and system management.(Citation: Symantec Living off the Land)(Citation: CrowdStrike 2015 Global Threat Report)(Citation: CrySyS Blog TeamSpy) Adversaries may similarly abuse response features included in EDR and other defensive tools that enable remote access.\n\nRemote access tools may be installed and used post-compromise as an alternate communications channel for redundant access or to establish an interactive remote desktop session with the target system. It may also be used as a malware component to establish a reverse connection or back-connect to a service or adversary-controlled system.\n\nInstallation of many remote access tools may also include persistence (e.g., the software's installation routine creates a [Windows Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/003)). Remote access modules/features may also exist as part of otherwise existing software (e.g., Google Chrome’s Remote Desktop).(Citation: Google Chrome Remote Desktop)(Citation: Chrome Remote Desktop)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1219",
        "standard_attck_name": "Remote Access Tools",
        "gid": 183,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "carbanak",
            "muddywater",
            "fin7",
            "inc ransom",
            "teamtnt",
            "darkvishnya",
            "blackbyte",
            "gold southfield",
            "medusa group",
            "akira",
            "sandworm team",
            "cobalt group",
            "oilrig"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1219",
        "uncertain_name": "remote access tools",
        "uncertain_mes": "An adversary may use legitimate remote access tools to establish an interactive command and control channel within a network. Remote access tools create a session between two trusted hosts through a graphical interface, a command line interaction, a protocol tunnel via development or management software, or hardware-level access such as KVM (Keyboard, Video, Mouse) over IP solutions. Desktop support software (usually graphical interface) and remote management software (typically command line interface) allow a user to control a computer remotely as if they are a local user inheriting the user or software permissions. This software is commonly used for troubleshooting, software installation, and system management.(Citation: Symantec Living off the Land)(Citation: CrowdStrike 2015 Global Threat Report)(Citation: CrySyS Blog TeamSpy) Adversaries may similarly abuse response features included in EDR and other defensive tools that enable remote access.\n\nRemote access tools may be installed and used post-compromise as an alternate communications channel for redundant access or to establish an interactive remote desktop session with the target system. It may also be used as a malware component to establish a reverse connection or back-connect to a service or adversary-controlled system.\n\nInstallation of many remote access tools may also include persistence (e.g., the software's installation routine creates a [Windows Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543/003)). Remote access modules/features may also exist as part of otherwise existing software (e.g., Google Chrome’s Remote Desktop).(Citation: Google Chrome Remote Desktop)(Citation: Chrome Remote Desktop)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.710664022626332,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1219, known as Remote Access Tools, is a Command and Control technique used across Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves abusing legitimate remote access or desktop support software (like Chrome Remote Desktop) to establish interactive C2 channels, often installing persistence via services. This activity is commonly associated with groups like Carbanak, MuddyWater, and FIN7, and adversaries may use it as a follow-on from User Execution (T1204) or leverage IDE extensions for tunneling. Related CVEs include CVE-2021-44228.[/profile]"
    },
    "16308": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "initial-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Spearphishing Attachment"
        ],
        "unique_id": "c2e47fff-f3a2-4318-a6ee-e82d76a8c0b5",
        "name": "T1566.001",
        "description": "Adversaries may send spearphishing emails with a malicious attachment in an attempt to gain access to victim systems. Spearphishing attachment is a specific variant of spearphishing. Spearphishing attachment is different from other forms of spearphishing in that it employs the use of malware attached to an email. All forms of spearphishing are electronically delivered social engineering targeted at a specific individual, company, or industry. In this scenario, adversaries attach a file to the spearphishing email and usually rely upon [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204) to gain execution.(Citation: Unit 42 DarkHydrus July 2018) Spearphishing may also involve social engineering techniques, such as posing as a trusted source.\n\nThere are many options for the attachment such as Microsoft Office documents, executables, PDFs, or archived files. Upon opening the attachment (and potentially clicking past protections), the adversary's payload exploits a vulnerability or directly executes on the user's system. The text of the spearphishing email usually tries to give a plausible reason why the file should be opened, and may explain how to bypass system protections in order to do so. The email may also contain instructions on how to decrypt an attachment, such as a zip file password, in order to evade email boundary defenses. Adversaries frequently manipulate file extensions and icons in order to make attached executables appear to be document files, or files exploiting one application appear to be a file for a different one.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
