            "menupass",
            "apt39"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1140",
        "uncertain_name": "deobfuscate/decode files or information",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use [Obfuscated Files or Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027) to hide artifacts of an intrusion from analysis. They may require separate mechanisms to decode or deobfuscate that information depending on how they intend to use it. Methods for doing that include built-in functionality of malware or by using utilities present on the system.\n\nOne such example is the use of [certutil](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0160) to decode a remote access tool portable executable file that has been hidden inside a certificate file.(Citation: Malwarebytes Targeted Attack against Saudi Arabia) Another example is using the Windows <code>copy /b</code> or <code>type</code> command to reassemble binary fragments into a malicious payload.(Citation: Carbon Black Obfuscation Sept 2016)(Citation: Sentinel One Tainted Love 2023)\n\nSometimes a user's action may be required to open it for deobfuscation or decryption as part of [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204). The user may also be required to input a password to open a password protected compressed/encrypted file that was provided by the adversary.(Citation: Volexity PowerDuke November 2016)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 8.520232934720674,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1140 - Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information is a Defense Evasion technique for macOS, Linux, Windows, and ESXi. It involves adversaries using separate mechanisms to decode or deobfuscate hidden artifacts for execution, such as employing built-in utilities like `certutil` to decode a remote access tool from a certificate file or using Windows commands like `copy /b` or `type` to reassemble binary fragments into a malicious payload. User action may sometimes be required to open or provide a password for these files. It is commonly used by groups like Lazarus Group, APT28, and MuddyWater.[/profile]"
    },
    "16432": {
        "semantic": 0,
        "unique_id": "2c91607c-1715-4a84-9450-e2ff5568dc98",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "64677CAE14A2EC4D393A81548417B61B",
        "is_family": false,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 0,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The malware known by the hash 64677CAE14A2EC4D393A81548417B61B has been observed in locations including the Republic of Korea, Japan, and the United States. It employs techniques for defense evasion such as Rootkit (T1014) and Indicator Removal (T1070), for credential access via Input Capture (T1056), and for data collection through Archive Collected Data (T1560). Its initial access vector involves Supply Chain Compromise (T1195). The malware is associated with groups linked to the NJRAT family, including Aquatic Panda, Earth Lusca, Operation C-Major, and The Gorgon Group.[/profile]"
    },
    "16433": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "discovery"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "fbcd556b-fe1e-4703-90c0-cf1d783c9ae9",
        "aliases": [
            "Network Share Discovery"
        ],
        "name": "Network Share Discovery",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may look for folders and drives shared on remote systems as a means of identifying sources of information to gather as a precursor for Collection and to identify potential systems of interest for Lateral Movement. Networks often contain shared network drives and folders that enable users to access file directories on various systems across a network. \n\nFile sharing over a Windows network occurs over the SMB protocol. (Citation: Wikipedia Shared Resource) (Citation: TechNet Shared Folder) [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039) can be used to query a remote system for available shared drives using the `net view \\\\\\\\remotesystem` command. It can also be used to query shared drives on the local system using `net share`. For macOS, the `sharing -l` command lists all shared points used for smb services.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1135",
        "standard_attck_name": "Network Share Discovery",
        "gid": 309,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [
            "64677CAE14A2EC4D393A81548417B61B"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "fin13",
            "chimera",
            "apt38",
            "dragonfly",
            "inc ransom",
            "darkvishnya",
            "blackbyte",
            "apt1",
            "tropic trooper",
            "medusa group",
            "apt41",
            "apt32",
            "sowbug",
            "tonto team",
            "apt39",
            "wizard spider"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1135",
        "uncertain_name": "network share discovery",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may look for folders and drives shared on remote systems as a means of identifying sources of information to gather as a precursor for Collection and to identify potential systems of interest for Lateral Movement. Networks often contain shared network drives and folders that enable users to access file directories on various systems across a network. \n\nFile sharing over a Windows network occurs over the SMB protocol. (Citation: Wikipedia Shared Resource) (Citation: TechNet Shared Folder) [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039) can be used to query a remote system for available shared drives using the <code>net view \\\\\\\\remotesystem</code> command. It can also be used to query shared drives on the local system using <code>net share</code>. For macOS, the <code>sharing -l</code> command lists all shared points used for smb services.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.17821816487764,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Network Share Discovery, identified as T1135, is a Discovery tactic used on Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. It involves adversaries enumerating shared folders and drives on remote or local systems to identify information sources for collection or lateral movement. Specific technical mechanics include using the `net view \\\\\\\\remotesystem` and `net share` commands on Windows, and the `sharing -l` command on macOS to list SMB shares. This technique is commonly associated with groups like APT41, Wizard Spider, and FIN13.[/profile]"
    },
    "16434": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Indicator Removal"
        ],
        "unique_id": "1889bf37-1369-456f-82b9-984a85dec016",
        "name": "T1070",
        "description": "Adversaries may delete or modify artifacts generated within systems to remove evidence of their presence or hinder defenses. Various artifacts may be created by an adversary or something that can be attributed to an adversary’s actions. Typically these artifacts are used as defensive indicators related to monitored events, such as strings from downloaded files, logs that are generated from user actions, and other data analyzed by defenders. Location, format, and type of artifact (such as command or login history) are often specific to each platform.\n\nRemoval of these indicators may interfere with event collection, reporting, or other processes used to detect intrusion activity. This may compromise the integrity of security solutions by causing notable events to go unreported. This activity may also impede forensic analysis and incident response, due to lack of sufficient data to determine what occurred.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "Containers",
            "ESXi",
            "Office Suite"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1070",
        "standard_attck_name": "Indicator Removal",
        "gid": 310,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [
            "64677CAE14A2EC4D393A81548417B61B"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "lazarus group",
            "apt42",
            "apt5",
            "mustang panda"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1070",
        "uncertain_name": "indicator removal",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may delete or modify artifacts generated within systems to remove evidence of their presence or hinder defenses. Various artifacts may be created by an adversary or something that can be attributed to an adversary’s actions. Typically these artifacts are used as defensive indicators related to monitored events, such as strings from downloaded files, logs that are generated from user actions, and other data analyzed by defenders. Location, format, and type of artifact (such as command or login history) are often specific to each platform.\n\nRemoval of these indicators may interfere with event collection, reporting, or other processes used to detect intrusion activity. This may compromise the integrity of security solutions by causing notable events to go unreported. This activity may also impede forensic analysis and incident response, due to lack of sufficient data to determine what occurred.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.926255697413994,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity is MITRE ATT&CK technique T1070, named Indicator Removal, which is a Defense Evasion tactic applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, and other platforms. It involves adversaries deleting or modifying system artifacts like logs and downloaded files to remove evidence of their presence, thereby hindering event collection, forensic analysis, and security reporting. This action can compromise security solution integrity by causing notable events to go unreported.[/profile]"
    },
