        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1560",
        "uncertain_name": "archive collected data",
        "uncertain_mes": "An adversary may compress and/or encrypt data that is collected prior to exfiltration. Compressing the data can help to obfuscate the collected data and minimize the amount of data sent over the network.(Citation: DOJ GRU Indictment Jul 2018) Encryption can be used to hide information that is being exfiltrated from detection or make exfiltration less conspicuous upon inspection by a defender.\n\nBoth compression and encryption are done prior to exfiltration, and can be performed using a utility, 3rd party library, or custom method.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.31561536182975,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Archive Collected Data (T1560) is a Collection technique used on Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves compressing or encrypting collected data prior to exfiltration to obfuscate its contents and reduce network footprint. This can be performed using system utilities, third-party libraries, or custom methods. It is commonly used in conjunction with data staging techniques (T1074) and has sub-techniques for specific archiving methods. Associated threat groups include APT28, Lazarus Group, and FIN6."
    },
