        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Data Transfer Size Limits, identified as T1030, is an exfiltration technique used on Windows, Linux, macOS, and ESXi platforms. Adversaries implement this by breaking data into fixed-size chunks or limiting packet sizes below network monitoring thresholds to avoid triggering alerts for large data transfers. This method has been associated with groups including APT41, APT28, and LuminousMoth."
    },
    "16438": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Rootkit"
        ],
        "unique_id": "3570f5b7-f28d-4bdb-9b87-b33468178ddf",
        "name": "T1014",
        "description": "Adversaries may use rootkits to hide the presence of programs, files, network connections, services, drivers, and other system components. Rootkits are programs that hide the existence of malware by intercepting/hooking and modifying operating system API calls that supply system information. (Citation: Symantec Windows Rootkits) \n\nRootkits or rootkit enabling functionality may reside at the user or kernel level in the operating system or lower, to include a hypervisor or [System Firmware](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1542/001). (Citation: Wikipedia Rootkit) Rootkits have been seen for Windows, Linux, and Mac OS X systems. (Citation: CrowdStrike Linux Rootkit) (Citation: BlackHat Mac OSX Rootkit)\n\nRootkits that reside or modify boot sectors are known as [Bootkit](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1542/003)s and specifically target the boot process of the operating system.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1014",
        "standard_attck_name": "Rootkit",
        "gid": 314,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [
            "64677CAE14A2EC4D393A81548417B61B"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "winnti group",
            "teamtnt",
            "apt41",
            "rocke",
            "apt28",
            "unc3886"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1014",
        "uncertain_name": "rootkit",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use rootkits to hide the presence of programs, files, network connections, services, drivers, and other system components. Rootkits are programs that hide the existence of malware by intercepting/hooking and modifying operating system API calls that supply system information. (Citation: Symantec Windows Rootkits) \n\nRootkits or rootkit enabling functionality may reside at the user or kernel level in the operating system or lower, to include a hypervisor or [System Firmware](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1542/001). (Citation: Wikipedia Rootkit) Rootkits have been seen for Windows, Linux, and Mac OS X systems. (Citation: CrowdStrike Linux Rootkit) (Citation: BlackHat Mac OSX Rootkit)\n\nRootkits that reside or modify boot sectors are known as [Bootkit](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1542/003)s and specifically target the boot process of the operating system.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.961140867915013,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity is MITRE ATT&CK technique T1014, known as Rootkit, a Defense Evasion tactic applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS. It hides malware by intercepting and modifying OS API calls to conceal programs, files, network connections, and other components. Rootkits can operate at the user or kernel level, with kernel-mode variants like Loadable Kernel Modules (LKMs) running with high privilege (Ring 0) to hide processes and provide backdoors. They are also associated with bootkits targeting the boot process and have been used by groups like APT41 and APT28.[/profile]"
    },
    "16439": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "initial-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Supply Chain Compromise"
        ],
        "unique_id": "60652881-a623-4816-802a-82c7517a2c0b",
        "name": "T1195",
        "description": "Adversaries may manipulate products or product delivery mechanisms prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise.\n\nSupply chain compromise can take place at any stage of the supply chain including:\n\n* Manipulation of development tools\n* Manipulation of a development environment\n* Manipulation of source code repositories (public or private)\n* Manipulation of source code in open-source dependencies\n* Manipulation of software update/distribution mechanisms\n* Compromised/infected system images (removable media infected at the factory)(Citation: IBM Storwize)(Citation: Schneider Electric USB Malware) \n* Replacement of legitimate software with modified versions\n* Sales of modified/counterfeit products to legitimate distributors\n* Shipment interdiction\n\nWhile supply chain compromise can impact any component of hardware or software, adversaries looking to gain execution have often focused on malicious additions to legitimate software in software distribution or update channels.(Citation: Avast CCleaner3 2018)(Citation: Microsoft Dofoil 2018)(Citation: Command Five SK 2011) Adversaries may limit targeting to a desired victim set or distribute malicious software to a broad set of consumers but only follow up with specific victims.(Citation: Symantec Elderwood Sept 2012)(Citation: Avast CCleaner3 2018)(Citation: Command Five SK 2011) Popular open-source projects that are used as dependencies in many applications may also be targeted as a means to add malicious code to users of the dependency.(Citation: Trendmicro NPM Compromise)\n\nIn some cases, adversaries may conduct “second-order” supply chain compromises by leveraging the access gained from an initial supply chain compromise to further compromise a software component.(Citation: Krebs 3cx overview 2023) This may allow the threat actor to spread to even more victims.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [
            "64677CAE14A2EC4D393A81548417B61B"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "sandworm team",
            "ember bear",
            "oilrig"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1195",
        "uncertain_name": "supply chain compromise",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may manipulate products or product delivery mechanisms prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise.\n\nSupply chain compromise can take place at any stage of the supply chain including:\n\n* Manipulation of development tools\n* Manipulation of a development environment\n* Manipulation of source code repositories (public or private)\n* Manipulation of source code in open-source dependencies\n* Manipulation of software update/distribution mechanisms\n* Compromised/infected system images (removable media infected at the factory)(Citation: IBM Storwize)(Citation: Schneider Electric USB Malware) \n* Replacement of legitimate software with modified versions\n* Sales of modified/counterfeit products to legitimate distributors\n* Shipment interdiction\n\nWhile supply chain compromise can impact any component of hardware or software, adversaries looking to gain execution have often focused on malicious additions to legitimate software in software distribution or update channels.(Citation: Avast CCleaner3 2018)(Citation: Microsoft Dofoil 2018)(Citation: Command Five SK 2011) Adversaries may limit targeting to a desired victim set or distribute malicious software to a broad set of consumers but only follow up with specific victims.(Citation: Symantec Elderwood Sept 2012)(Citation: Avast CCleaner3 2018)(Citation: Command Five SK 2011) Popular open-source projects that are used as dependencies in many applications may also be targeted as a means to add malicious code to users of the dependency.(Citation: Trendmicro NPM Compromise)\n\nIn some cases, adversaries may conduct “second-order” supply chain compromises by leveraging the access gained from an initial supply chain compromise to further compromise a software component.(Citation: Krebs 3cx overview 2023) This may allow the threat actor to spread to even more victims.  ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.744006710926168,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1195, known as Supply Chain Compromise, is an Initial Access technique targeting macOS, Linux, Windows, and SaaS platforms. It involves adversaries manipulating products or delivery mechanisms before consumer receipt to compromise systems. This can occur through tampering with development tools, environments, source code repositories (including open-source dependencies), software update channels, or system images. Attackers may distribute malicious code broadly or target specific victims, and have been known to conduct \"second-order\" compromises by leveraging initial access to further infiltrate software components. Associated vulnerabilities include CVE-2024-38193, CVE-2024-3400, and CVE-2023-42793.[/profile]"
    },
    "16440": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
