    "16446": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "initial-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "cac75b2a-7f96-4819-a8b9-7c4ce0f672f2",
        "aliases": [
            "Supply Chain Compromise"
        ],
        "name": "Supply Chain Compromise",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may manipulate products or product delivery mechanisms prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise.\n\nSupply chain compromise can take place at any stage of the supply chain including:\n\n* Manipulation of development tools\n* Manipulation of a development environment\n* Manipulation of source code repositories (public or private)\n* Manipulation of source code in open-source dependencies\n* Manipulation of software update/distribution mechanisms\n* Compromised/infected system images (removable media infected at the factory)(Citation: IBM Storwize)(Citation: Schneider Electric USB Malware) \n* Replacement of legitimate software with modified versions\n* Sales of modified/counterfeit products to legitimate distributors\n* Shipment interdiction\n\nWhile supply chain compromise can impact any component of hardware or software, adversaries looking to gain execution have often focused on malicious additions to legitimate software in software distribution or update channels.(Citation: Avast CCleaner3 2018)(Citation: Microsoft Dofoil 2018)(Citation: Command Five SK 2011) Adversaries may limit targeting to a desired victim set or distribute malicious software to a broad set of consumers but only follow up with specific victims.(Citation: Symantec Elderwood Sept 2012)(Citation: Avast CCleaner3 2018)(Citation: Command Five SK 2011) Popular open-source projects that are used as dependencies in many applications may also be targeted as a means to add malicious code to users of the dependency.(Citation: Trendmicro NPM Compromise)\n\nIn some cases, adversaries may conduct “second-order” supply chain compromises by leveraging the access gained from an initial supply chain compromise to further compromise a software component.(Citation: Krebs 3cx overview 2023) This may allow the threat actor to spread to even more victims.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1474",
        "standard_attck_name": "Supply Chain Compromise",
        "gid": 322,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "sandworm team",
            "ember bear",
            "oilrig"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1195",
        "uncertain_name": "supply chain compromise",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may manipulate products or product delivery mechanisms prior to receipt by a final consumer for the purpose of data or system compromise.\n\nSupply chain compromise can take place at any stage of the supply chain including:\n\n* Manipulation of development tools\n* Manipulation of a development environment\n* Manipulation of source code repositories (public or private)\n* Manipulation of source code in open-source dependencies\n* Manipulation of software update/distribution mechanisms\n* Compromised/infected system images (removable media infected at the factory)(Citation: IBM Storwize)(Citation: Schneider Electric USB Malware) \n* Replacement of legitimate software with modified versions\n* Sales of modified/counterfeit products to legitimate distributors\n* Shipment interdiction\n\nWhile supply chain compromise can impact any component of hardware or software, adversaries looking to gain execution have often focused on malicious additions to legitimate software in software distribution or update channels.(Citation: Avast CCleaner3 2018)(Citation: Microsoft Dofoil 2018)(Citation: Command Five SK 2011) Adversaries may limit targeting to a desired victim set or distribute malicious software to a broad set of consumers but only follow up with specific victims.(Citation: Symantec Elderwood Sept 2012)(Citation: Avast CCleaner3 2018)(Citation: Command Five SK 2011) Popular open-source projects that are used as dependencies in many applications may also be targeted as a means to add malicious code to users of the dependency.(Citation: Trendmicro NPM Compromise)\n\nIn some cases, adversaries may conduct “second-order” supply chain compromises by leveraging the access gained from an initial supply chain compromise to further compromise a software component.(Citation: Krebs 3cx overview 2023) This may allow the threat actor to spread to even more victims.  ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 11.714290416760953,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Supply Chain Compromise is a MITRE ATT&CK technique with ID T1195 under the Initial Access tactic, targeting macOS, Linux, Windows, and SaaS platforms. It involves adversaries manipulating products or delivery mechanisms before consumer receipt to compromise systems. Specific methods include tampering with development tools, source code repositories, open-source dependencies, and software update channels to insert malicious code into legitimate distribution flows. This technique is often associated with groups like Sandworm Team and OilRig.[/profile]"
    },
    "16447": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "collection"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "4d258986-e86c-4f0a-a683-5f96a73753c2",
        "aliases": [
            "Archive Collected Data"
        ],
        "name": "Archive Collected Data",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "An adversary may compress and/or encrypt data that is collected prior to exfiltration. Compressing the data can help to obfuscate the collected data and minimize the amount of data sent over the network.(Citation: DOJ GRU Indictment Jul 2018) Encryption can be used to hide information that is being exfiltrated from detection or make exfiltration less conspicuous upon inspection by a defender.\n\nBoth compression and encryption are done prior to exfiltration, and can be performed using a utility, 3rd party library, or custom method.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1532",
        "standard_attck_name": "Archive Collected Data",
        "gid": 323,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "dragonfly",
            "ke3chang",
            "patchwork",
            "fin6",
            "blackbyte",
            "axiom",
            "leviathan",
            "apt32",
            "luminousmoth",
            "menupass",
            "apt28",
            "lazarus group",
            "ember bear"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1560",
        "uncertain_name": "archive collected data",
        "uncertain_mes": "An adversary may compress and/or encrypt data that is collected prior to exfiltration. Compressing the data can help to obfuscate the collected data and minimize the amount of data sent over the network.(Citation: DOJ GRU Indictment Jul 2018) Encryption can be used to hide information that is being exfiltrated from detection or make exfiltration less conspicuous upon inspection by a defender.\n\nBoth compression and encryption are done prior to exfiltration, and can be performed using a utility, 3rd party library, or custom method.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 9.61599565790144,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Archive Collected Data is identified as MITRE ATT&CK technique T1560 under the Collection tactic, applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. This technique involves compressing and/or encrypting collected data prior to exfiltration to obfuscate content and reduce network footprint. Execution methods include using built-in utilities like tar, zip, diantz, or certutil on Windows, employing third-party libraries such as Python's rarfile or zlib, or implementing custom archival algorithms. It is commonly associated with data staging procedures (T1074) and numerous threat groups including APT28, Lazarus Group, and FIN6.[/profile]"
    },
    "16448": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "collection",
            "credential-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Input Capture"
        ],
        "unique_id": "57eaa0e5-9b62-45a9-808c-63217f7f6b85",
        "name": "Input Capture",
        "description": "Adversaries may use methods of capturing user input to obtain credentials or collect information. During normal system usage, users often provide credentials to various different locations, such as login pages/portals or system dialog boxes. Input capture mechanisms may be transparent to the user (e.g. [Credential API Hooking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1056/004)) or rely on deceiving the user into providing input into what they believe to be a genuine service (e.g. [Web Portal Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1056/003)).",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1417",
        "standard_attck_name": "Input Capture",
        "gid": 324,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "storm-1811",
            "apt39",
            "apt42"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1056",
        "uncertain_name": "input capture",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use methods of capturing user input to obtain credentials or collect information. During normal system usage, users often provide credentials to various different locations, such as login pages/portals or system dialog boxes. Input capture mechanisms may be transparent to the user (e.g. [Credential API Hooking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1056/004)) or rely on deceiving the user into providing input into what they believe to be a genuine service (e.g. [Web Portal Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1056/003)).",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.819856282025011,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Input Capture is identified as MITRE ATT&CK technique T1056 under the Credential Access and Collection tactics, targeting Windows, Linux, macOS, and Network Devices. It involves methods to intercept user input, such as keystrokes, to steal credentials. This can be achieved transparently via API hooking or deceptively through fake prompts mimicking legitimate system or software authentication dialogs, often using scripting languages like AppleScript or PowerShell. Associated threat groups include APT39 and APT42.[/profile]"
    },
    "16449": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "collection"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Screen Capture"
        ],
        "unique_id": "d906cbb2-5cd2-4034-b451-3673b05c5166",
        "name": "Screen Capture",
        "description": "Adversaries may attempt to take screen captures of the desktop to gather information over the course of an operation. Screen capturing functionality may be included as a feature of a remote access tool used in post-compromise operations. Taking a screenshot is also typically possible through native utilities or API calls, such as `CopyFromScreen`, `xwd`, or `screencapture`.(Citation: CopyFromScreen .NET)(Citation: Antiquated Mac Malware)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "winter vivern",
            "gamaredon group",
            "dragonfly",
            "muddywater",
            "fin7",
            "kimsuky",
            "magic hound",
            "gold southfield",
            "bronze butler",
            "moustachedbouncer",
            "apt42",
            "apt28",
            "silence",
            "group5",
            "dark caracal",
            "apt39",
            "oilrig",
            "volt typhoon"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1113",
        "uncertain_name": "screen capture",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may attempt to take screen captures of the desktop to gather information over the course of an operation. Screen capturing functionality may be included as a feature of a remote access tool used in post-compromise operations. Taking a screenshot is also typically possible through native utilities or API calls, such as <code>CopyFromScreen</code>, <code>xwd</code>, or <code>screencapture</code>.(Citation: CopyFromScreen .NET)(Citation: Antiquated Mac Malware)\n",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 9.037495856744648,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Screen Capture is MITRE ATT&CK technique T1113 under the Collection tactic, targeting Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. It involves adversaries taking screenshots of the desktop to gather information, often via remote access tools or native utilities and API calls like CopyFromScreen, xwd, or screencapture. The technique is associated with groups including APT28, FIN7, and MuddyWater."
    },
