    "16461": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "command-and-control"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "eee3d320-f153-48dd-99c6-50cfc6e58452",
        "aliases": [
            "Non-Standard Port"
        ],
        "name": "Non-Standard Port",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may communicate using a protocol and port pairing that are typically not associated. For example, HTTPS over port 8088(Citation: Symantec Elfin Mar 2019) or port 587(Citation: Fortinet Agent Tesla April 2018) as opposed to the traditional port 443. Adversaries may make changes to the standard port used by a protocol to bypass filtering or muddle analysis/parsing of network data.\n\nAdversaries may also make changes to victim systems to abuse non-standard ports. For example, Registry keys and other configuration settings can be used to modify protocol and port pairings.(Citation: change_rdp_port_conti)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1509",
        "standard_attck_name": "Non-Standard Port",
        "gid": 337,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "gamaredon group",
            "fin7",
            "redecho",
            "contagious interview",
            "lazarus group",
            "darkvishnya",
            "wirte",
            "magic hound",
            "velvet ant",
            "apt32",
            "sandworm team",
            "rocke",
            "silence",
            "apt33",
            "ember bear",
            "apt-c-36"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1571",
        "uncertain_name": "non-standard port",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may communicate using a protocol and port pairing that are typically not associated. For example, HTTPS over port 8088(Citation: Symantec Elfin Mar 2019) or port 587(Citation: Fortinet Agent Tesla April 2018) as opposed to the traditional port 443. Adversaries may make changes to the standard port used by a protocol to bypass filtering or muddle analysis/parsing of network data.\n\nAdversaries may also make changes to victim systems to abuse non-standard ports. For example, Registry keys and other configuration settings can be used to modify protocol and port pairings.(Citation: change_rdp_port_conti)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 9.310995746912404,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Non-Standard Port, designated as T1571 under the Command and Control tactic, operates on platforms including Windows, Linux, macOS, and ESXi. This technique involves adversaries conducting communications using a protocol and port pairing that are not typically associated, such as HTTPS over port 8088 or 587 instead of the standard port 443, to bypass network filtering and complicate traffic analysis. Attackers may also modify victim system settings, like Registry keys, to reconfigure services to use these non-standard ports. The method is associated with numerous threat groups including FIN7, Lazarus Group, and APT32."
    },
    "16462": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "exfiltration"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Exfiltration to Cloud Storage"
        ],
        "unique_id": "b110f39b-53fe-412c-9414-d3b0bba475b8",
        "name": "T1567.002",
        "description": "Adversaries may exfiltrate data to a cloud storage service rather than over their primary command and control channel. Cloud storage services allow for the storage, edit, and retrieval of data from a remote cloud storage server over the Internet.\n\nExamples of cloud storage services include Dropbox and Google Docs. Exfiltration to these cloud storage services can provide a significant amount of cover to the adversary if hosts within the network are already communicating with the service.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1567.002",
        "standard_attck_name": "Exfiltration to Cloud Storage",
        "gid": 338,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "zirconium",
            "indrik spider",
            "mustang panda",
            "scattered spider",
            "cinnamon tempest",
            "hafnium",
            "medusa group",
            "turla",
            "threat group-3390",
