        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1567.002",
        "uncertain_name": "exfiltration to cloud storage",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may exfiltrate data to a cloud storage service rather than over their primary command and control channel. Cloud storage services allow for the storage, edit, and retrieval of data from a remote cloud storage server over the Internet.\n\nExamples of cloud storage services include Dropbox and Google Docs. Exfiltration to these cloud storage services can provide a significant amount of cover to the adversary if hosts within the network are already communicating with the service. ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.956754571029638,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1567.002, known as Exfiltration to Cloud Storage, is a technique under the Exfiltration tactic. It targets macOS, Linux, Windows, and ESXi platforms. This method involves adversaries exfiltrating stolen data to public cloud storage services like Dropbox or Google Docs instead of using their primary command and control channels. Leveraging these common services can provide significant operational cover, especially if the targeted network already has legitimate traffic to them, helping the activity blend in."
    },
    "16463": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "exfiltration"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Exfiltration to Code Repository"
        ],
        "unique_id": "18e6783e-9d62-40af-bd6a-05b07f8b0e2a",
        "name": "T1567.001",
        "description": "Adversaries may exfiltrate data to a code repository rather than over their primary command and control channel. Code repositories are often accessible via an API (ex: https://api.github.com). Access to these APIs are often over HTTPS, which gives the adversary an additional level of protection.\n\nExfiltration to a code repository can also provide a significant amount of cover to the adversary if it is a popular service already used by hosts within the network.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1567.001",
        "standard_attck_name": "Exfiltration to Code Repository",
        "gid": 339,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1567.001",
        "uncertain_name": "exfiltration to code repository",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may exfiltrate data to a code repository rather than over their primary command and control channel. Code repositories are often accessible via an API (ex: https://api.github.com). Access to these APIs are often over HTTPS, which gives the adversary an additional level of protection.\n\nExfiltration to a code repository can also provide a significant amount of cover to the adversary if it is a popular service already used by hosts within the network. ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 7.44779377854116,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The technique T1567.001, known as Exfiltration to Code Repository, falls under the Exfiltration tactic and targets macOS, Linux, Windows, and ESXi platforms. It involves adversaries exfiltrating stolen data to a code repository service, such as GitHub, often using its HTTPS-based API. This method provides an additional layer of protection for the data in transit and can blend in with legitimate network traffic if the repository service is commonly used within the victim environment."
    },
    "16464": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "collection"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Clipboard Data"
        ],
        "unique_id": "3f7f6882-2ed8-4f21-947e-f858fdc96477",
        "name": "T1115",
        "description": "Adversaries may collect data stored in the clipboard from users copying information within or between applications. \n\nFor example, on Windows adversaries can access clipboard data by using `clip.exe` or `Get-Clipboard`.(Citation: MSDN Clipboard)(Citation: clip_win_server)(Citation: CISA_AA21_200B) Additionally, adversaries may monitor then replace users’ clipboard with their data (e.g., [Transmitted Data Manipulation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1565/002)).(Citation: mining_ruby_reversinglabs)\n\nmacOS and Linux also have commands, such as `pbpaste`, to grab clipboard contents.(Citation: Operating with EmPyre)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "apt38",
            "apt39",
            "oilrig"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1115",
        "uncertain_name": "clipboard data",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may collect data stored in the clipboard from users copying information within or between applications. \n\nFor example, on Windows adversaries can access clipboard data by using <code>clip.exe</code> or <code>Get-Clipboard</code>.(Citation: MSDN Clipboard)(Citation: clip_win_server)(Citation: CISA_AA21_200B) Additionally, adversaries may monitor then replace users’ clipboard with their data (e.g., [Transmitted Data Manipulation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1565/002)).(Citation: mining_ruby_reversinglabs)\n\nmacOS and Linux also have commands, such as <code>pbpaste</code>, to grab clipboard contents.(Citation: Operating with EmPyre)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.643856155580222,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1115, known as Clipboard Data, is a Collection technique within the MITRE ATTACK framework applicable to Windows, macOS, and Linux platforms. It involves adversaries collecting information users have copied to the clipboard. On Windows, this can be done using utilities like `clip.exe` or PowerShell's `Get-Clipboard`, while macOS and Linux systems can be targeted with commands such as `pbpaste`. This method is used by groups like APT38, APT39, and OilRig to gather sensitive data from a compromised system's clipboard."
    },
    "16465": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
