        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1573.001",
        "uncertain_name": "symmetric cryptography",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may employ a known symmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Symmetric encryption algorithms use the same key for plaintext encryption and ciphertext decryption. Common symmetric encryption algorithms include AES, DES, 3DES, Blowfish, and RC4.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.747479465095381,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1573.001, Symmetric Cryptography, is a command-and-control technique used on platforms including Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves adversaries using symmetric encryption algorithms like AES, DES, or RC4 to conceal C2 traffic, employing the same key for both encryption and decryption. This method is distinct from relying on inherent protocol protections and has been associated with groups such as APT28, Lazarus Group, and MuddyWater."
    },
    "16466": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "command-and-control"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "94f05f49-657c-4a79-a61a-fb85365fb250",
        "aliases": [
            "Standard Encoding"
        ],
        "name": "Standard Encoding",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may encode data with a standard data encoding system to make the content of command and control traffic more difficult to detect. Command and control (C2) information can be encoded using a standard data encoding system that adheres to existing protocol specifications. Common data encoding schemes include ASCII, Unicode, hexadecimal, Base64, and MIME.(Citation: Wikipedia Binary-to-text Encoding)(Citation: Wikipedia Character Encoding) Some data encoding systems may also result in data compression, such as gzip.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1132.001",
        "standard_attck_name": "Standard Encoding",
        "gid": 342,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "hafnium",
            "muddywater",
            "patchwork",
            "tropic trooper",
            "sandworm team",
            "bronze butler",
            "apt42",
            "ta551",
            "lazarus group",
            "apt19",
            "apt33"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1132.001",
        "uncertain_name": "standard encoding",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may encode data with a standard data encoding system to make the content of command and control traffic more difficult to detect. Command and control (C2) information can be encoded using a standard data encoding system that adheres to existing protocol specifications. Common data encoding schemes include ASCII, Unicode, hexadecimal, Base64, and MIME.(Citation: Wikipedia Binary-to-text Encoding)(Citation: Wikipedia Character Encoding) Some data encoding systems may also result in data compression, such as gzip.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 9.75586334082774,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Standard Encoding is the MITRE ATT&CK technique T1132.001 under the Command and Control tactic, applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, and ESXi platforms. It involves obfuscating C2 traffic by encoding data using common, protocol-compliant schemes like ASCII, Unicode, hexadecimal, Base64, or MIME, which can also provide data compression via systems like gzip. This technique is employed by groups including Hafnium, MuddyWater, and Lazarus Group.[profile]"
    },
