        "uncertain_id": "t1059.001",
        "uncertain_name": "powershell",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may abuse PowerShell commands and scripts for execution. PowerShell is a powerful interactive command-line interface and scripting environment included in the Windows operating system.(Citation: TechNet PowerShell) Adversaries can use PowerShell to perform a number of actions, including discovery of information and execution of code. Examples include the <code>Start-Process</code> cmdlet which can be used to run an executable and the <code>Invoke-Command</code> cmdlet which runs a command locally or on a remote computer (though administrator permissions are required to use PowerShell to connect to remote systems).\n\nPowerShell may also be used to download and run executables from the Internet, which can be executed from disk or in memory without touching disk.\n\nA number of PowerShell-based offensive testing tools are available, including [Empire](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0363),  [PowerSploit](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0194), [PoshC2](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0378), and PSAttack.(Citation: Github PSAttack)\n\nPowerShell commands/scripts can also be executed without directly invoking the <code>powershell.exe</code> binary through interfaces to PowerShell's underlying <code>System.Management.Automation</code> assembly DLL exposed through the .NET framework and Windows Common Language Interface (CLI).(Citation: Sixdub PowerPick Jan 2016)(Citation: SilentBreak Offensive PS Dec 2015)(Citation: Microsoft PSfromCsharp APR 2014)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 8.181486235153688,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1059.001 is the MITRE ATT&CK ID for the technique named \"PowerShell\", which falls under the Execution tactic and targets the Windows platform. This technique involves adversaries abusing the PowerShell scripting environment to execute commands and scripts, often for discovery and code execution. Key mechanics include using cmdlets like `Start-Process` to run executables and `Invoke-Command` for remote execution, as well as downloading and running payloads directly in memory to avoid disk. Execution can also occur without the `powershell.exe` binary by leveraging the underlying `System.Management.Automation` assembly. It is commonly used by numerous threat groups and is associated with several CVEs.[/profile]"
    },
    "16469": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "command-and-control"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "eba161ad-4045-4962-b7d9-11cbe0e9dcac",
        "aliases": [
            "Symmetric Cryptography"
        ],
        "name": "Symmetric Cryptography",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may employ a known symmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Symmetric encryption algorithms use the same key for plaintext encryption and ciphertext decryption. Common symmetric encryption algorithms include AES, DES, 3DES, Blowfish, and RC4.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1521.001",
        "standard_attck_name": "Symmetric Cryptography",
        "gid": 345,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "muddywater",
            "zirconium",
            "contagious interview",
            "mustang panda",
            "higaisa",
            "redcurl",
            "inception",
            "stealth falcon",
            "bronze butler",
            "apt28",
            "lazarus group",
            "darkhotel",
            "volt typhoon",
            "apt33"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1573.001",
        "uncertain_name": "symmetric cryptography",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may employ a known symmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Symmetric encryption algorithms use the same key for plaintext encryption and ciphertext decryption. Common symmetric encryption algorithms include AES, DES, 3DES, Blowfish, and RC4.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.078413548734963,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Symmetric Cryptography, identified as T1573.001, is a Command and Control technique used on Windows, Linux, macOS, ESXi, and Network Devices. It involves adversaries using a shared secret key for both encryption and decryption to conceal C2 traffic, independent of a protocol's inherent encryption. Common algorithms for this purpose include AES, DES, 3DES, Blowfish, and RC4. This method is distinct from its asymmetric counterpart (T1573.002) and is associated with threat groups like APT28, Lazarus Group, and MuddyWater.[/profile]"
    },
    "16470": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "collection",
            "credential-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Keylogging"
        ],
        "unique_id": "817cb0a6-b8fd-4de8-acfa-5751fd99bc4f",
        "name": "T1056.001",
        "description": "Adversaries may log user keystrokes to intercept credentials as the user types them. Keylogging is likely to be used to acquire credentials for new access opportunities when [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003) efforts are not effective, and may require an adversary to intercept keystrokes on a system for a substantial period of time before credentials can be successfully captured. In order to increase the likelihood of capturing credentials quickly, an adversary may also perform actions such as clearing browser cookies to force users to reauthenticate to systems.(Citation: Talos Kimsuky Nov 2021)\n\nKeylogging is the most prevalent type of input capture, with many different ways of intercepting keystrokes.(Citation: Adventures of a Keystroke) Some methods include:\n\n* Hooking API callbacks used for processing keystrokes. Unlike [Credential API Hooking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1056/004), this focuses solely on API functions intended for processing keystroke data.\n* Reading raw keystroke data from the hardware buffer.\n* Windows Registry modifications.\n* Custom drivers.\n* [Modify System Image](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1601) may provide adversaries with hooks into the operating system of network devices to read raw keystrokes for login sessions.(Citation: Cisco Blog Legacy Device Attacks)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "fin13",
            "sowbug",
            "apt28",
            "tonto team",
            "platinum",
            "ke3chang",
            "magic hound",
