    "16618": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "resource-development"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Virtual Private Server"
        ],
        "unique_id": "7cbbbc34-8bb1-4aae-98b5-059c4ac7c229",
        "name": "T1583.003",
        "description": "Adversaries may rent Virtual Private Servers (VPSs) that can be used during targeting. There exist a variety of cloud service providers that will sell virtual machines/containers as a service. By utilizing a VPS, adversaries can make it difficult to physically tie back operations to them. The use of cloud infrastructure can also make it easier for adversaries to rapidly provision, modify, and shut down their infrastructure.\n\nAcquiring a VPS for use in later stages of the adversary lifecycle, such as Command and Control, can allow adversaries to benefit from the ubiquity and trust associated with higher reputation cloud service providers. Adversaries may also acquire infrastructure from VPS service providers that are known for renting VPSs with minimal registration information, allowing for more anonymous acquisitions of infrastructure.(Citation: TrendmicroHideoutsLease)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "PRE"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1583.003",
        "standard_attck_name": "Virtual Private Server",
        "gid": 494,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "winter vivern",
            "gamaredon group",
            "dragonfly",
            "sea turtle",
            "hafnium",
            "curium",
            "contagious interview",
            "blackbyte",
            "axiom",
            "moonstone sleet",
            "lapsus$",
            "apt42",
            "apt28",
            "ember bear"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1583.003",
        "uncertain_name": "virtual private server",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may rent Virtual Private Servers (VPSs) that can be used during targeting. There exist a variety of cloud service providers that will sell virtual machines/containers as a service. By utilizing a VPS, adversaries can make it difficult to physically tie back operations to them. The use of cloud infrastructure can also make it easier for adversaries to rapidly provision, modify, and shut down their infrastructure.\n\nAcquiring a VPS for use in later stages of the adversary lifecycle, such as Command and Control, can allow adversaries to benefit from the ubiquity and trust associated with higher reputation cloud service providers. Adversaries may also acquire infrastructure from VPS service providers that are known for renting VPSs with minimal registration information, allowing for more anonymous acquisitions of infrastructure.(Citation: TrendmicroHideoutsLease)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.80543313003672,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1583.003, also known as Acquire Infrastructure: Virtual Private Server, is a resource development technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework, applicable to the PRE platform. Adversaries rent VPSs from cloud providers to provision infrastructure for operations like command and control, benefiting from the providers' reputation and ubiquity. This approach aids in obscuring the physical origin of attacks, with some adversaries specifically choosing VPS services that require minimal registration for anonymity."
    },
    "16619": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "command-and-control"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Dynamic Resolution"
        ],
        "unique_id": "7208ccbb-ff29-4758-ba90-eb2eb453cba9",
        "name": "T1568",
        "description": "Adversaries may dynamically establish connections to command and control infrastructure to evade common detections and remediations. This may be achieved by using malware that shares a common algorithm with the infrastructure the adversary uses to receive the malware's communications. These calculations can be used to dynamically adjust parameters such as the domain name, IP address, or port number the malware uses for command and control.\n\nAdversaries may use dynamic resolution for the purpose of [Fallback Channels](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1008). When contact is lost with the primary command and control server malware may employ dynamic resolution as a means to reestablishing command and control.(Citation: Talos CCleanup 2017)(Citation: FireEye POSHSPY April 2017)(Citation: ESET Sednit 2017 Activity)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1568.001",
        "standard_attck_name": "Fast Flux DNS",
        "gid": 495,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "bitter",
            "ta2541",
            "gamaredon group",
            "redecho",
            "transparent tribe",
            "apt29"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1568",
        "uncertain_name": "dynamic resolution",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may dynamically establish connections to command and control infrastructure to evade common detections and remediations. This may be achieved by using malware that shares a common algorithm with the infrastructure the adversary uses to receive the malware's communications. These calculations can be used to dynamically adjust parameters such as the domain name, IP address, or port number the malware uses for command and control.\n\nAdversaries may use dynamic resolution for the purpose of [Fallback Channels](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1008). When contact is lost with the primary command and control server malware may employ dynamic resolution as a means to reestablishing command and control.(Citation: Talos CCleanup 2017)(Citation: FireEye POSHSPY April 2017)(Citation: ESET Sednit 2017 Activity)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 11.869397107698141,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1568 (Dynamic Resolution) is a command and control technique used across Windows, Linux, macOS, and ESXi platforms. It involves malware dynamically establishing connections to C2 infrastructure to evade detection, often by using a shared algorithm to calculate and adjust parameters like domain names, IP addresses, or port numbers for communication. This technique can facilitate fallback channels and may involve specific sub-techniques like DNS Calculation (T1568.003), where adversaries perform calculations on DNS response data to determine the C2 destination. It is also associated with the use of protocol tunneling methods like DNS over HTTPS to conceal these resolution requests."
    },
