        "uncertain_id": "t1083",
        "uncertain_name": "file and directory discovery",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may enumerate files and directories or may search in specific locations of a host or network share for certain information within a file system. Adversaries may use the information from [File and Directory Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1083) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.\n\nMany command shell utilities can be used to obtain this information. Examples include <code>dir</code>, <code>tree</code>, <code>ls</code>, <code>find</code>, and <code>locate</code>.(Citation: Windows Commands JPCERT) Custom tools may also be used to gather file and directory information and interact with the [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106). Adversaries may also leverage a [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) on network devices to gather file and directory information (e.g. <code>dir</code>, <code>show flash</code>, and/or <code>nvram</code>).(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A)\n\nSome files and directories may require elevated or specific user permissions to access.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 7.55310421625702,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]File and Directory Discovery, identified as T1083, is a Discovery tactic used on platforms including Windows, Linux, macOS, ESXi, and Network Devices. Adversaries enumerate files and directories to gather information that shapes follow-on actions, using command shell utilities like `dir`, `tree`, `ls`, `find`, and `locate`, or custom tools interacting with the Native API. On network devices, this can be performed via CLI commands such as `show flash` or `nvram`.[/profile]"
    },
    "16655": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "command-and-control"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Data Encoding"
        ],
        "unique_id": "d64d3a21-b22f-4318-8f4f-a0784c16737b",
        "name": "Data Encoding",
        "description": "Adversaries may encode data to make the content of command and control traffic more difficult to detect. Command and control (C2) information can be encoded using a standard data encoding system. Use of data encoding may adhere to existing protocol specifications and includes use of ASCII, Unicode, Base64, MIME, or other binary-to-text and character encoding systems.(Citation: Wikipedia Binary-to-text Encoding) (Citation: Wikipedia Character Encoding) Some data encoding systems may also result in data compression, such as gzip.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1132",
        "standard_attck_name": "Data Encoding",
        "gid": 531,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "velvet ant"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1132",
        "uncertain_name": "data encoding",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may encode data to make the content of command and control traffic more difficult to detect. Command and control (C2) information can be encoded using a standard data encoding system. Use of data encoding may adhere to existing protocol specifications and includes use of ASCII, Unicode, Base64, MIME, or other binary-to-text and character encoding systems.(Citation: Wikipedia Binary-to-text Encoding) (Citation: Wikipedia Character Encoding) Some data encoding systems may also result in data compression, such as gzip.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.004586539041924,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Data Encoding is a Command and Control technique, MITRE ATT&CK ID T1132, applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. It involves obfuscating C2 traffic by using standard encoding systems like ASCII, Unicode, Base64, MIME, or hexadecimal to convert binary data into text, which may also provide compression. This technique is employed by groups like Velvet Ant to evade detection by making network traffic blend with normal protocol specifications.[/profile]"
    },
    "16656": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "collection"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Data Staged"
        ],
        "unique_id": "14c70072-11a8-4ffa-bf3a-84fdcbe330b4",
        "name": "T1074",
        "description": "Adversaries may stage collected data in a central location or directory prior to Exfiltration. Data may be kept in separate files or combined into one file through techniques such as [Archive Collected Data](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1560). Interactive command shells may be used, and common functionality within [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106) and bash may be used to copy data into a staging location.(Citation: PWC Cloud Hopper April 2017)\n\nIn cloud environments, adversaries may stage data within a particular instance or virtual machine before exfiltration. An adversary may [Create Cloud Instance](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1578/002) and stage data in that instance.(Citation: Mandiant M-Trends 2020)\n\nAdversaries may choose to stage data from a victim network in a centralized location prior to Exfiltration to minimize the number of connections made to their C2 server and better evade detection.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "IaaS",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1074",
        "standard_attck_name": "Data Staged",
        "gid": 532,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "scattered spider",
            "volt typhoon",
            "wizard spider",
            "inc ransom"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1074",
        "uncertain_name": "data staged",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may stage collected data in a central location or directory prior to Exfiltration. Data may be kept in separate files or combined into one file through techniques such as [Archive Collected Data](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1560). Interactive command shells may be used, and common functionality within [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106) and bash may be used to copy data into a staging location.(Citation: PWC Cloud Hopper April 2017)\n\nIn cloud environments, adversaries may stage data within a particular instance or virtual machine before exfiltration. An adversary may [Create Cloud Instance](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1578/002) and stage data in that instance.(Citation: Mandiant M-Trends 2020)\n\nAdversaries may choose to stage data from a victim network in a centralized location prior to Exfiltration to minimize the number of connections made to their C2 server and better evade detection.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.807296173113453,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity is Data Staged, identified as MITRE ATT&CK technique T1074 under the Collection tactic, applicable to platforms including Windows, Linux, and macOS. This technique involves adversaries staging collected data in a central location or directory, such as within a particular cloud instance, prior to exfiltration to minimize connections and evade detection. Data may be kept in separate files or combined using utilities like `tar`, `zip`, or `diantz.exe`, and common shell commands in cmd or bash are used to copy data into the staging location. Related groups include Scattered Spider, Volt Typhoon, and Wizard Spider.[profile]"
    },
    "16657": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "discovery"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "File and Directory Discovery"
        ],
        "unique_id": "9466c278-718a-4a6a-a08c-79e519d7b6bb",
        "name": "T1083",
        "description": "Adversaries may enumerate files and directories or may search in specific locations of a host or network share for certain information within a file system. Adversaries may use the information from [File and Directory Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1083) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.\n\nMany command shell utilities can be used to obtain this information. Examples include `dir`, `tree`, `ls`, `find`, and `locate`.(Citation: Windows Commands JPCERT) Custom tools may also be used to gather file and directory information and interact with the [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106). Adversaries may also leverage a [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) on network devices to gather file and directory information (e.g. `dir`, `show flash`, and/or `nvram`).(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A)\n\nSome files and directories may require elevated or specific user permissions to access.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
