        "uncertain_id": "t1083",
        "uncertain_name": "file and directory discovery",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may enumerate files and directories or may search in specific locations of a host or network share for certain information within a file system. Adversaries may use the information from [File and Directory Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1083) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.\n\nMany command shell utilities can be used to obtain this information. Examples include <code>dir</code>, <code>tree</code>, <code>ls</code>, <code>find</code>, and <code>locate</code>.(Citation: Windows Commands JPCERT) Custom tools may also be used to gather file and directory information and interact with the [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106). Adversaries may also leverage a [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) on network devices to gather file and directory information (e.g. <code>dir</code>, <code>show flash</code>, and/or <code>nvram</code>).(Citation: US-CERT-TA18-106A)\n\nSome files and directories may require elevated or specific user permissions to access.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 8.432161765694099,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity is the MITRE ATT&CK technique T1083, File and Directory Discovery, under the Discovery tactic. It operates on platforms including Windows, Linux, macOS, ESXi, and Network Devices. The technique involves adversaries enumerating files and directories to gather information, commonly using command shell utilities like `dir`, `tree`, `ls`, `find`, and `locate`, or network device commands such as `show flash` and `nvram`. This information is used to shape subsequent malicious actions, and the activity is associated with numerous threat groups including APT28, MuddyWater, and Volt Typhoon."
    },
    "16658": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "exfiltration"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "7aa0fcf0-f59f-4cd0-97cd-b513ba7e0f94",
        "aliases": [
            "Automated Exfiltration"
        ],
        "name": "Automated Exfiltration",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may exfiltrate data, such as sensitive documents, through the use of automated processing after being gathered during Collection.(Citation: ESET Gamaredon June 2020) \n\nWhen automated exfiltration is used, other exfiltration techniques likely apply as well to transfer the information out of the network, such as [Exfiltration Over C2 Channel](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1041) and [Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1048).",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1020",
        "standard_attck_name": "Automated Exfiltration",
        "gid": 534,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "sidewinder",
            "winter vivern",
            "gamaredon group",
            "ke3chang",
            "tropic trooper",
            "redcurl"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1020",
        "uncertain_name": "automated exfiltration",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may exfiltrate data, such as sensitive documents, through the use of automated processing after being gathered during Collection.(Citation: ESET Gamaredon June 2020) \n\nWhen automated exfiltration is used, other exfiltration techniques likely apply as well to transfer the information out of the network, such as [Exfiltration Over C2 Channel](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1041) and [Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1048).",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.946730085770199,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Automated Exfiltration, designated as T1020, is an Exfiltration tactic used across Windows, Linux, macOS, and Network Devices. This technique involves using automated processing to transfer collected data out of a network, often functioning as a wrapper that leverages other specific exfiltration methods like Exfiltration Over C2 Channel or Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol for the actual data transfer. It has been associated with threat groups including Gamaredon Group, SideWinder, and RedCurl."
    },
