    "16682": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "exfiltration"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Exfiltration Over Unencrypted Non-C2 Protocol"
        ],
        "unique_id": "7417ad5b-4c3b-488b-a8fc-ec12d5f8d585",
        "name": "T1048.003",
        "description": "Adversaries may steal data by exfiltrating it over an un-encrypted network protocol other than that of the existing command and control channel. The data may also be sent to an alternate network location from the main command and control server.(Citation: copy_cmd_cisco)\n\nAdversaries may opt to obfuscate this data, without the use of encryption, within network protocols that are natively unencrypted (such as HTTP, FTP, or DNS). This may include custom or publicly available encoding/compression algorithms (such as base64) as well as embedding data within protocol headers and fields.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "fin6",
            "contagious interview",
            "mustang panda",
            "apt32",
            "salt typhoon",
            "lazarus group",
            "fin8",
            "thrip",
            "oilrig",
            "wizard spider",
            "apt33"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1048.003",
        "uncertain_name": "exfiltration over unencrypted non-c2 protocol",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may steal data by exfiltrating it over an un-encrypted network protocol other than that of the existing command and control channel. The data may also be sent to an alternate network location from the main command and control server.(Citation: copy_cmd_cisco)\n\nAdversaries may opt to obfuscate this data, without the use of encryption, within network protocols that are natively unencrypted (such as HTTP, FTP, or DNS). This may include custom or publicly available encoding/compression algorithms (such as base64) as well as embedding data within protocol headers and fields. ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.730443665182332,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The attack pattern T1048.003, known as Exfiltration Over Unencrypted Non-C2 Protocol, falls under the Exfiltration tactic and targets macOS, Linux, Windows, Network Devices, and ESXi platforms. This technique involves stealing data by transmitting it over a native, unencrypted network protocol like HTTP, FTP, or DNS, which is different from the primary command and control channel. To avoid detection, adversaries may obfuscate the stolen data using methods such as base64 encoding or by embedding information within the headers and fields of the protocol itself, all without employing encryption."
    },
