            "muddywater",
            "dragonfly",
            "machete",
            "zirconium",
            "kimsuky",
            "contagious interview",
            "apt29",
            "redcurl",
            "bronze butler",
            "cinnamon tempest",
            "turla",
            "rocke",
            "tonto team",
            "apt39",
            "unc3886"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1059.006",
        "uncertain_name": "python",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may abuse Python commands and scripts for execution. Python is a very popular scripting/programming language, with capabilities to perform many functions. Python can be executed interactively from the command-line (via the <code>python.exe</code> interpreter) or via scripts (.py) that can be written and distributed to different systems. Python code can also be compiled into binary executables.(Citation: Zscaler APT31 Covid-19 October 2020)\n\nPython comes with many built-in packages to interact with the underlying system, such as file operations and device I/O. Adversaries can use these libraries to download and execute commands or other scripts as well as perform various malicious behaviors.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.208246062213227,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1059.006, also known as Python, is a Command and Scripting Interpreter technique within the Execution tactic, applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, and ESXi platforms. Adversaries abuse the Python interpreter (`python.exe`) or `.py` scripts to execute malicious commands, leveraging its built-in libraries for system interaction, file operations, and device I/O. This technique is commonly used by threat groups including APT37, MuddyWater, and APT29."
    },
    "16700": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "discovery"
        ],
        "aliases": [
            "System Owner/User Discovery"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "c4be9045-ebae-40e4-a0db-8528c84072c5",
        "name": "System Owner/User Discovery",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may attempt to identify the primary user, currently logged in user, set of users that commonly uses a system, or whether a user is actively using the system. They may do this, for example, by retrieving account usernames or by using [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003). The information may be collected in a number of different ways using other Discovery techniques, because user and username details are prevalent throughout a system and include running process ownership, file/directory ownership, session information, and system logs. Adversaries may use the information from [System Owner/User Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1033) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.\n\nVarious utilities and commands may acquire this information, including `whoami`. In macOS and Linux, the currently logged in user can be identified with `w` and `who`. On macOS the `dscl . list /Users | grep -v '_'` command can also be used to enumerate user accounts. Environment variables, such as `%USERNAME%` and `$USER`, may also be used to access this information.\n\nOn network devices, [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008) commands such as `show users` and `show ssh` can be used to display users currently logged into the device.(Citation: show_ssh_users_cmd_cisco)(Citation: US-CERT TA18-106A Network Infrastructure Devices 2018)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1033",
        "standard_attck_name": "System Owner/User Discovery",
        "gid": 576,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "winter vivern",
            "apt37",
            "sidewinder",
            "patchwork",
            "zirconium",
            "storm-1811",
            "windshift",
