            "8780e03bbbe833f797509f9ca0b3fd37eb84b63299a88723c82d9518c56bd5a7",
            "55a0bbde3e32c559715cdc9c7d30d003b9e14725a6369d30edef20c1ed6dd994",
            "8d4b11acce641ec5b33b3fc90ec82a2fcdf2e243cb33558e16d7321488a2c70b",
            "89d4d85592bf0b5e8b55c2d62c9050bfa8c3017f9f497134dbacbb2a0f13a09e",
            "2a183e571fa26a7f74943c42d3997c6b18ed133ee4b749fb1770ffadd7241f1e",
            "1a88ef58675971eb18eeb267b1be90594cd6c7ebddf1c67d66729fa3e68de323",
            "15ce7d3c879975ca81777cf58f47409283e34ec1fe8e966fde608bc7eda16646",
            "170ccf1225154fa0cd92a14219f0b912479cc4095203646c38a31bb78baafe9f"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 6.04872540542902,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Backdoor loader module is a malware component. Its aliases include transferloader and it is associated with the UNC4990 group. Key technical behaviors involve executing a backdoor module, managing its configuration data, and hooking Windows APIs when residing in processes like explorer.exe or wordpad.exe. It targets platforms including Windows and Android, as indicated by its loader module improving crypto algorithms for payloads. Infrastructure includes domains like dynamic.nactagovpk.org and split.tyoin.biz, and it exploits vulnerabilities such as CVE-2017-11882. It has been observed in locations including Morocco, Djibouti, Nepal, Maldives, and Bangladesh.[/profile]"
    },
