    "17006": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion",
            "privilege-escalation"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism"
        ],
        "unique_id": "55c21834-568b-4865-ad17-c706a11bb4f2",
        "name": "T1548",
        "description": "Adversaries may circumvent mechanisms designed to control elevate privileges to gain higher-level permissions. Most modern systems contain native elevation control mechanisms that are intended to limit privileges that a user can perform on a machine. Authorization has to be granted to specific users in order to perform tasks that can be considered of higher risk.(Citation: TechNet How UAC Works)(Citation: sudo man page 2018) An adversary can perform several methods to take advantage of built-in control mechanisms in order to escalate privileges on a system.(Citation: OSX Keydnap malware)(Citation: Fortinet Fareit)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "IaaS",
            "Office Suite",
            "Identity Provider"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1548.003",
        "standard_attck_name": "Sudo and Sudo Caching",
        "gid": 882,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "unc3886"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1548",
        "uncertain_name": "abuse elevation control mechanism",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may circumvent mechanisms designed to control elevate privileges to gain higher-level permissions. Most modern systems contain native elevation control mechanisms that are intended to limit privileges that a user can perform on a machine. Authorization has to be granted to specific users in order to perform tasks that can be considered of higher risk.(Citation: TechNet How UAC Works)(Citation: sudo man page 2018) An adversary can perform several methods to take advantage of built-in control mechanisms in order to escalate privileges on a system.(Citation: OSX Keydnap malware)(Citation: Fortinet Fareit)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 14.193016215224194,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1548, known as Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism, is a Defense Evasion and Privilege Escalation technique applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves circumventing native system controls like User Account Control (UAC) or sudo mechanisms to gain higher-level permissions without proper authorization. Adversaries may abuse legitimate tools such as CMSTP.exe with malicious INF files to bypass UAC or add accounts to privileged groups like sudoers to persistently leverage elevated access."
    },
    "17007": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion",
            "privilege-escalation"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "9bc8d9df-4d89-4157-bc0c-b3071193f334",
        "aliases": [
            "Access Token Manipulation"
        ],
        "name": "Access Token Manipulation",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may modify access tokens to operate under a different user or system security context to perform actions and bypass access controls. Windows uses access tokens to determine the ownership of a running process. A user can manipulate access tokens to make a running process appear as though it is the child of a different process or belongs to someone other than the user that started the process. When this occurs, the process also takes on the security context associated with the new token.\n\nAn adversary can use built-in Windows API functions to copy access tokens from existing processes; this is known as token stealing. These token can then be applied to an existing process (i.e. [Token Impersonation/Theft](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1134/001)) or used to spawn a new process (i.e. [Create Process with Token](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1134/002)). An adversary must already be in a privileged user context (i.e. administrator) to steal a token. However, adversaries commonly use token stealing to elevate their security context from the administrator level to the SYSTEM level. An adversary can then use a token to authenticate to a remote system as the account for that token if the account has appropriate permissions on the remote system.(Citation: Pentestlab Token Manipulation)\n\nAny standard user can use the `runas` command, and the Windows API functions, to create impersonation tokens; it does not require access to an administrator account. There are also other mechanisms, such as Active Directory fields, that can be used to modify access tokens.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1134",
        "standard_attck_name": "Access Token Manipulation",
        "gid": 883,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "fin6",
            "lotus blossom",
            "blue mockingbird"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1134",
        "uncertain_name": "access token manipulation",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may modify access tokens to operate under a different user or system security context to perform actions and bypass access controls. Windows uses access tokens to determine the ownership of a running process. A user can manipulate access tokens to make a running process appear as though it is the child of a different process or belongs to someone other than the user that started the process. When this occurs, the process also takes on the security context associated with the new token.\n\nAn adversary can use built-in Windows API functions to copy access tokens from existing processes; this is known as token stealing. These token can then be applied to an existing process (i.e. [Token Impersonation/Theft](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1134/001)) or used to spawn a new process (i.e. [Create Process with Token](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1134/002)). An adversary must already be in a privileged user context (i.e. administrator) to steal a token. However, adversaries commonly use token stealing to elevate their security context from the administrator level to the SYSTEM level. An adversary can then use a token to authenticate to a remote system as the account for that token if the account has appropriate permissions on the remote system.(Citation: Pentestlab Token Manipulation)\n\nAny standard user can use the <code>runas</code> command, and the Windows API functions, to create impersonation tokens; it does not require access to an administrator account. There are also other mechanisms, such as Active Directory fields, that can be used to modify access tokens.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 11.500820617493744,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Access Token Manipulation, identified as T1134, is a Defense Evasion and Privilege Escalation technique targeting Windows. It involves adversaries using built-in Windows API functions to copy access tokens from processes, a method known as token stealing. These stolen tokens can be applied to an existing process (Token Impersonation/Theft) or used to spawn a new process (Create Process with Token), allowing the adversary to operate under a different user or SYSTEM security context to bypass access controls. While token stealing typically requires an administrator context, standard users can create impersonation tokens using the `runas` command. Associated groups include FIN6 and Lotus Blossom, and related vulnerabilities include CVE-2021-35207 and CVE-2022-27926.[/profile]"
    },
    "17008": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "discovery"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Query Registry"
        ],
        "unique_id": "1f07842a-eeee-43ff-a800-a30dc731c0fa",
        "name": "Query Registry",
        "description": "Adversaries may interact with the Windows Registry to gather information about the system, configuration, and installed software.\n\nThe Registry contains a significant amount of information about the operating system, configuration, software, and security.(Citation: Wikipedia Windows Registry) Information can easily be queried using the [Reg](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0075) utility, though other means to access the Registry exist. Some of the information may help adversaries to further their operation within a network. Adversaries may use the information from [Query Registry](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1012) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1012",
        "standard_attck_name": "Query Registry",
        "gid": 884,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "zirconium",
            "indrik spider",
            "stealth falcon",
            "dragonfly",
            "blackbyte",
            "fox kitten",
            "apt41",
            "turla",
            "threat group-3390",
            "volt typhoon",
            "gamaredon group",
            "chimera",
            "apt32",
            "daggerfly",
            "lotus blossom",
            "lazarus group",
            "oilrig",
            "kimsuky",
            "apt39"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1012",
        "uncertain_name": "query registry",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may interact with the Windows Registry to gather information about the system, configuration, and installed software.\n\nThe Registry contains a significant amount of information about the operating system, configuration, software, and security.(Citation: Wikipedia Windows Registry) Information can easily be queried using the [Reg](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0075) utility, though other means to access the Registry exist. Some of the information may help adversaries to further their operation within a network. Adversaries may use the information from [Query Registry](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1012) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors, including whether or not the adversary fully infects the target and/or attempts specific actions.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 9.439978118711728,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Query Registry is identified as MITRE ATT&CK technique T1012 under the Discovery tactic for Windows platforms. It involves adversaries querying the Windows Registry to gather information about system configuration, installed software, and security settings, often using the built-in Reg utility. This reconnaissance activity helps shape follow-on attack behaviors and is associated with threat groups including APT41, Volt Typhoon, and Lazarus Group.[/profile]"
    },
    "17009": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "lateral-movement"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "6739a2b2-11ea-4929-8dd7-4dac4860536c",
        "aliases": [
            "Remote Services"
        ],
        "name": "Remote Services",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may use [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) to log into a service that accepts remote connections, such as telnet, SSH, and VNC. The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user.\n\nIn an enterprise environment, servers and workstations can be organized into domains. Domains provide centralized identity management, allowing users to login using one set of credentials across the entire network. If an adversary is able to obtain a set of valid domain credentials, they could login to many different machines using remote access protocols such as secure shell (SSH) or remote desktop protocol (RDP).(Citation: SSH Secure Shell)(Citation: TechNet Remote Desktop Services) They could also login to accessible SaaS or IaaS services, such as those that federate their identities to the domain, or management platforms for internal virtualization environments such as VMware vCenter. \n\nLegitimate applications (such as [Software Deployment Tools](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1072) and other administrative programs) may utilize [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021) to access remote hosts. For example, Apple Remote Desktop (ARD) on macOS is native software used for remote management. ARD leverages a blend of protocols, including [VNC](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/005) to send the screen and control buffers and [SSH](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/004) for secure file transfer.(Citation: Remote Management MDM macOS)(Citation: Kickstart Apple Remote Desktop commands)(Citation: Apple Remote Desktop Admin Guide 3.3) Adversaries can abuse applications such as ARD to gain remote code execution and perform lateral movement. In versions of macOS prior to 10.14, an adversary can escalate an SSH session to an ARD session which enables an adversary to accept TCC (Transparency, Consent, and Control) prompts without user interaction and gain access to data.(Citation: FireEye 2019 Apple Remote Desktop)(Citation: Lockboxx ARD 2019)(Citation: Kickstart Apple Remote Desktop commands)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "IaaS",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1021",
        "standard_attck_name": "Remote Services",
        "gid": 885,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "aquatic panda",
            "ember bear",
            "wizard spider"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1021",
        "uncertain_name": "remote services",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) to log into a service that accepts remote connections, such as telnet, SSH, and VNC. The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user.\n\nIn an enterprise environment, servers and workstations can be organized into domains. Domains provide centralized identity management, allowing users to login using one set of credentials across the entire network. If an adversary is able to obtain a set of valid domain credentials, they could login to many different machines using remote access protocols such as secure shell (SSH) or remote desktop protocol (RDP).(Citation: SSH Secure Shell)(Citation: TechNet Remote Desktop Services) They could also login to accessible SaaS or IaaS services, such as those that federate their identities to the domain, or management platforms for internal virtualization environments such as VMware vCenter. \n\nLegitimate applications (such as [Software Deployment Tools](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1072) and other administrative programs) may utilize [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021) to access remote hosts. For example, Apple Remote Desktop (ARD) on macOS is native software used for remote management. ARD leverages a blend of protocols, including [VNC](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/005) to send the screen and control buffers and [SSH](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/004) for secure file transfer.(Citation: Remote Management MDM macOS)(Citation: Kickstart Apple Remote Desktop commands)(Citation: Apple Remote Desktop Admin Guide 3.3) Adversaries can abuse applications such as ARD to gain remote code execution and perform lateral movement. In versions of macOS prior to 10.14, an adversary can escalate an SSH session to an ARD session which enables an adversary to accept TCC (Transparency, Consent, and Control) prompts without user interaction and gain access to data.(Citation: FireEye 2019 Apple Remote Desktop)(Citation: Lockboxx ARD 2019)(Citation: Kickstart Apple Remote Desktop commands)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.23713294196891,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Remote Services, identified as MITRE ATT&CK technique T1021 under the Lateral Movement tactic, is applicable across Windows, Linux, macOS, IaaS, and ESXi platforms. Adversaries use valid accounts to log into services that accept remote connections, such as SSH, RDP, VNC, and telnet, to perform actions on remote systems. This includes abusing legitimate administrative tools like Apple Remote Desktop (ARD) on macOS, which can be leveraged for lateral movement and, in older versions, to bypass security prompts. The technique is associated with vulnerabilities like CVE-2024-1709 and is utilized by threat groups including Aquatic Panda, Ember Bear, and Wizard Spider.[/profile]"
    },
