            "inception",
            "bronze butler",
            "sandworm team",
            "lapsus$",
            "lazarus group",
            "oilrig",
            "ember bear",
            "apt38",
            "kimsuky",
            "windigo",
            "luminousmoth",
            "menupass",
            "andariel",
            "apt39",
            "aquatic panda"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1005",
        "uncertain_name": "data from local system",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may search local system sources, such as file systems, configuration files, local databases, virtual machine files, or process memory, to find files of interest and sensitive data prior to Exfiltration.\n\nAdversaries may do this using a [Command and Scripting Interpreter](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059), such as [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106) as well as a [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008), which have functionality to interact with the file system to gather information.(Citation: show_run_config_cmd_cisco) Adversaries may also use [Automated Collection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1119) on the local system.\n",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 8.212885312681646,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Data from Local System is the MITRE ATT&CK technique T1005 under the Collection tactic, targeting Windows, Linux, macOS, ESXi, and Network Devices. Adversaries search local sources like file systems, configuration files, local databases, virtual machine files, and process memory for sensitive data prior to exfiltration. This is commonly performed using Command and Scripting Interpreters such as cmd or a Network Device CLI, which interact with the file system, and may involve Automated Collection. The technique is associated with numerous threat groups including APT28, Lazarus Group, and Volt Typhoon.[/profile]"
    },
    "17013": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "credential-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "7b49d227-e4fd-4711-983f-157e9f1ec560",
        "aliases": [
            "OS Credential Dumping"
        ],
        "name": "OS Credential Dumping",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may attempt to dump credentials to obtain account login and credential material, normally in the form of a hash or a clear text password. Credentials can be obtained from OS caches, memory, or structures.(Citation: Brining MimiKatz to Unix) Credentials can then be used to perform [Lateral Movement](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0008) and access restricted information.\n\nSeveral of the tools mentioned in associated sub-techniques may be used by both adversaries and professional security testers. Additional custom tools likely exist as well.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1003",
        "standard_attck_name": "OS Credential Dumping",
        "gid": 889,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "blackbyte",
            "poseidon group",
            "axiom",
            "mustang panda",
            "storm-0501",
            "leviathan",
            "apt32",
            "sowbug",
            "apt28",
            "tonto team",
            "suckfly",
            "apt39",
            "ember bear"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1003",
        "uncertain_name": "os credential dumping",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may attempt to dump credentials to obtain account login and credential material, normally in the form of a hash or a clear text password. Credentials can be obtained from OS caches, memory, or structures.(Citation: Brining MimiKatz to Unix) Credentials can then be used to perform [Lateral Movement](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0008) and access restricted information.\n\nSeveral of the tools mentioned in associated sub-techniques may be used by both adversaries and professional security testers. Additional custom tools likely exist as well.\n",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 9.105478986086046,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]OS Credential Dumping is MITRE ATT&CK technique T1003 under the Credential Access tactic. It is applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. This technique involves adversaries dumping credential material, such as password hashes or clear-text passwords, from operating system components like security account manager (SAM) files, Local Security Authority (LSA) secrets, or process memory. Tools like Mimikatz are commonly used for this purpose on Windows, and similar utilities exist for Unix-based systems. The dumped credentials are often used for lateral movement and further system access. Associated vulnerabilities include CVE-2023-20269 and CVE-2024-1709, and it is a technique used by numerous threat groups including APT28, APT32, and Wizard Spider.[/profile]"
    },
