    "17138": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "collection"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Data from Local System"
        ],
        "unique_id": "6a3fc769-0330-4311-8fec-ea9efe6b17c3",
        "name": "T1005",
        "description": "Adversaries may search local system sources, such as file systems, configuration files, local databases, virtual machine files, or process memory, to find files of interest and sensitive data prior to Exfiltration.\n\nAdversaries may do this using a [Command and Scripting Interpreter](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059), such as [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106) as well as a [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008), which have functionality to interact with the file system to gather information.(Citation: show_run_config_cmd_cisco) Adversaries may also use [Automated Collection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1119) on the local system.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "fin13",
            "apt37",
            "fin6",
            "patchwork",
            "curium",
            "axiom",
            "agrius",
            "redcurl",
            "stealth falcon",
            "apt28",
            "hafnium",
            "dragonfly",
            "ke3chang",
            "apt1",
            "magic hound",
            "fox kitten",
            "apt41",
            "wizard spider",
            "turla",
            "dark caracal",
            "threat group-3390",
            "toddycat",
            "apt29",
            "volt typhoon",
            "gamaredon group",
            "fin7",
            "apt3",
            "gallium",
            "inception",
            "bronze butler",
            "sandworm team",
            "lapsus$",
            "lazarus group",
            "oilrig",
            "ember bear",
            "apt38",
            "kimsuky",
            "windigo",
            "luminousmoth",
            "menupass",
            "andariel",
            "apt39",
            "aquatic panda"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1005",
        "uncertain_name": "data from local system",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may search local system sources, such as file systems, configuration files, local databases, virtual machine files, or process memory, to find files of interest and sensitive data prior to Exfiltration.\n\nAdversaries may do this using a [Command and Scripting Interpreter](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059), such as [cmd](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0106) as well as a [Network Device CLI](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/008), which have functionality to interact with the file system to gather information.(Citation: show_run_config_cmd_cisco) Adversaries may also use [Automated Collection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1119) on the local system.\n",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 9.377685176863032,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1005, Data from Local System, is a Collection technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, ESXi, and Network Devices. It involves adversaries searching local file systems, configuration files, local databases, virtual machine files, or process memory to find sensitive data prior to exfiltration. This is often performed using command-line interpreters or automated scripts. The technique is utilized by numerous threat groups and can be facilitated in cloud or virtualized environments by creating new instances with less restrictive policies or by abusing ESXi administration APIs to execute commands on guest VMs.[/profile]"
    },
    "17139": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "lateral-movement"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Remote Services"
        ],
        "unique_id": "22382acf-ab60-4726-a355-3baabd0cfd1b",
        "name": "T1021",
        "description": "Adversaries may use [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) to log into a service that accepts remote connections, such as telnet, SSH, and VNC. The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user.\n\nIn an enterprise environment, servers and workstations can be organized into domains. Domains provide centralized identity management, allowing users to login using one set of credentials across the entire network. If an adversary is able to obtain a set of valid domain credentials, they could login to many different machines using remote access protocols such as secure shell (SSH) or remote desktop protocol (RDP).(Citation: SSH Secure Shell)(Citation: TechNet Remote Desktop Services) They could also login to accessible SaaS or IaaS services, such as those that federate their identities to the domain, or management platforms for internal virtualization environments such as VMware vCenter. \n\nLegitimate applications (such as [Software Deployment Tools](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1072) and other administrative programs) may utilize [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021) to access remote hosts. For example, Apple Remote Desktop (ARD) on macOS is native software used for remote management. ARD leverages a blend of protocols, including [VNC](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/005) to send the screen and control buffers and [SSH](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/004) for secure file transfer.(Citation: Remote Management MDM macOS)(Citation: Kickstart Apple Remote Desktop commands)(Citation: Apple Remote Desktop Admin Guide 3.3) Adversaries can abuse applications such as ARD to gain remote code execution and perform lateral movement. In versions of macOS prior to 10.14, an adversary can escalate an SSH session to an ARD session which enables an adversary to accept TCC (Transparency, Consent, and Control) prompts without user interaction and gain access to data.(Citation: FireEye 2019 Apple Remote Desktop)(Citation: Lockboxx ARD 2019)(Citation: Kickstart Apple Remote Desktop commands)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "IaaS",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1021",
        "standard_attck_name": "Remote Services",
        "gid": 1015,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "aquatic panda",
            "ember bear",
            "wizard spider"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1021",
        "uncertain_name": "remote services",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) to log into a service that accepts remote connections, such as telnet, SSH, and VNC. The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user.\n\nIn an enterprise environment, servers and workstations can be organized into domains. Domains provide centralized identity management, allowing users to login using one set of credentials across the entire network. If an adversary is able to obtain a set of valid domain credentials, they could login to many different machines using remote access protocols such as secure shell (SSH) or remote desktop protocol (RDP).(Citation: SSH Secure Shell)(Citation: TechNet Remote Desktop Services) They could also login to accessible SaaS or IaaS services, such as those that federate their identities to the domain, or management platforms for internal virtualization environments such as VMware vCenter. \n\nLegitimate applications (such as [Software Deployment Tools](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1072) and other administrative programs) may utilize [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021) to access remote hosts. For example, Apple Remote Desktop (ARD) on macOS is native software used for remote management. ARD leverages a blend of protocols, including [VNC](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/005) to send the screen and control buffers and [SSH](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/004) for secure file transfer.(Citation: Remote Management MDM macOS)(Citation: Kickstart Apple Remote Desktop commands)(Citation: Apple Remote Desktop Admin Guide 3.3) Adversaries can abuse applications such as ARD to gain remote code execution and perform lateral movement. In versions of macOS prior to 10.14, an adversary can escalate an SSH session to an ARD session which enables an adversary to accept TCC (Transparency, Consent, and Control) prompts without user interaction and gain access to data.(Citation: FireEye 2019 Apple Remote Desktop)(Citation: Lockboxx ARD 2019)(Citation: Kickstart Apple Remote Desktop commands)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.027498271037825,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1021, known as Remote Services, is a Lateral Movement technique applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, IaaS, and ESXi platforms. It involves adversaries using Valid Accounts to log into services that accept remote connections, such as SSH, RDP, VNC, and telnet, to perform actions as the logged-on user. This enables access across many machines in a domain environment, including SaaS, IaaS, and management platforms like VMware vCenter. Legitimate tools like Apple Remote Desktop (ARD) can also be abused for this purpose, where prior to macOS 10.14, an SSH session could be escalated to an ARD session to bypass user consent prompts. The technique is associated with groups like Aquatic Panda and can be leveraged following other attacks like SID-History Injection."
    },
    "17140": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "execution",
            "persistence",
            "privilege-escalation"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Scheduled Task/Job"
        ],
        "unique_id": "71f1d73a-9cde-4571-b41b-160852b07888",
        "name": "T1053",
        "description": "Adversaries may abuse task scheduling functionality to facilitate initial or recurring execution of malicious code. Utilities exist within all major operating systems to schedule programs or scripts to be executed at a specified date and time. A task can also be scheduled on a remote system, provided the proper authentication is met (ex: RPC and file and printer sharing in Windows environments). Scheduling a task on a remote system typically may require being a member of an admin or otherwise privileged group on the remote system.(Citation: TechNet Task Scheduler Security)\n\nAdversaries may use task scheduling to execute programs at system startup or on a scheduled basis for persistence. These mechanisms can also be abused to run a process under the context of a specified account (such as one with elevated permissions/privileges). Similar to [System Binary Proxy Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1218), adversaries have also abused task scheduling to potentially mask one-time execution under a trusted system process.(Citation: ProofPoint Serpent)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Containers",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1053.005",
        "standard_attck_name": "Scheduled Task",
        "gid": 1016,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1053",
        "uncertain_name": "scheduled task/job",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may abuse task scheduling functionality to facilitate initial or recurring execution of malicious code. Utilities exist within all major operating systems to schedule programs or scripts to be executed at a specified date and time. A task can also be scheduled on a remote system, provided the proper authentication is met (ex: RPC and file and printer sharing in Windows environments). Scheduling a task on a remote system typically may require being a member of an admin or otherwise privileged group on the remote system.(Citation: TechNet Task Scheduler Security)\n\nAdversaries may use task scheduling to execute programs at system startup or on a scheduled basis for persistence. These mechanisms can also be abused to run a process under the context of a specified account (such as one with elevated permissions/privileges). Similar to [System Binary Proxy Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1218), adversaries have also abused task scheduling to potentially mask one-time execution under a trusted system process.(Citation: ProofPoint Serpent)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 2.934514550489134,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1053, known as Scheduled Task/Job, is an attack pattern within the MITRE ATT&CK framework under the Execution, Persistence, and Privilege Escalation tactics. It operates across Windows, Linux, macOS, ESXi, and Container platforms. This technique involves abusing built-in system utilities like cron, at, or the Windows Task Scheduler to execute malicious code at specified times or upon system events, enabling initial execution, recurring persistence, and privilege escalation by running tasks under privileged contexts. It can also be used to schedule tasks on remote systems and to mask execution behind trusted processes. Sub-techniques include T1053.003 (Cron) and T1053.005 (Scheduled Task), and it is often leveraged in conjunction with other techniques like Group Policy Modification.[/profile]"
    },
