    "17216": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Compile After Delivery"
        ],
        "unique_id": "a64bc81c-fe00-4787-81f3-7adf334fea67",
        "name": "T1027.004",
        "description": "Adversaries may attempt to make payloads difficult to discover and analyze by delivering files to victims as uncompiled code. Text-based source code files may subvert analysis and scrutiny from protections targeting executables/binaries. These payloads will need to be compiled before execution; typically via native utilities such as ilasm.exe(Citation: ATTACK IQ), csc.exe, or GCC/MinGW.(Citation: ClearSky MuddyWater Nov 2018)\n\nSource code payloads may also be encrypted, encoded, and/or embedded within other files, such as those delivered as a [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566). Payloads may also be delivered in formats unrecognizable and inherently benign to the native OS (ex: EXEs on macOS/Linux) before later being (re)compiled into a proper executable binary with a bundled compiler and execution framework.(Citation: TrendMicro WindowsAppMac)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "gamaredon group",
            "muddywater",
            "rocke",
            "sea turtle"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1027.004",
        "uncertain_name": "compile after delivery",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may attempt to make payloads difficult to discover and analyze by delivering files to victims as uncompiled code. Text-based source code files may subvert analysis and scrutiny from protections targeting executables/binaries. These payloads will need to be compiled before execution; typically via native utilities such as ilasm.exe(Citation: ATTACK IQ), csc.exe, or GCC/MinGW.(Citation: ClearSky MuddyWater Nov 2018)\n\nSource code payloads may also be encrypted, encoded, and/or embedded within other files, such as those delivered as a [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566). Payloads may also be delivered in formats unrecognizable and inherently benign to the native OS (ex: EXEs on macOS/Linux) before later being (re)compiled into a proper executable binary with a bundled compiler and execution framework.(Citation: TrendMicro WindowsAppMac)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.137989933638101,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1027.004, Obfuscated Files or Information: Compile After Delivery, is a Defense Evasion technique for macOS, Linux, and Windows. It involves delivering payloads as uncompiled source code to evade detection tools that scan for binaries. Adversaries then compile this code on the victim system using native utilities like csc.exe or GCC. This technique is used by groups like Gamaredon Group and MuddyWater, and payloads may be further hidden within phishing attachments or encrypted files."
    },
    "17217": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "d679ff3a-1f66-4d6b-a04a-691a7826e588",
        "aliases": [
            "Compile After Delivery"
        ],
        "name": "Compile After Delivery",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may attempt to make payloads difficult to discover and analyze by delivering files to victims as uncompiled code. Text-based source code files may subvert analysis and scrutiny from protections targeting executables/binaries. These payloads will need to be compiled before execution; typically via native utilities such as ilasm.exe(Citation: ATTACK IQ), csc.exe, or GCC/MinGW.(Citation: ClearSky MuddyWater Nov 2018)\n\nSource code payloads may also be encrypted, encoded, and/or embedded within other files, such as those delivered as a [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566). Payloads may also be delivered in formats unrecognizable and inherently benign to the native OS (ex: EXEs on macOS/Linux) before later being (re)compiled into a proper executable binary with a bundled compiler and execution framework.(Citation: TrendMicro WindowsAppMac)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1500",
        "standard_attck_name": "Compile After Delivery",
        "gid": 1096,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "gamaredon group",
            "muddywater",
            "rocke",
            "sea turtle"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1027.004",
        "uncertain_name": "compile after delivery",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may attempt to make payloads difficult to discover and analyze by delivering files to victims as uncompiled code. Text-based source code files may subvert analysis and scrutiny from protections targeting executables/binaries. These payloads will need to be compiled before execution; typically via native utilities such as ilasm.exe(Citation: ATTACK IQ), csc.exe, or GCC/MinGW.(Citation: ClearSky MuddyWater Nov 2018)\n\nSource code payloads may also be encrypted, encoded, and/or embedded within other files, such as those delivered as a [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566). Payloads may also be delivered in formats unrecognizable and inherently benign to the native OS (ex: EXEs on macOS/Linux) before later being (re)compiled into a proper executable binary with a bundled compiler and execution framework.(Citation: TrendMicro WindowsAppMac)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.19269836658108,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Compile After Delivery is a Defense Evasion technique (T1027.004) applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS. Adversaries deliver payloads as uncompiled source code files to subvert protections targeting binaries, requiring compilation on the victim system before execution. This compilation is typically performed using native utilities like csc.exe, GCC, or MSBuild, or via a bundled compiler. The source code may be further obfuscated through encryption or embedding within other files like phishing attachments to hinder analysis. Groups such as MuddyWater and Gamaredon Group have been observed using this method."
    },
