            "7795a7f3bd08cb62ec6f828ad1f6836114b3e8cf153d905e3f03d6199f1f8354",
            "6005ecce702b84de6d46838839b2271df631ab42325b70e27324e6cabda76e7f",
            "898a5bd86c5d99eb70088a90f1d8f90b03bd38c15a232200538d0601c888acb6",
            "d7be3494b3e1722eb28f317f3b85ee68bf7ea5508aa2d5782392619e078b78af",
            "97982e098a4538d05e78c172c9bbc5b412754df86dc73e760004f0038ec928fb",
            "5299fe79a66b407555cdab68806564ae988b745be589767b004f7bccd7f7ac3b"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "gelsemium"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "firewood",
        "uncertain_mes": "A new and low-detected variant of the FireWood backdoor was discovered by Intezer’s Research Team, with some changes in the implementation and the configuration of the backdoor.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.400380408859009,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]FireWood is a backdoor malware. Its aliases include none explicitly stated. It is attributed to the Gelsemium group. Key TTPs include using encrypted C2 channels (T1573), rootkit functionality (T1014) for defense evasion, indicator removal (T1070), abuse of remote services (T1021) for lateral movement, and registry modification (T1112) for persistence. It targets Windows systems. Associated infrastructure includes domains like domain.dns04.com, info.96html.com, sitesafecdn.hopto.org, acro.ns1.name, and dsdsei.com, with victim locations in Iran, Singapore, the Philippines, and Taiwan.[/profile]"
    },
