        "uncertain_id": "t1087",
        "uncertain_name": "account discovery",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of valid accounts, usernames, or email addresses on a system or within a compromised environment. This information can help adversaries determine which accounts exist, which can aid in follow-on behavior such as brute-forcing, spear-phishing attacks, or account takeovers (e.g., [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078)).\n\nAdversaries may use several methods to enumerate accounts, including abuse of existing tools, built-in commands, and potential misconfigurations that leak account names and roles or permissions in the targeted environment.\n\nFor examples, cloud environments typically provide easily accessible interfaces to obtain user lists.(Citation: AWS List Users)(Citation: Google Cloud - IAM Servie Accounts List API) On hosts, adversaries can use default [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001) and other command line functionality to identify accounts. Information about email addresses and accounts may also be extracted by searching an infected system’s files.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 11.101981396183433,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Account Discovery is identified as MITRE ATT&CK technique T1087 under the Discovery tactic, applicable across Windows, Linux, macOS, and cloud platforms like IaaS and SaaS. It involves adversaries enumerating valid accounts, usernames, or email addresses to enable follow-on attacks. Technical mechanics include using built-in commands such as `net user /domain` for domain accounts, `dscl . list /Users` on macOS, `ldapsearch` on Linux, and cloud CLI commands like `aws iam list-users` or `az ad user list`. Information can also be gathered by searching local files or abusing APIs and misconfigurations in cloud environments to leak user lists and permissions.[/profile]"
    },
