            "2847ae693533406defecb226bfe6d62dd36905ff07add4e773426bde83e85ddc",
            "c290ab5d8ce9fcaa91da3b488c93dee1a4d0581c1335f19cb48027a5a03fe525",
            "88f2d42bf225c930bc644f82bbd229e170d53dd1072e846e2883265a7ac33301",
            "844cc1807cc5b628b7aa807ef3b682d051c8ad5427df3d3e36c7e7633bfc5768",
            "944be9bb167a2f76fe2f539d3860bbf26301830c479bc68509af46e047993c8c",
            "95995686b9af8b56c3fed1dadccf8b2ed5f417bb4eb8947a406a6e943cca33c6"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "未知"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "sshdoor",
        "uncertain_mes": "In his summary of New Year predictions by security researchers here at ESET, Stephen Cobb pointed to expanded efforts by malware authors to target the Linux operating system. Looks like that might be right: A blog post published by Sucuri yesterday describes a backdoored version of the SSH daemon discovered on compromised servers.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.443270760105154,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]SSHDoor, also known as SSHDoor.CE, Linux/SSHDoor.CF and \"bl0wsshd00r67p1\", is a backdoored SSH server daemon compiled from modified OpenSSH source code. It is attributed to the Water Barghest group. Its key behaviors include modifying the authentication process (T1556) to log remote hosts, usernames, and passwords, allowing attackers to log in as root, and employing anti-logging features and data encryption. It primarily targets Linux platforms and has used infrastructure domains like packinstall.kozow.com and changepassword.giize.com.[/profile]"
    },
