    "17904": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Exploitation for Defense Evasion"
        ],
        "unique_id": "2b69d1d1-59cc-45c4-aa8b-46f276a3be3d",
        "name": "T1211",
        "description": "Adversaries may exploit a system or application vulnerability to bypass security features. Exploitation of a vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. Vulnerabilities may exist in defensive security software that can be used to disable or circumvent them.\n\nAdversaries may have prior knowledge through reconnaissance that security software exists within an environment or they may perform checks during or shortly after the system is compromised for [Security Software Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1518/001). The security software will likely be targeted directly for exploitation. There are examples of antivirus software being targeted by persistent threat groups to avoid detection.\n\nThere have also been examples of vulnerabilities in public cloud infrastructure of SaaS applications that may bypass defense boundaries (Citation: Salesforce zero-day in facebook phishing attack), evade security logs (Citation: Bypassing CloudTrail in AWS Service Catalog), or deploy hidden infrastructure.(Citation: GhostToken GCP flaw)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "IaaS",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1211",
        "standard_attck_name": "Exploitation for Defense Evasion",
        "gid": 1783,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "velvet ant",
            "apt28"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1211",
        "uncertain_name": "exploitation for defense evasion",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may exploit a system or application vulnerability to bypass security features. Exploitation of a vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. Vulnerabilities may exist in defensive security software that can be used to disable or circumvent them.\n\nAdversaries may have prior knowledge through reconnaissance that security software exists within an environment or they may perform checks during or shortly after the system is compromised for [Security Software Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1518/001). The security software will likely be targeted directly for exploitation. There are examples of antivirus software being targeted by persistent threat groups to avoid detection.\n\nThere have also been examples of vulnerabilities in public cloud infrastructure of SaaS applications that may bypass defense boundaries (Citation: Salesforce zero-day in facebook phishing attack), evade security logs (Citation: Bypassing CloudTrail in AWS Service Catalog), or deploy hidden infrastructure.(Citation: GhostToken GCP flaw)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 14.742543927972363,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1211 - Exploitation for Defense Evasion is a Defense Evasion technique used on Windows, Linux, macOS, IaaS, and SaaS platforms. It involves exploiting vulnerabilities, such as CVE-2024-4947, to bypass or disable security features. Adversaries target defensive software directly, as seen with antivirus programs, or exploit flaws in cloud infrastructure to evade logs and deploy hidden resources, with groups like Velvet Ant and APT28 linked to its use.[/profile]"
    },
    "17905": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "reconnaissance"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "c6a270c8-1a84-4333-9c09-972b978df735",
        "aliases": [
            "Phishing for Information"
        ],
        "name": "Phishing for Information",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may send phishing messages to elicit sensitive information that can be used during targeting. Phishing for information is an attempt to trick targets into divulging information, frequently credentials or other actionable information. Phishing for information is different from [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566) in that the objective is gathering data from the victim rather than executing malicious code.\n\nAll forms of phishing are electronically delivered social engineering. Phishing can be targeted, known as spearphishing. In spearphishing, a specific individual, company, or industry will be targeted by the adversary. More generally, adversaries can conduct non-targeted phishing, such as in mass credential harvesting campaigns.\n\nAdversaries may also try to obtain information directly through the exchange of emails, instant messages, or other electronic conversation means.(Citation: ThreatPost Social Media Phishing)(Citation: TrendMictro Phishing)(Citation: PCMag FakeLogin)(Citation: Sophos Attachment)(Citation: GitHub Phishery) Victims may also receive phishing messages that direct them to call a phone number where the adversary attempts to collect confidential information.(Citation: Avertium callback phishing)\n\nPhishing for information frequently involves social engineering techniques, such as posing as a source with a reason to collect information (ex: [Establish Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1585) or [Compromise Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1586)) and/or sending multiple, seemingly urgent messages. Another way to accomplish this is by [Email Spoofing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1672)(Citation: Proofpoint-spoof) the identity of the sender, which can be used to fool both the human recipient as well as automated security tools.(Citation: cyberproof-double-bounce) \n\nPhishing for information may also involve evasive techniques, such as removing or manipulating emails or metadata/headers from compromised accounts being abused to send messages (e.g., [Email Hiding Rules](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/008)).(Citation: Microsoft OAuth Spam 2022)(Citation: Palo Alto Unit 42 VBA Infostealer 2014)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "PRE"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1598",
        "standard_attck_name": "Phishing for Information",
        "gid": 1784,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "zirconium",
            "kimsuky",
            "scattered spider",
            "moonstone sleet",
            "apt28"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1598",
        "uncertain_name": "phishing for information",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may send phishing messages to elicit sensitive information that can be used during targeting. Phishing for information is an attempt to trick targets into divulging information, frequently credentials or other actionable information. Phishing for information is different from [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566) in that the objective is gathering data from the victim rather than executing malicious code.\n\nAll forms of phishing are electronically delivered social engineering. Phishing can be targeted, known as spearphishing. In spearphishing, a specific individual, company, or industry will be targeted by the adversary. More generally, adversaries can conduct non-targeted phishing, such as in mass credential harvesting campaigns.\n\nAdversaries may also try to obtain information directly through the exchange of emails, instant messages, or other electronic conversation means.(Citation: ThreatPost Social Media Phishing)(Citation: TrendMictro Phishing)(Citation: PCMag FakeLogin)(Citation: Sophos Attachment)(Citation: GitHub Phishery) Victims may also receive phishing messages that direct them to call a phone number where the adversary attempts to collect confidential information.(Citation: Avertium callback phishing)\n\nPhishing for information frequently involves social engineering techniques, such as posing as a source with a reason to collect information (ex: [Establish Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1585) or [Compromise Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1586)) and/or sending multiple, seemingly urgent messages. Another way to accomplish this is by [Email Spoofing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1672)(Citation: Proofpoint-spoof) the identity of the sender, which can be used to fool both the human recipient as well as automated security tools.(Citation: cyberproof-double-bounce) \n\nPhishing for information may also involve evasive techniques, such as removing or manipulating emails or metadata/headers from compromised accounts being abused to send messages (e.g., [Email Hiding Rules](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/008)).(Citation: Microsoft OAuth Spam 2022)(Citation: Palo Alto Unit 42 VBA Infostealer 2014)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.447719076591515,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Phishing for Information is the MITRE ATT&CK technique T1598 under the Reconnaissance tactic, targeting the PRE platform. It involves adversaries sending electronic messages, such as emails or instant messages, to trick victims into divulging sensitive data like credentials. This technique is distinct from code-execution phishing and often employs social engineering, such as posing as a trusted source or using email spoofing. It can be conducted via targeted spearphishing or broad campaigns and may include evasive tactics like manipulating email headers from compromised accounts."
    },
    "17906": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "exfiltration"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Exfiltration Over Web Service"
        ],
        "unique_id": "90f857e0-2441-46c2-b946-3572be59ff4f",
        "name": "T1567",
        "description": "Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service to exfiltrate data rather than their primary command and control channel. Popular Web services acting as an exfiltration mechanism may give a significant amount of cover due to the likelihood that hosts within a network are already communicating with them prior to compromise. Firewall rules may also already exist to permit traffic to these services.\n\nWeb service providers also commonly use SSL/TLS encryption, giving adversaries an added level of protection.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi",
            "Office Suite",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1567",
        "standard_attck_name": "Exfiltration Over Web Service",
        "gid": 1785,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "contagious interview",
            "blackbyte",
            "magic hound",
            "apt28"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1567",
        "uncertain_name": "exfiltration over web service",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external Web service to exfiltrate data rather than their primary command and control channel. Popular Web services acting as an exfiltration mechanism may give a significant amount of cover due to the likelihood that hosts within a network are already communicating with them prior to compromise. Firewall rules may also already exist to permit traffic to these services.\n\nWeb service providers also commonly use SSL/TLS encryption, giving adversaries an added level of protection.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.127151738442947,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1567, Exfiltration Over Web Service, is an Exfiltration tactic used on platforms including Windows, Linux, and macOS. Adversaries exfiltrate data by leveraging legitimate external web services (e.g., Google, GitHub, or Twitter) instead of their primary C2 channel. This provides cover through pre-existing network communication and firewall rules to these common services, and often benefits from the inherent SSL/TLS encryption offered by the providers. Sub-techniques include exfiltration to code repositories (T1567.001) and text storage sites (T1567.003)."
    },
    "17907": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "execution"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Container Administration Command"
        ],
        "unique_id": "121bfca3-e20b-4afe-8b7a-33da8fc73595",
        "name": "T1609",
        "description": "Adversaries may abuse a container administration service to execute commands within a container. A container administration service such as the Docker daemon, the Kubernetes API server, or the kubelet may allow remote management of containers within an environment.(Citation: Docker Daemon CLI)(Citation: Kubernetes API)(Citation: Kubernetes Kubelet)\n\nIn Docker, adversaries may specify an entrypoint during container deployment that executes a script or command, or they may use a command such as `docker exec` to execute a command within a running container.(Citation: Docker Entrypoint)(Citation: Docker Exec) In Kubernetes, if an adversary has sufficient permissions, they may gain remote execution in a container in the cluster via interaction with the Kubernetes API server, the kubelet, or by running a command such as `kubectl exec`.(Citation: Kubectl Exec Get Shell)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "Containers"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1609",
        "standard_attck_name": "Container Administration Command",
        "gid": 1786,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "teamtnt"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1609",
        "uncertain_name": "container administration command",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may abuse a container administration service to execute commands within a container. A container administration service such as the Docker daemon, the Kubernetes API server, or the kubelet may allow remote management of containers within an environment.(Citation: Docker Daemon CLI)(Citation: Kubernetes API)(Citation: Kubernetes Kubelet)\n\nIn Docker, adversaries may specify an entrypoint during container deployment that executes a script or command, or they may use a command such as <code>docker exec</code> to execute a command within a running container.(Citation: Docker Entrypoint)(Citation: Docker Exec) In Kubernetes, if an adversary has sufficient permissions, they may gain remote execution in a container in the cluster via interaction with the Kubernetes API server, the kubelet, or by running a command such as <code>kubectl exec</code>.(Citation: Kubectl Exec Get Shell)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 16.12667925425332,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1609, also known as Container Administration Command, is an execution technique within the MITRE ATTACK framework targeting container platforms. It involves adversaries abusing container administration services like the Docker daemon or Kubernetes API server to execute commands inside containers. Specific methods include using `docker exec` to run commands in a running container, specifying a malicious entrypoint during deployment, or leveraging `kubectl exec` via the Kubernetes API for remote execution, often to facilitate further actions like container escape. This technique has been associated with groups like TeamTNT."
    },
    "17908": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "credential-access",
            "defense-evasion",
            "persistence"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Modify Authentication Process"
        ],
        "unique_id": "65aeb213-5745-412c-9af2-6c727548e2e7",
        "name": "T1556",
        "description": "Adversaries may modify authentication mechanisms and processes to access user credentials or enable otherwise unwarranted access to accounts. The authentication process is handled by mechanisms, such as the Local Security Authentication Server (LSASS) process and the Security Accounts Manager (SAM) on Windows, pluggable authentication modules (PAM) on Unix-based systems, and authorization plugins on MacOS systems, responsible for gathering, storing, and validating credentials. By modifying an authentication process, an adversary may be able to authenticate to a service or system without using [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078).\n\nAdversaries may maliciously modify a part of this process to either reveal credentials or bypass authentication mechanisms. Compromised credentials or access may be used to bypass access controls placed on various resources on systems within the network and may even be used for persistent access to remote systems and externally available services, such as VPNs, Outlook Web Access and remote desktop.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "IaaS",
            "Office Suite",
            "Identity Provider",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1556",
        "standard_attck_name": "Modify Authentication Process",
        "gid": 1787,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "fin13"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1556",
        "uncertain_name": "modify authentication process",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may modify authentication mechanisms and processes to access user credentials or enable otherwise unwarranted access to accounts. The authentication process is handled by mechanisms, such as the Local Security Authentication Server (LSASS) process and the Security Accounts Manager (SAM) on Windows, pluggable authentication modules (PAM) on Unix-based systems, and authorization plugins on MacOS systems, responsible for gathering, storing, and validating credentials. By modifying an authentication process, an adversary may be able to authenticate to a service or system without using [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078).\n\nAdversaries may maliciously modify a part of this process to either reveal credentials or bypass authentication mechanisms. Compromised credentials or access may be used to bypass access controls placed on various resources on systems within the network and may even be used for persistent access to remote systems and externally available services, such as VPNs, Outlook Web Access and remote desktop.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 15.434627720664817,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1556, known as Modify Authentication Process, is a Credential Access, Defense Evasion, and Persistence technique applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, and cloud platforms. It involves maliciously altering core authentication components like Windows LSASS, Unix PAM, or macOS authorization plugins to either steal credentials or bypass authentication entirely, enabling persistent access without valid accounts. This technique can be implemented by modifying host software binaries, such as SSH clients, to capture login credentials. A related sub-technique, T1556.009, involves disabling or modifying conditional access policies in systems like Entra ID to remove security controls like MFA. It has been associated with threat groups including FIN13, Scattered Spider, and Storm-0501.[/profile]"
    },
    "17909": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "initial-access",
            "lateral-movement"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Replication Through Removable Media"
        ],
        "unique_id": "d366f9c2-331a-49e0-9c0f-47fd19ba282c",
        "name": "T1091",
        "description": "Adversaries may move onto systems, possibly those on disconnected or air-gapped networks, by copying malware to removable media and taking advantage of Autorun features when the media is inserted into a system and executes. In the case of Lateral Movement, this may occur through modification of executable files stored on removable media or by copying malware and renaming it to look like a legitimate file to trick users into executing it on a separate system. In the case of Initial Access, this may occur through manual manipulation of the media, modification of systems used to initially format the media, or modification to the media's firmware itself.\n\nMobile devices may also be used to infect PCs with malware if connected via USB.(Citation: Exploiting Smartphone USB ) This infection may be achieved using devices (Android, iOS, etc.) and, in some instances, USB charging cables.(Citation: Windows Malware Infecting Android)(Citation: iPhone Charging Cable Hack) For example, when a smartphone is connected to a system, it may appear to be mounted similar to a USB-connected disk drive. If malware that is compatible with the connected system is on the mobile device, the malware could infect the machine (especially if Autorun features are enabled).",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "windows"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "gamaredon group",
            "fin7",
            "tropic trooper",
            "mustang panda",
            "luminousmoth",
            "apt28",
            "aoqin dragon",
            "darkhotel"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1091",
        "uncertain_name": "replication through removable media",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may move onto systems, possibly those on disconnected or air-gapped networks, by copying malware to removable media and taking advantage of Autorun features when the media is inserted into a system and executes. In the case of Lateral Movement, this may occur through modification of executable files stored on removable media or by copying malware and renaming it to look like a legitimate file to trick users into executing it on a separate system. In the case of Initial Access, this may occur through manual manipulation of the media, modification of systems used to initially format the media, or modification to the media's firmware itself.\n\nMobile devices may also be used to infect PCs with malware if connected via USB.(Citation: Exploiting Smartphone USB ) This infection may be achieved using devices (Android, iOS, etc.) and, in some instances, USB charging cables.(Citation: Windows Malware Infecting Android)(Citation: iPhone Charging Cable Hack) For example, when a smartphone is connected to a system, it may appear to be mounted similar to a USB-connected disk drive. If malware that is compatible with the connected system is on the mobile device, the malware could infect the machine (especially if Autorun features are enabled).",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.954529109618797,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1091, Replication Through Removable Media, is an attack technique used for Initial Access and Lateral Movement on Windows platforms. It involves copying malware to removable media like USB drives to exploit Autorun features for automatic execution when the media is connected. This method can also use mobile devices connected via USB to infect PCs. The technique is associated with threat groups including Gamaredon Group, FIN7, and APT28."
    },
    "17910": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "reconnaissance"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Gather Victim Host Information"
        ],
        "unique_id": "d7ac1e36-7630-43e5-8c24-8f97c519e54c",
        "name": "T1592",
        "description": "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's hosts that can be used during targeting. Information about hosts may include a variety of details, including administrative data (ex: name, assigned IP, functionality, etc.) as well as specifics regarding its configuration (ex: operating system, language, etc.).\n\nAdversaries may gather this information in various ways, such as direct collection actions via [Active Scanning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1595) or [Phishing for Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598). Adversaries may also compromise sites then include malicious content designed to collect host information from visitors.(Citation: ATT ScanBox) Information about hosts may also be exposed to adversaries via online or other accessible data sets (ex: [Social Media](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1593/001) or [Search Victim-Owned Websites](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1594)). Gathering this information may reveal opportunities for other forms of reconnaissance (ex: [Search Open Websites/Domains](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1593) or [Search Open Technical Databases](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1596)), establishing operational resources (ex: [Develop Capabilities](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1587) or [Obtain Capabilities](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588)), and/or initial access (ex: [Supply Chain Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1195) or [External Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1133)).\n\nAdversaries may also gather victim host information via User-Agent HTTP headers, which are sent to a server to identify the application, operating system, vendor, and/or version of the requesting user agent. This can be used to inform the adversary’s follow-on action. For example, adversaries may check user agents for the requesting operating system, then only serve malware for target operating systems while ignoring others.(Citation: TrellixQakbot)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "PRE"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1592",
        "standard_attck_name": "Gather Victim Host Information",
        "gid": 1789,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "volt typhoon"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1592",
        "uncertain_name": "gather victim host information",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's hosts that can be used during targeting. Information about hosts may include a variety of details, including administrative data (ex: name, assigned IP, functionality, etc.) as well as specifics regarding its configuration (ex: operating system, language, etc.).\n\nAdversaries may gather this information in various ways, such as direct collection actions via [Active Scanning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1595) or [Phishing for Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598). Adversaries may also compromise sites then include malicious content designed to collect host information from visitors.(Citation: ATT ScanBox) Information about hosts may also be exposed to adversaries via online or other accessible data sets (ex: [Social Media](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1593/001) or [Search Victim-Owned Websites](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1594)). Gathering this information may reveal opportunities for other forms of reconnaissance (ex: [Search Open Websites/Domains](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1593) or [Search Open Technical Databases](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1596)), establishing operational resources (ex: [Develop Capabilities](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1587) or [Obtain Capabilities](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588)), and/or initial access (ex: [Supply Chain Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1195) or [External Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1133)).\n\nAdversaries may also gather victim host information via User-Agent HTTP headers, which are sent to a server to identify the application, operating system, vendor, and/or version of the requesting user agent. This can be used to inform the adversary’s follow-on action. For example, adversaries may check user agents for the requesting operating system, then only serve malware for target operating systems while ignoring others.(Citation: TrellixQakbot)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 14.190142171080733,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1592, named Gather Victim Host Information, is a reconnaissance technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework applicable to the PRE platform. It involves collecting details about a victim's hosts, such as administrative data, operating system, and software versions, to inform subsequent attack stages. This information can be gathered through methods like active scanning, phishing, or by compromising websites to deploy malicious scripts that profile visitors via their User-Agent HTTP headers. The technique is associated with groups like Volt Typhoon and is often a precursor to vulnerability scanning and other forms of reconnaissance."
    },
    "17911": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "resource-development"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "ad846455-2b0c-48e0-8b68-7d91ce9e0923",
        "aliases": [
            "Acquire Infrastructure"
        ],
        "name": "Acquire Infrastructure",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may buy, lease, rent, or obtain infrastructure that can be used during targeting. A wide variety of infrastructure exists for hosting and orchestrating adversary operations. Infrastructure solutions include physical or cloud servers, domains, and third-party web services.(Citation: TrendmicroHideoutsLease) Some infrastructure providers offer free trial periods, enabling infrastructure acquisition at limited to no cost.(Citation: Free Trial PurpleUrchin) Additionally, botnets are available for rent or purchase.\n\nUse of these infrastructure solutions allows adversaries to stage, launch, and execute operations. Solutions may help adversary operations blend in with traffic that is seen as normal, such as contacting third-party web services or acquiring infrastructure to support [Proxy](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1090), including from residential proxy services.(Citation: amnesty_nso_pegasus)(Citation: FBI Proxies Credential Stuffing)(Citation: Mandiant APT29 Microsoft 365 2022) Depending on the implementation, adversaries may use infrastructure that makes it difficult to physically tie back to them as well as utilize infrastructure that can be rapidly provisioned, modified, and shut down.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "PRE"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1583",
        "standard_attck_name": "Acquire Infrastructure",
        "gid": 1790,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "sea turtle",
            "kimsuky",
            "contagious interview",
            "star blizzard",
            "indrik spider",
            "agrius",
            "sandworm team",
            "ember bear"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1583",
        "uncertain_name": "acquire infrastructure",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may buy, lease, rent, or obtain infrastructure that can be used during targeting. A wide variety of infrastructure exists for hosting and orchestrating adversary operations. Infrastructure solutions include physical or cloud servers, domains, and third-party web services.(Citation: TrendmicroHideoutsLease) Some infrastructure providers offer free trial periods, enabling infrastructure acquisition at limited to no cost.(Citation: Free Trial PurpleUrchin) Additionally, botnets are available for rent or purchase.\n\nUse of these infrastructure solutions allows adversaries to stage, launch, and execute operations. Solutions may help adversary operations blend in with traffic that is seen as normal, such as contacting third-party web services or acquiring infrastructure to support [Proxy](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1090), including from residential proxy services.(Citation: amnesty_nso_pegasus)(Citation: FBI Proxies Credential Stuffing)(Citation: Mandiant APT29 Microsoft 365 2022) Depending on the implementation, adversaries may use infrastructure that makes it difficult to physically tie back to them as well as utilize infrastructure that can be rapidly provisioned, modified, and shut down.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.255646251194408,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Acquire Infrastructure is a Resource Development tactic (T1583) within the MITRE ATT&CK framework, applicable to pre-attack platforms (PRE). It involves adversaries buying, leasing, renting, or otherwise obtaining infrastructure—such as physical/cloud servers, domains, botnets, and third-party web services—to host and orchestrate operations. This infrastructure can be rapidly provisioned, helps blend malicious traffic with normal activity (e.g., by supporting proxy services), and is often acquired from providers offering free trials or minimal registration to obscure attribution. Related sub-techniques include acquiring Virtual Private Servers (T1583.003).[/profile]"
    },
    "17912": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "aliases": [
            "Exploitation for Defense Evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "6f8ff93e-a23a-4dd8-9d5a-fd1bdc2b009e",
        "name": "Exploitation for Defense Evasion",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may exploit a system or application vulnerability to bypass security features. Exploitation of a vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. Vulnerabilities may exist in defensive security software that can be used to disable or circumvent them.\n\nAdversaries may have prior knowledge through reconnaissance that security software exists within an environment or they may perform checks during or shortly after the system is compromised for [Security Software Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1518/001). The security software will likely be targeted directly for exploitation. There are examples of antivirus software being targeted by persistent threat groups to avoid detection.\n\nThere have also been examples of vulnerabilities in public cloud infrastructure of SaaS applications that may bypass defense boundaries (Citation: Salesforce zero-day in facebook phishing attack), evade security logs (Citation: Bypassing CloudTrail in AWS Service Catalog), or deploy hidden infrastructure.(Citation: GhostToken GCP flaw)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "IaaS",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1211",
        "standard_attck_name": "Exploitation for Defense Evasion",
        "gid": 1791,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "velvet ant",
            "apt28"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1211",
        "uncertain_name": "exploitation for defense evasion",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may exploit a system or application vulnerability to bypass security features. Exploitation of a vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. Vulnerabilities may exist in defensive security software that can be used to disable or circumvent them.\n\nAdversaries may have prior knowledge through reconnaissance that security software exists within an environment or they may perform checks during or shortly after the system is compromised for [Security Software Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1518/001). The security software will likely be targeted directly for exploitation. There are examples of antivirus software being targeted by persistent threat groups to avoid detection.\n\nThere have also been examples of vulnerabilities in public cloud infrastructure of SaaS applications that may bypass defense boundaries (Citation: Salesforce zero-day in facebook phishing attack), evade security logs (Citation: Bypassing CloudTrail in AWS Service Catalog), or deploy hidden infrastructure.(Citation: GhostToken GCP flaw)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.827910850664104,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Exploitation for Defense Evasion, identified as T1211, is a Defense Evasion technique applicable across Windows, Linux, macOS, IaaS, and SaaS platforms. It involves exploiting vulnerabilities, particularly in defensive security software or cloud infrastructure, to bypass or disable security features. Adversaries target these vulnerabilities to execute their own code, evading detection by mechanisms like antivirus or cloud security logs, as observed in campaigns by groups like APT28 and Velvet Ant."
    },
    "17913": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "initial-access",
            "lateral-movement"
        ],
        "aliases": [
            "Replication Through Removable Media"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "7953e86a-0ffa-4769-af9e-a4f7c30ac813",
        "name": "Replication Through Removable Media",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may move onto systems, possibly those on disconnected or air-gapped networks, by copying malware to removable media and taking advantage of Autorun features when the media is inserted into a system and executes. In the case of Lateral Movement, this may occur through modification of executable files stored on removable media or by copying malware and renaming it to look like a legitimate file to trick users into executing it on a separate system. In the case of Initial Access, this may occur through manual manipulation of the media, modification of systems used to initially format the media, or modification to the media's firmware itself.\n\nMobile devices may also be used to infect PCs with malware if connected via USB.(Citation: Exploiting Smartphone USB ) This infection may be achieved using devices (Android, iOS, etc.) and, in some instances, USB charging cables.(Citation: Windows Malware Infecting Android)(Citation: iPhone Charging Cable Hack) For example, when a smartphone is connected to a system, it may appear to be mounted similar to a USB-connected disk drive. If malware that is compatible with the connected system is on the mobile device, the malware could infect the machine (especially if Autorun features are enabled).",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1458",
        "standard_attck_name": "Replication Through Removable Media",
        "gid": 1792,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "gamaredon group",
            "fin7",
            "tropic trooper",
            "mustang panda",
            "luminousmoth",
            "apt28",
            "aoqin dragon",
            "darkhotel"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1091",
        "uncertain_name": "replication through removable media",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may move onto systems, possibly those on disconnected or air-gapped networks, by copying malware to removable media and taking advantage of Autorun features when the media is inserted into a system and executes. In the case of Lateral Movement, this may occur through modification of executable files stored on removable media or by copying malware and renaming it to look like a legitimate file to trick users into executing it on a separate system. In the case of Initial Access, this may occur through manual manipulation of the media, modification of systems used to initially format the media, or modification to the media's firmware itself.\n\nMobile devices may also be used to infect PCs with malware if connected via USB.(Citation: Exploiting Smartphone USB ) This infection may be achieved using devices (Android, iOS, etc.) and, in some instances, USB charging cables.(Citation: Windows Malware Infecting Android)(Citation: iPhone Charging Cable Hack) For example, when a smartphone is connected to a system, it may appear to be mounted similar to a USB-connected disk drive. If malware that is compatible with the connected system is on the mobile device, the malware could infect the machine (especially if Autorun features are enabled).",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 11.97910282245683,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Replication Through Removable Media, designated as T1091, is a technique used in the Initial Access and Lateral Movement tactics on Windows platforms. It involves copying malware to removable media like USB drives to infect systems, often exploiting Autorun features for automatic execution when the media is inserted. Attackers may also modify legitimate files on the media or use mobile devices connected via USB to deliver the payload. This method has been associated with groups including APT28, Gamaredon Group, and Fin7."
    },
    "17914": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "reconnaissance"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Phishing for Information"
        ],
        "unique_id": "2cc15c3a-98eb-4e9f-a311-4f34383187d6",
        "name": "T1598",
        "description": "Adversaries may send phishing messages to elicit sensitive information that can be used during targeting. Phishing for information is an attempt to trick targets into divulging information, frequently credentials or other actionable information. Phishing for information is different from [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566) in that the objective is gathering data from the victim rather than executing malicious code.\n\nAll forms of phishing are electronically delivered social engineering. Phishing can be targeted, known as spearphishing. In spearphishing, a specific individual, company, or industry will be targeted by the adversary. More generally, adversaries can conduct non-targeted phishing, such as in mass credential harvesting campaigns.\n\nAdversaries may also try to obtain information directly through the exchange of emails, instant messages, or other electronic conversation means.(Citation: ThreatPost Social Media Phishing)(Citation: TrendMictro Phishing)(Citation: PCMag FakeLogin)(Citation: Sophos Attachment)(Citation: GitHub Phishery) Victims may also receive phishing messages that direct them to call a phone number where the adversary attempts to collect confidential information.(Citation: Avertium callback phishing)\n\nPhishing for information frequently involves social engineering techniques, such as posing as a source with a reason to collect information (ex: [Establish Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1585) or [Compromise Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1586)) and/or sending multiple, seemingly urgent messages. Another way to accomplish this is by [Email Spoofing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1672)(Citation: Proofpoint-spoof) the identity of the sender, which can be used to fool both the human recipient as well as automated security tools.(Citation: cyberproof-double-bounce) \n\nPhishing for information may also involve evasive techniques, such as removing or manipulating emails or metadata/headers from compromised accounts being abused to send messages (e.g., [Email Hiding Rules](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/008)).(Citation: Microsoft OAuth Spam 2022)(Citation: Palo Alto Unit 42 VBA Infostealer 2014)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "PRE"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1598",
        "standard_attck_name": "Phishing for Information",
        "gid": 1793,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "zirconium",
            "kimsuky",
            "scattered spider",
            "moonstone sleet",
            "apt28"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1598",
        "uncertain_name": "phishing for information",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may send phishing messages to elicit sensitive information that can be used during targeting. Phishing for information is an attempt to trick targets into divulging information, frequently credentials or other actionable information. Phishing for information is different from [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566) in that the objective is gathering data from the victim rather than executing malicious code.\n\nAll forms of phishing are electronically delivered social engineering. Phishing can be targeted, known as spearphishing. In spearphishing, a specific individual, company, or industry will be targeted by the adversary. More generally, adversaries can conduct non-targeted phishing, such as in mass credential harvesting campaigns.\n\nAdversaries may also try to obtain information directly through the exchange of emails, instant messages, or other electronic conversation means.(Citation: ThreatPost Social Media Phishing)(Citation: TrendMictro Phishing)(Citation: PCMag FakeLogin)(Citation: Sophos Attachment)(Citation: GitHub Phishery) Victims may also receive phishing messages that direct them to call a phone number where the adversary attempts to collect confidential information.(Citation: Avertium callback phishing)\n\nPhishing for information frequently involves social engineering techniques, such as posing as a source with a reason to collect information (ex: [Establish Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1585) or [Compromise Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1586)) and/or sending multiple, seemingly urgent messages. Another way to accomplish this is by [Email Spoofing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1672)(Citation: Proofpoint-spoof) the identity of the sender, which can be used to fool both the human recipient as well as automated security tools.(Citation: cyberproof-double-bounce) \n\nPhishing for information may also involve evasive techniques, such as removing or manipulating emails or metadata/headers from compromised accounts being abused to send messages (e.g., [Email Hiding Rules](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1564/008)).(Citation: Microsoft OAuth Spam 2022)(Citation: Palo Alto Unit 42 VBA Infostealer 2014)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.134576928243895,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1598, known as Phishing for Information, is a reconnaissance technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework, operating on the PRE platform. It involves adversaries sending phishing messages, such as emails or instant messages, to trick specific targets or broad audiences into divulging sensitive information like credentials. This is distinct from code-execution phishing. Techniques include spearphishing, email spoofing to impersonate trusted sources, and using compromised accounts with evasive tactics like manipulating email headers or hiding rules. Groups like APT28 and Kimsuky have been associated with this method."
    },
    "17915": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "execution"
        ],
        "aliases": [
            "Container Administration Command"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "3a55a674-f42d-48e6-bb86-7e2d23872f04",
        "name": "Container Administration Command",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may abuse a container administration service to execute commands within a container. A container administration service such as the Docker daemon, the Kubernetes API server, or the kubelet may allow remote management of containers within an environment.(Citation: Docker Daemon CLI)(Citation: Kubernetes API)(Citation: Kubernetes Kubelet)\n\nIn Docker, adversaries may specify an entrypoint during container deployment that executes a script or command, or they may use a command such as `docker exec` to execute a command within a running container.(Citation: Docker Entrypoint)(Citation: Docker Exec) In Kubernetes, if an adversary has sufficient permissions, they may gain remote execution in a container in the cluster via interaction with the Kubernetes API server, the kubelet, or by running a command such as `kubectl exec`.(Citation: Kubectl Exec Get Shell)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "Containers"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1609",
        "standard_attck_name": "Container Administration Command",
        "gid": 1794,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "teamtnt"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1609",
        "uncertain_name": "container administration command",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may abuse a container administration service to execute commands within a container. A container administration service such as the Docker daemon, the Kubernetes API server, or the kubelet may allow remote management of containers within an environment.(Citation: Docker Daemon CLI)(Citation: Kubernetes API)(Citation: Kubernetes Kubelet)\n\nIn Docker, adversaries may specify an entrypoint during container deployment that executes a script or command, or they may use a command such as <code>docker exec</code> to execute a command within a running container.(Citation: Docker Entrypoint)(Citation: Docker Exec) In Kubernetes, if an adversary has sufficient permissions, they may gain remote execution in a container in the cluster via interaction with the Kubernetes API server, the kubelet, or by running a command such as <code>kubectl exec</code>.(Citation: Kubectl Exec Get Shell)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 15.040483359038003,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Container Administration Command is a MITRE ATT&CK technique with ID T1609 under the Execution tactic, targeting Container platforms. It involves abusing administration services like the Docker daemon or Kubernetes API to execute commands within containers. Specific mechanics include using `docker exec` to run commands in a running Docker container or leveraging `kubectl exec` and the Kubernetes API for remote execution in cluster containers, often requiring sufficient permissions. The technique is associated with groups like TeamTNT and can exploit vulnerabilities for initial access.[profile]"
    },
