        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "HeadLace",
        "is_family": false,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [
            "dad1a8869c950c2d1d322c8aed3757d3988ef4f06ba230b329c8d510d8d9a027",
            "c6a91cba00bf87cdb064c49adaac82255cbec6fdd48fd21f9b3b96abf019916b",
            "7c85ff89b535a39d47756dfce4597c239ee16df88badefe8f76051b836a7cbfb",
            "cda936ecae566ab871e5c0303d8ff98796b1e3661885afd9d4690fc1e945640e",
            "a06d74322a8761ec8e6f28d134f2a89c7ba611d920d080a3ccbfac7c3b61e2e7",
            "6b96b991e33240e5c2091d092079a440fa1bef9b5aecbf3039bf7c47223bdf96"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "未知"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "headlace",
        "uncertain_mes": "This week, the CERT Polska (CSIRT NASK) and CSIRT MON teams observed a large-scale malware campaign targeting Polish government institutions. Based on technical indicators and similarity to attacks described in the past (e.g.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 7.699500340161678,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]HeadLace is a multi-component malware that includes a dropper, a VBS launcher, and a backdoor which leverages MSEdge in headless mode to continuously download secondary payloads for credential and data exfiltration. It employs Masquerading, such as disguising itself as a Windows update and printing fake status messages, and relies on User Execution and Phishing for initial access. Its attack patterns also include Command and Scripting Interpreter abuse. It has targeted Polish government institutions.[/profile]"
    },
