    "18158": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "persistence"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Create Account"
        ],
        "unique_id": "ee4f9822-b950-4352-9444-92bf0ebc7826",
        "name": "T1136",
        "description": "Adversaries may create an account to maintain access to victim systems.(Citation: Symantec WastedLocker June 2020) With a sufficient level of access, creating such accounts may be used to establish secondary credentialed access that do not require persistent remote access tools to be deployed on the system.\n\nAccounts may be created on the local system or within a domain or cloud tenant. In cloud environments, adversaries may create accounts that only have access to specific services, which can reduce the chance of detection.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "Containers",
            "IaaS",
            "ESXi",
            "Office Suite",
            "Identity Provider",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1136",
        "standard_attck_name": "Create Account",
        "gid": 2041,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "scattered spider",
            "indrik spider",
            "salt typhoon"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1136",
        "uncertain_name": "create account",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may create an account to maintain access to victim systems.(Citation: Symantec WastedLocker June 2020) With a sufficient level of access, creating such accounts may be used to establish secondary credentialed access that do not require persistent remote access tools to be deployed on the system.\n\nAccounts may be created on the local system or within a domain or cloud tenant. In cloud environments, adversaries may create accounts that only have access to specific services, which can reduce the chance of detection.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.249470448084924,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1136, Create Account, is a Persistence technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework applicable across Windows, Linux, macOS, and cloud platforms. Adversaries create local, domain, or cloud tenant accounts to establish secondary credentialed access, reducing reliance on persistent malware. In cloud environments, accounts may be created with limited service-specific permissions to evade detection. This activity is often followed by modifications to account roles or permissions for privilege escalation."
    },
    "18159": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "impact"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "f2d8ce35-f673-4a86-99c6-9ac4dc1f6e17",
        "aliases": [
            "Data Encrypted for Impact"
        ],
        "name": "Data Encrypted for Impact",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may encrypt data on target systems or on large numbers of systems in a network to interrupt availability to system and network resources. They can attempt to render stored data inaccessible by encrypting files or data on local and remote drives and withholding access to a decryption key. This may be done in order to extract monetary compensation from a victim in exchange for decryption or a decryption key (ransomware) or to render data permanently inaccessible in cases where the key is not saved or transmitted.(Citation: US-CERT Ransomware 2016)(Citation: FireEye WannaCry 2017)(Citation: US-CERT NotPetya 2017)(Citation: US-CERT SamSam 2018)\n\nIn the case of ransomware, it is typical that common user files like Office documents, PDFs, images, videos, audio, text, and source code files will be encrypted (and often renamed and/or tagged with specific file markers). Adversaries may need to first employ other behaviors, such as [File and Directory Permissions Modification](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1222) or [System Shutdown/Reboot](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1529), in order to unlock and/or gain access to manipulate these files.(Citation: CarbonBlack Conti July 2020) In some cases, adversaries may encrypt critical system files, disk partitions, and the MBR.(Citation: US-CERT NotPetya 2017) Adversaries may also encrypt virtual machines hosted on ESXi or other hypervisors.(Citation: Crowdstrike Hypervisor Jackpotting Pt 2 2021) \n\nTo maximize impact on the target organization, malware designed for encrypting data may have worm-like features to propagate across a network by leveraging other attack techniques like [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078), [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003), and [SMB/Windows Admin Shares](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/002).(Citation: FireEye WannaCry 2017)(Citation: US-CERT NotPetya 2017) Encryption malware may also leverage [Internal Defacement](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1491/001), such as changing victim wallpapers or ESXi server login messages, or otherwise intimidate victims by sending ransom notes or other messages to connected printers (known as \"print bombing\").(Citation: NHS Digital Egregor Nov 2020)(Citation: Varonis)\n\nIn cloud environments, storage objects within compromised accounts may also be encrypted.(Citation: Rhino S3 Ransomware Part 1) For example, in AWS environments, adversaries may leverage services such as AWS’s Server-Side Encryption with Customer Provided Keys (SSE-C) to encrypt data.(Citation: Halcyon AWS Ransomware 2025)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "IaaS",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1471",
        "standard_attck_name": "Data Encrypted for Impact",
        "gid": 2042,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "apt38",
            "fin7",
            "inc ransom",
            "ta505",
            "blackbyte",
            "storm-1811",
            "indrik spider",
            "magic hound",
            "medusa group",
            "akira",
            "apt41",
            "scattered spider",
            "sandworm team",
            "moonstone sleet",
            "water galura",
            "fin8",
            "storm-0501"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1486",
        "uncertain_name": "data encrypted for impact",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may encrypt data on target systems or on large numbers of systems in a network to interrupt availability to system and network resources. They can attempt to render stored data inaccessible by encrypting files or data on local and remote drives and withholding access to a decryption key. This may be done in order to extract monetary compensation from a victim in exchange for decryption or a decryption key (ransomware) or to render data permanently inaccessible in cases where the key is not saved or transmitted.(Citation: US-CERT Ransomware 2016)(Citation: FireEye WannaCry 2017)(Citation: US-CERT NotPetya 2017)(Citation: US-CERT SamSam 2018)\n\nIn the case of ransomware, it is typical that common user files like Office documents, PDFs, images, videos, audio, text, and source code files will be encrypted (and often renamed and/or tagged with specific file markers). Adversaries may need to first employ other behaviors, such as [File and Directory Permissions Modification](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1222) or [System Shutdown/Reboot](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1529), in order to unlock and/or gain access to manipulate these files.(Citation: CarbonBlack Conti July 2020) In some cases, adversaries may encrypt critical system files, disk partitions, and the MBR.(Citation: US-CERT NotPetya 2017) Adversaries may also encrypt virtual machines hosted on ESXi or other hypervisors.(Citation: Crowdstrike Hypervisor Jackpotting Pt 2 2021) \n\nTo maximize impact on the target organization, malware designed for encrypting data may have worm-like features to propagate across a network by leveraging other attack techniques like [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078), [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003), and [SMB/Windows Admin Shares](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/002).(Citation: FireEye WannaCry 2017)(Citation: US-CERT NotPetya 2017) Encryption malware may also leverage [Internal Defacement](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1491/001), such as changing victim wallpapers or ESXi server login messages, or otherwise intimidate victims by sending ransom notes or other messages to connected printers (known as \"print bombing\").(Citation: NHS Digital Egregor Nov 2020)(Citation: Varonis)\n\nIn cloud environments, storage objects within compromised accounts may also be encrypted.(Citation: Rhino S3 Ransomware Part 1) For example, in AWS environments, adversaries may leverage services such as AWS’s Server-Side Encryption with Customer Provided Keys (SSE-C) to encrypt data.(Citation: Halcyon AWS Ransomware 2025)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 9.95358502570497,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Data Encrypted for Impact, designated as T1486 within the MITRE ATT&CK framework, is an Impact tactic technique targeting Windows, Linux, macOS, IaaS, and ESXi platforms. Adversaries encrypt data on local and remote drives, including critical system files, disk partitions, the MBR, and virtual machines on hypervisors like ESXi, to render it inaccessible and disrupt availability, often for ransomware extortion. The technique may involve preparatory actions like File and Directory Permissions Modification or System Shutdown/Reboot to access files, and can propagate via worm-like features using other techniques such as Valid Accounts and SMB/Windows Admin Shares. In cloud environments, adversaries may encrypt storage objects using services like AWS's Server-Side Encryption with Customer Provided Keys (SSE-C). Associated threat groups include APT38, FIN7, and Sandworm Team.[/profile]"
    },
    "18160": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "impact"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "77f2c417-f258-40e4-bedf-7a776ddeecd2",
        "aliases": [
            "Network Denial of Service"
        ],
        "name": "Network Denial of Service",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may perform Network Denial of Service (DoS) attacks to degrade or block the availability of targeted resources to users. Network DoS can be performed by exhausting the network bandwidth services rely on. Example resources include specific websites, email services, DNS, and web-based applications. Adversaries have been observed conducting network DoS attacks for political purposes(Citation: FireEye OpPoisonedHandover February 2016) and to support other malicious activities, including distraction(Citation: FSISAC FraudNetDoS September 2012), hacktivism, and extortion.(Citation: Symantec DDoS October 2014)\n\nA Network DoS will occur when the bandwidth capacity of the network connection to a system is exhausted due to the volume of malicious traffic directed at the resource or the network connections and network devices the resource relies on. For example, an adversary may send 10Gbps of traffic to a server that is hosted by a network with a 1Gbps connection to the internet. This traffic can be generated by a single system or multiple systems spread across the internet, which is commonly referred to as a distributed DoS (DDoS).\n\nTo perform Network DoS attacks several aspects apply to multiple methods, including IP address spoofing, and botnets.\n\nAdversaries may use the original IP address of an attacking system, or spoof the source IP address to make the attack traffic more difficult to trace back to the attacking system or to enable reflection. This can increase the difficulty defenders have in defending against the attack by reducing or eliminating the effectiveness of filtering by the source address on network defense devices.\n\nFor DoS attacks targeting the hosting system directly, see [Endpoint Denial of Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1499).",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Containers",
            "IaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1464",
        "standard_attck_name": "Network Denial of Service",
        "gid": 2043,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "apt28"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1498",
        "uncertain_name": "network denial of service",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may perform Network Denial of Service (DoS) attacks to degrade or block the availability of targeted resources to users. Network DoS can be performed by exhausting the network bandwidth services rely on. Example resources include specific websites, email services, DNS, and web-based applications. Adversaries have been observed conducting network DoS attacks for political purposes(Citation: FireEye OpPoisonedHandover February 2016) and to support other malicious activities, including distraction(Citation: FSISAC FraudNetDoS September 2012), hacktivism, and extortion.(Citation: Symantec DDoS October 2014)\n\nA Network DoS will occur when the bandwidth capacity of the network connection to a system is exhausted due to the volume of malicious traffic directed at the resource or the network connections and network devices the resource relies on. For example, an adversary may send 10Gbps of traffic to a server that is hosted by a network with a 1Gbps connection to the internet. This traffic can be generated by a single system or multiple systems spread across the internet, which is commonly referred to as a distributed DoS (DDoS).\n\nTo perform Network DoS attacks several aspects apply to multiple methods, including IP address spoofing, and botnets.\n\nAdversaries may use the original IP address of an attacking system, or spoof the source IP address to make the attack traffic more difficult to trace back to the attacking system or to enable reflection. This can increase the difficulty defenders have in defending against the attack by reducing or eliminating the effectiveness of filtering by the source address on network defense devices.\n\nFor DoS attacks targeting the hosting system directly, see [Endpoint Denial of Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1499).",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.214665490833344,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Network Denial of Service, identified as MITRE ATT&CK technique T1498, is an Impact tactic executed on platforms including Windows, Linux, macOS, Containers, and IaaS. The technique works by exhausting the bandwidth capacity of a target's network connection through a high volume of malicious traffic, such as sending 10Gbps to a server on a 1Gbps link. This can be achieved via a single system or a distributed network (DDoS), often employing IP address spoofing to obscure the attack source and using botnets to generate traffic. It is distinct from Endpoint Denial of Service (T1499) which targets system resources directly.[/profile]"
    },
    "18161": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion",
            "discovery"
        ],
        "aliases": [
            "Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "db640ba9-e441-48d2-9998-043cf4cf660c",
        "name": "Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may employ various means to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behaviors based on the results of checks for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox. If the adversary detects a VME, they may alter their malware to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for VME artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads. Adversaries may use the information learned from [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors.(Citation: Deloitte Environment Awareness)\n\nAdversaries may use several methods to accomplish [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497) such as checking for security monitoring tools (e.g., Sysinternals, Wireshark, etc.) or other system artifacts associated with analysis or virtualization. Adversaries may also check for legitimate user activity to help determine if it is in an analysis environment. Additional methods include use of sleep timers or loops within malware code to avoid operating within a temporary sandbox.(Citation: Unit 42 Pirpi July 2015)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1633",
        "standard_attck_name": "Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion",
        "gid": 2044,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "contagious interview",
            "saint bear",
            "darkhotel"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1497",
        "uncertain_name": "virtualization/sandbox evasion",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may employ various means to detect and avoid virtualization and analysis environments. This may include changing behaviors based on the results of checks for the presence of artifacts indicative of a virtual machine environment (VME) or sandbox. If the adversary detects a VME, they may alter their malware to disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant. They may also search for VME artifacts before dropping secondary or additional payloads. Adversaries may use the information learned from [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497) during automated discovery to shape follow-on behaviors.(Citation: Deloitte Environment Awareness)\n\nAdversaries may use several methods to accomplish [Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497) such as checking for security monitoring tools (e.g., Sysinternals, Wireshark, etc.) or other system artifacts associated with analysis or virtualization. Adversaries may also check for legitimate user activity to help determine if it is in an analysis environment. Additional methods include use of sleep timers or loops within malware code to avoid operating within a temporary sandbox.(Citation: Unit 42 Pirpi July 2015)\n\n",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 11.131163672255308,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion is MITRE ATT&CK technique T1497, used for Defense Evasion and Discovery on Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. It involves detecting analysis environments by checking for artifacts like security tools (e.g., Sysinternals), virtual machine hardware/processes, registry keys, or low user activity, and then altering malware behavior—such as disengaging, delaying execution with sleep timers, or withholding payloads—to evade automated sandbox analysis.[/profile]"
    },
    "18162": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [],
        "unique_id": "c15fd4c8-6971-4603-93d2-8245806c99ff",
        "name": "T1155",
        "description": "",
        "kill_chain_names": [],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1155",
        "standard_attck_name": "AppleScript",
        "gid": 2045,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1155",
        "uncertain_name": "applescript",
        "uncertain_mes": "macOS and OS X applications send AppleEvent messages to each other for interprocess communications (IPC). These messages can be easily scripted with AppleScript for local or remote IPC. Osascript executes AppleScript and any other Open Scripting Architecture (OSA) language scripts. A list of OSA languages installed on a system can be found by using the <code>osalang</code> program.\nAppleEvent messages can be sent independently or as part of a script. These events can locate open windows, send keystrokes, and interact with almost any open application locally or remotely. \n\nAdversaries can use this to interact with open SSH connection, move to remote machines, and even present users with fake dialog boxes. These events cannot start applications remotely (they can start them locally though), but can interact with applications if they're already running remotely. Since this is a scripting language, it can be used to launch more common techniques as well such as a reverse shell via python  (Citation: Macro Malware Targets Macs). Scripts can be run from the command-line via <code>osascript /path/to/script</code> or <code>osascript -e \"script here\"</code>.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 6.065754593317335,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity is AppleScript, identified as MITRE ATT&CK technique T1155 under the Execution tactic for the macOS platform. It is a scripting language used for interprocess communication (IPC) via AppleEvent messages, allowing interaction with open applications locally or remotely through commands like `osascript`. Adversaries leverage it to send keystrokes, present fake dialog boxes for credential theft, and establish persistence by registering login items. It cannot remotely start applications but can script interactions with already running ones, including open SSH connections."
    },
    "18163": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "persistence"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Create Account"
        ],
        "unique_id": "0e826aec-a3e5-4fc3-aa06-c3703169d40f",
        "name": "Create Account",
        "description": "Adversaries may create an account to maintain access to victim systems.(Citation: Symantec WastedLocker June 2020) With a sufficient level of access, creating such accounts may be used to establish secondary credentialed access that do not require persistent remote access tools to be deployed on the system.\n\nAccounts may be created on the local system or within a domain or cloud tenant. In cloud environments, adversaries may create accounts that only have access to specific services, which can reduce the chance of detection.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "Containers",
            "IaaS",
            "ESXi",
            "Office Suite",
            "Identity Provider",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1136",
        "standard_attck_name": "Create Account",
        "gid": 2046,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "scattered spider",
            "indrik spider",
            "salt typhoon"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1136",
        "uncertain_name": "create account",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may create an account to maintain access to victim systems.(Citation: Symantec WastedLocker June 2020) With a sufficient level of access, creating such accounts may be used to establish secondary credentialed access that do not require persistent remote access tools to be deployed on the system.\n\nAccounts may be created on the local system or within a domain or cloud tenant. In cloud environments, adversaries may create accounts that only have access to specific services, which can reduce the chance of detection.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 11.593131468552627,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Create Account is a persistence technique (T1136) applicable across Windows, Linux, macOS, and network/cloud platforms. It involves adversaries creating new user accounts to establish secondary, credentialed access without deploying persistent remote tools. Specific mechanics include using commands like `net user /add` on Windows, `useradd` on Linux, `dscl -create` on macOS, or `username` commands on network devices. Accounts can be created locally (T1136.001), within a domain (T1136.002), or in cloud tenants, often with restricted permissions to evade detection."
    },
