            "fcefba64cfd18a3899cb5c87328eabad18a0efebfb5d8f8e774c570cad332e64",
            "3a6887963920c8bc1ae35fdca69af2c0865f8b5c6ef90b4db91fa152bc56050d",
            "01273b6bb129a54d59e91c389a71add9892d392ea5f145169ae628ec99eda935",
            "e90d5c6ee2bb69dcd327ca344263ce1e033a04c6e054c69c46b01236691b7641",
            "fa7ad2f45128120bccc33f996f87a81faa2e9c1236666dd69b943a755f332eb1"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "mustang panda"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "pubload",
        "uncertain_mes": "[PUBLOAD](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1228) is a stager malware that has been observed installing itself in existing directories such as `C:\\Users\\Public` or creating new directories to stage the malware and its components.(Citation: 2022 November_TrendMicro_Earth Preta_Toneshell_Pubload)  [PUBLOAD](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1228) malware collects details of the victim host, establishes persistence, encrypts victim details using RC4 and communicates victim details back to C2.  [PUBLOAD](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1228) malware has previously been leveraged by China-affiliated actors identified as [Mustang Panda](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0129).   [PUBLOAD](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1228) is also known as “NoFive” and some public reporting identifies the loader component as [CLAIMLOADER](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1236).(Citation: 2025_IBM_PUBLOAD_TONESHELL_HIUPAN_CLAIMLOADER_MUSTANG PANDA)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.409275446490186,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]PUBLOAD, also known as NoFive and with its loader component identified as CLAIMLOADER, is a stager malware attributed to the China-affiliated group Mustang Panda. It installs itself in directories like C:\\Users\\Public, collects victim host details, establishes persistence, and encrypts victim information using RC4 before exfiltrating it to command-and-control servers. Its technical behaviors align with MITRE ATT&CK techniques including Unsecured Credentials (T1552), Supply Chain Compromise (T1195), Access Token Manipulation (T1134), Subvert Trust Controls (T1553), and Data Encoding (T1132). It primarily targets Windows systems, with infrastructure involving domains like www.openservername.com and IPs such as 188.208.141.196, and has been observed in regions including Laos, Myanmar, Cambodia, Singapore, and Japan.[/profile]"
    },
