    "18742": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Subvert Trust Controls"
        ],
        "unique_id": "4dc9447f-0340-40bb-aef4-31bff5b3ea53",
        "name": "T1553",
        "description": "Adversaries may undermine security controls that will either warn users of untrusted activity or prevent execution of untrusted programs. Operating systems and security products may contain mechanisms to identify programs or websites as possessing some level of trust. Examples of such features would include a program being allowed to run because it is signed by a valid code signing certificate, a program prompting the user with a warning because it has an attribute set from being downloaded from the Internet, or getting an indication that you are about to connect to an untrusted site.\n\nAdversaries may attempt to subvert these trust mechanisms. The method adversaries use will depend on the specific mechanism they seek to subvert. Adversaries may conduct [File and Directory Permissions Modification](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1222) or [Modify Registry](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1112) in support of subverting these controls.(Citation: SpectorOps Subverting Trust Sept 2017) Adversaries may also create or steal code signing certificates to acquire trust on target systems.(Citation: Securelist Digital Certificates)(Citation: Symantec Digital Certificates)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1553.002",
        "standard_attck_name": "Code Signing",
        "gid": 2625,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "axiom"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1553",
        "uncertain_name": "subvert trust controls",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may undermine security controls that will either warn users of untrusted activity or prevent execution of untrusted programs. Operating systems and security products may contain mechanisms to identify programs or websites as possessing some level of trust. Examples of such features would include a program being allowed to run because it is signed by a valid code signing certificate, a program prompting the user with a warning because it has an attribute set from being downloaded from the Internet, or getting an indication that you are about to connect to an untrusted site.\n\nAdversaries may attempt to subvert these trust mechanisms. The method adversaries use will depend on the specific mechanism they seek to subvert. Adversaries may conduct [File and Directory Permissions Modification](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1222) or [Modify Registry](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1112) in support of subverting these controls.(Citation: SpectorOps Subverting Trust Sept 2017) Adversaries may also create or steal code signing certificates to acquire trust on target systems.(Citation: Securelist Digital Certificates)(Citation: Symantec Digital Certificates) ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.711068053347695,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1553, Subvert Trust Controls, is a Defense Evasion technique targeting Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves undermining OS and security product mechanisms that warn about or block untrusted programs, such as code signing checks or Mark-of-the-Web warnings. Methods include file permission or registry modification to disable these controls, or the use of stolen or self-signed certificates to falsely establish trust. This technique is distinct from its sub-techniques like Code Signing (T1553.002) and is associated with groups like Axiom."
    },
    "18743": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion",
            "persistence",
            "privilege-escalation"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "b1d2ba6d-78e4-4d00-b708-7f0276126dd3",
        "aliases": [
            "Hijack Execution Flow"
        ],
        "name": "Hijack Execution Flow",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking the way operating systems run programs. Hijacking execution flow can be for the purposes of persistence, since this hijacked execution may reoccur over time. Adversaries may also use these mechanisms to elevate privileges or evade defenses, such as application control or other restrictions on execution.\n\nThere are many ways an adversary may hijack the flow of execution, including by manipulating how the operating system locates programs to be executed. How the operating system locates libraries to be used by a program can also be intercepted. Locations where the operating system looks for programs/resources, such as file directories and in the case of Windows the Registry, could also be poisoned to include malicious payloads.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1625",
        "standard_attck_name": "Hijack Execution Flow",
        "gid": 2626,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1574",
        "uncertain_name": "hijack execution flow",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may execute their own malicious payloads by hijacking the way operating systems run programs. Hijacking execution flow can be for the purposes of persistence, since this hijacked execution may reoccur over time. Adversaries may also use these mechanisms to elevate privileges or evade defenses, such as application control or other restrictions on execution.\n\nThere are many ways an adversary may hijack the flow of execution, including by manipulating how the operating system locates programs to be executed. How the operating system locates libraries to be used by a program can also be intercepted. Locations where the operating system looks for programs/resources, such as file directories and in the case of Windows the Registry, could also be poisoned to include malicious payloads.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 3.868251654958436,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Hijack Execution Flow is identified as MITRE ATT&CK technique T1574, falling under the Defense Evasion, Persistence, and Privilege Escalation tactics for Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. This technique involves adversaries executing malicious payloads by subverting standard OS mechanisms for locating and running programs or libraries. Specific methods include poisoning trusted file directories or the Windows Registry to insert malicious binaries, and more advanced sub-techniques like KernelCallbackTable hijacking, where attackers replace a legitimate callback function pointer in a process's PEB with the address of their payload to trigger execution under a legitimate process, often evading detection."
    },
    "18744": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "167b9689-d817-438e-9ba3-752c85744087",
        "aliases": [
            "Subvert Trust Controls"
        ],
        "name": "Subvert Trust Controls",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may undermine security controls that will either warn users of untrusted activity or prevent execution of untrusted programs. Operating systems and security products may contain mechanisms to identify programs or websites as possessing some level of trust. Examples of such features would include a program being allowed to run because it is signed by a valid code signing certificate, a program prompting the user with a warning because it has an attribute set from being downloaded from the Internet, or getting an indication that you are about to connect to an untrusted site.\n\nAdversaries may attempt to subvert these trust mechanisms. The method adversaries use will depend on the specific mechanism they seek to subvert. Adversaries may conduct [File and Directory Permissions Modification](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1222) or [Modify Registry](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1112) in support of subverting these controls.(Citation: SpectorOps Subverting Trust Sept 2017) Adversaries may also create or steal code signing certificates to acquire trust on target systems.(Citation: Securelist Digital Certificates)(Citation: Symantec Digital Certificates)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1632",
        "standard_attck_name": "Subvert Trust Controls",
        "gid": 2627,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "axiom"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1553",
        "uncertain_name": "subvert trust controls",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may undermine security controls that will either warn users of untrusted activity or prevent execution of untrusted programs. Operating systems and security products may contain mechanisms to identify programs or websites as possessing some level of trust. Examples of such features would include a program being allowed to run because it is signed by a valid code signing certificate, a program prompting the user with a warning because it has an attribute set from being downloaded from the Internet, or getting an indication that you are about to connect to an untrusted site.\n\nAdversaries may attempt to subvert these trust mechanisms. The method adversaries use will depend on the specific mechanism they seek to subvert. Adversaries may conduct [File and Directory Permissions Modification](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1222) or [Modify Registry](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1112) in support of subverting these controls.(Citation: SpectorOps Subverting Trust Sept 2017) Adversaries may also create or steal code signing certificates to acquire trust on target systems.(Citation: Securelist Digital Certificates)(Citation: Symantec Digital Certificates) ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.667601151440977,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Subvert Trust Controls is a Defense Evasion technique (ID T1553) targeting Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. It involves undermining security mechanisms like code signing certificates or file origin warnings to bypass execution controls. Adversaries achieve this by modifying file/directory permissions or registry keys, or by creating or stealing certificates to gain system trust. The technique is associated with the Axiom threat group and is linked to related sub-techniques like SIP and Trust Provider Hijacking.[/profile]"
    },
    "18745": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion",
            "persistence",
            "privilege-escalation"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
