        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1046",
        "uncertain_name": "network service discovery",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of services running on remote hosts and local network infrastructure devices, including those that may be vulnerable to remote software exploitation. Common methods to acquire this information include port, vulnerability, and/or wordlist scans using tools that are brought onto a system.(Citation: CISA AR21-126A FIVEHANDS May 2021)   \n\nWithin cloud environments, adversaries may attempt to discover services running on other cloud hosts. Additionally, if the cloud environment is connected to a on-premises environment, adversaries may be able to identify services running on non-cloud systems as well.\n\nWithin macOS environments, adversaries may use the native Bonjour application to discover services running on other macOS hosts within a network. The Bonjour mDNSResponder daemon automatically registers and advertises a host’s registered services on the network. For example, adversaries can use a mDNS query (such as <code>dns-sd -B _ssh._tcp .</code>) to find other systems broadcasting the ssh service.(Citation: apple doco bonjour description)(Citation: macOS APT Activity Bradley)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.521281166765856,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1046, Network Service Discovery, is a Discovery tactic technique applicable to macOS, Linux, Windows, Network Devices, Containers, and IaaS platforms. It involves adversaries scanning for services running on remote hosts and local network devices to identify potential targets for exploitation, using tools like port scanners. In cloud environments, this can extend to discovering services on other cloud hosts or connected on-premises systems. Specific implementations include using macOS's Bonjour mDNSResponder daemon with queries like `dns-sd -B _ssh._tcp .` to find SSH services. The technique is commonly used by groups like Volt Typhoon and APT41 as part of initial reconnaissance and is a precursor to techniques like Exploitation of Remote Services (T1210).[/profile]"
    },
    "19176": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "execution"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "System Services"
        ],
        "unique_id": "f034d558-51bc-4924-b02a-d0bc0430192e",
        "name": "T1569",
        "description": "Adversaries may abuse system services or daemons to execute commands or programs. Adversaries can execute malicious content by interacting with or creating services either locally or remotely. Many services are set to run at boot, which can aid in achieving persistence ([Create or Modify System Process](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543)), but adversaries can also abuse services for one-time or temporary execution.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1569",
        "standard_attck_name": "System Services",
        "gid": 3059,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1569",
        "uncertain_name": "system services",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may abuse system services or daemons to execute commands or programs. Adversaries can execute malicious content by interacting with or creating services either locally or remotely. Many services are set to run at boot, which can aid in achieving persistence ([Create or Modify System Process](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543)), but adversaries can also abuse services for one-time or temporary execution.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.27414882504449,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1569, known as System Services, is a MITRE ATT&CK execution technique for Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves adversaries abusing system services or daemons to execute commands or programs, either locally or remotely. This can be done by interacting with existing services or creating new ones, often using tools like launchctl on macOS. The technique is linked to related sub-techniques like Launchctl (T1569.001) and can facilitate persistence when services are set to run at boot.[/profile]"
    },
    "19177": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "impact"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Defacement"
        ],
        "unique_id": "791fce42-33cf-4155-8c34-fd4b780a6627",
        "name": "T1491",
        "description": "Adversaries may modify visual content available internally or externally to an enterprise network, thus affecting the integrity of the original content. Reasons for [Defacement](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1491) include delivering messaging, intimidation, or claiming (possibly false) credit for an intrusion. Disturbing or offensive images may be used as a part of [Defacement](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1491) in order to cause user discomfort, or to pressure compliance with accompanying messages.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "IaaS",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1491",
        "standard_attck_name": "Defacement",
        "gid": 3060,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1491",
        "uncertain_name": "defacement",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may modify visual content available internally or externally to an enterprise network, thus affecting the integrity of the original content. Reasons for [Defacement](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1491) include delivering messaging, intimidation, or claiming (possibly false) credit for an intrusion. Disturbing or offensive images may be used as a part of [Defacement](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1491) in order to cause user discomfort, or to pressure compliance with accompanying messages. \n",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 7.447305862541213,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1491 Defacement is an Impact tactic technique where adversaries modify visual content on internal or external systems, including websites, to compromise integrity for purposes like messaging, intimidation, or claiming credit. This action, which can involve using disturbing images to cause discomfort or pressure compliance, is applicable across Windows, Linux, macOS, IaaS, and ESXi platforms."
    },
    "19178": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "impact"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Service Stop"
        ],
        "unique_id": "ff00c09c-d058-44fc-bc42-a880c7c5f38f",
        "name": "T1489",
        "description": "Adversaries may stop or disable services on a system to render those services unavailable to legitimate users. Stopping critical services or processes can inhibit or stop response to an incident or aid in the adversary's overall objectives to cause damage to the environment.(Citation: Talos Olympic Destroyer 2018)(Citation: Novetta Blockbuster) \n\nAdversaries may accomplish this by disabling individual services of high importance to an organization, such as `MSExchangeIS`, which will make Exchange content inaccessible.(Citation: Novetta Blockbuster) In some cases, adversaries may stop or disable many or all services to render systems unusable.(Citation: Talos Olympic Destroyer 2018) Services or processes may not allow for modification of their data stores while running. Adversaries may stop services or processes in order to conduct [Data Destruction](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1485) or [Data Encrypted for Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1486) on the data stores of services like Exchange and SQL Server, or on virtual machines hosted on ESXi infrastructure.(Citation: SecureWorks WannaCry Analysis)(Citation: Crowdstrike Hypervisor Jackpotting Pt 2 2021)\n\nThreat actors may also disable or stop service in cloud environments. For example, by leveraging the `DisableAPIServiceAccess` API in AWS, a threat actor may prevent the service from creating service-linked roles on new accounts in the AWS Organization.(Citation: Datadog Security Labs Cloud Persistence 2025)(Citation: AWS DisableAWSServiceAccess)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi",
            "IaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1489",
        "standard_attck_name": "Service Stop",
        "gid": 3061,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "indrik spider",
            "medusa group",
            "sandworm team",
            "lapsus$",
            "lazarus group",
            "wizard spider"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1489",
        "uncertain_name": "service stop",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may stop or disable services on a system to render those services unavailable to legitimate users. Stopping critical services or processes can inhibit or stop response to an incident or aid in the adversary's overall objectives to cause damage to the environment.(Citation: Talos Olympic Destroyer 2018)(Citation: Novetta Blockbuster) \n\nAdversaries may accomplish this by disabling individual services of high importance to an organization, such as <code>MSExchangeIS</code>, which will make Exchange content inaccessible.(Citation: Novetta Blockbuster) In some cases, adversaries may stop or disable many or all services to render systems unusable.(Citation: Talos Olympic Destroyer 2018) Services or processes may not allow for modification of their data stores while running. Adversaries may stop services or processes in order to conduct [Data Destruction](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1485) or [Data Encrypted for Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1486) on the data stores of services like Exchange and SQL Server, or on virtual machines hosted on ESXi infrastructure.(Citation: SecureWorks WannaCry Analysis)(Citation: Crowdstrike Hypervisor Jackpotting Pt 2 2021)\n\nThreat actors may also disable or stop service in cloud environments. For example, by leveraging the `DisableAPIServiceAccess` API in AWS, a threat actor may prevent the service from creating service-linked roles on new accounts in the AWS Organization.(Citation: Datadog Security Labs Cloud Persistence 2025)(Citation: AWS DisableAWSServiceAccess)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.73384956518871,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1489, known as Service Stop, is an Impact tactic technique applicable across Windows, Linux, macOS, and ESXi platforms. Adversaries stop or disable services to deny availability, often targeting critical services like MSExchangeIS or using hypervisor commands on ESXi to facilitate follow-on attacks such as data destruction or encryption. It is associated with groups like Sandworm Team and Lazarus Group and can be linked to vulnerabilities like CVE-2023-20269."
    },
    "19179": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "initial-access",
            "persistence"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "External Remote Services"
        ],
        "unique_id": "3b1145d0-a25c-4e88-a9c6-f4dec9b584c7",
        "name": "T1133",
        "description": "Adversaries may leverage external-facing remote services to initially access and/or persist within a network. Remote services such as VPNs, Citrix, and other access mechanisms allow users to connect to internal enterprise network resources from external locations. There are often remote service gateways that manage connections and credential authentication for these services. Services such as [Windows Remote Management](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/006) and [VNC](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/005) can also be used externally.(Citation: MacOS VNC software for Remote Desktop)\n\nAccess to [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) to use the service is often a requirement, which could be obtained through credential pharming or by obtaining the credentials from users after compromising the enterprise network.(Citation: Volexity Virtual Private Keylogging) Access to remote services may be used as a redundant or persistent access mechanism during an operation.\n\nAccess may also be gained through an exposed service that doesn’t require authentication. In containerized environments, this may include an exposed Docker API, Kubernetes API server, kubelet, or web application such as the Kubernetes dashboard.(Citation: Trend Micro Exposed Docker Server)(Citation: Unit 42 Hildegard Malware)\n\nAdversaries may also establish persistence on network by configuring a Tor hidden service on a compromised system. Adversaries may utilize the tool `ShadowLink` to facilitate the installation and configuration of the Tor hidden service. Tor hidden service is then accessible via the Tor network because `ShadowLink` sets up a .onion address on the compromised system. `ShadowLink` may be used to forward any inbound connections to RDP, allowing the adversaries to have remote access.(Citation: The BadPilot campaign) Adversaries may get `ShadowLink` to persist on a system by masquerading it as an MS Defender application.(Citation: Russian threat actors dig in, prepare to seize on war fatigue)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Containers"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1133",
        "standard_attck_name": "External Remote Services",
        "gid": 3062,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
