            "69a90665113bd73b30360d87f7f6ed2c789a90a67f3b6e86474e21273a64f699",
            "22f98117e5e182bf3de5b86dbf29953163df88eff0a182729931b9976bf884c0",
            "9b0d0284c48659a7d81399fc9e9174ba373363116e66a21ecf84246fa2591b8b",
            "b1d2f8d627af261a1556ba289a6d171e9eda3b9d9d28e330262dd9559e5a1da1",
            "75fed14fd61067a1c0c2a10d0eefcc349308e1f4a1993a075a9f0c768affab13",
            "613fb5ffbce15d7c71a019dd0f80256b2d05772e4f62c2fbf7c74164f1227755",
            "35c7eb685fa4b03fd1e852c936768f003f8284ca96b1e1c73082053cd41fe63a"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 0.0,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]This intrusion set, likely a financially motivated cybercriminal group, is known to operate in Latin America, particularly targeting Colombia, Panama, Chile, and Ecuador. Its key malware includes HijackLoader, PureCrypter, AsyncRAT, and the REMCOS RAT. Its technical behaviors involve defense evasion through Mark-of-the-Web bypass (T1553.005) using container files, credential access via GUI Input Capture (T1056.002), and the use of acquired infrastructure such as Virtual Private Servers (VPS) and botnets (T1583.005, T1583.003). The group's activity shows overlaps with tools and infrastructure used by other actors, but a definitive APT designation or origin attribution cannot be confirmed from the provided data. Its primary motivation appears to be financial gain through information theft and remote access.[profile]"
    },
