    "19228": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "resource-development"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Botnet"
        ],
        "unique_id": "a3650c8d-a331-4bbe-a279-b0fabeb8d9f1",
        "name": "T1583.005",
        "description": "Adversaries may buy, lease, or rent a network of compromised systems that can be used during targeting. A botnet is a network of compromised systems that can be instructed to perform coordinated tasks.(Citation: Norton Botnet) Adversaries may purchase a subscription to use an existing botnet from a booter/stresser service. \n\nInternet-facing edge devices and related network appliances that are end-of-life (EOL) and unsupported by their manufacturers are commonly acquired for botnet activities. Adversaries may lease operational relay box (ORB) networks – consisting of virtual private servers (VPS), small office/home office (SOHO) routers, or Internet of Things (IoT) devices – to serve as a botnet.(Citation: ORB Mandiant) \n\nWith a botnet at their disposal, adversaries may perform follow-on activity such as large-scale [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566) or Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS).(Citation: Imperva DDoS for Hire)(Citation: Krebs-Anna)(Citation: Krebs-Bazaar)(Citation: Krebs-Booter) Acquired botnets may also be used to support Command and Control activity, such as [Hide Infrastructure](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1665) through an established [Proxy](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1090) network.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "PRE"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "hafnium",
            "ke3chang",
            "apt5"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1583.005",
        "uncertain_name": "botnet",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may buy, lease, or rent a network of compromised systems that can be used during targeting. A botnet is a network of compromised systems that can be instructed to perform coordinated tasks.(Citation: Norton Botnet) Adversaries may purchase a subscription to use an existing botnet from a booter/stresser service. \n\nInternet-facing edge devices and related network appliances that are end-of-life (EOL) and unsupported by their manufacturers are commonly acquired for botnet activities. Adversaries may lease operational relay box (ORB) networks – consisting of virtual private servers (VPS), small office/home office (SOHO) routers, or Internet of Things (IoT) devices – to serve as a botnet.(Citation: ORB Mandiant) \n\nWith a botnet at their disposal, adversaries may perform follow-on activity such as large-scale [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566) or Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS).(Citation: Imperva DDoS for Hire)(Citation: Krebs-Anna)(Citation: Krebs-Bazaar)(Citation: Krebs-Booter) Acquired botnets may also be used to support Command and Control activity, such as [Hide Infrastructure](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1665) through an established [Proxy](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1090) network.\n\n",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 14.334464185044835,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1583.005, known as Acquire Infrastructure: Botnet, is a Resource Development technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework on the PRE platform. It involves adversaries buying, leasing, or renting a network of compromised systems, such as end-of-life devices or ORB networks, to perform coordinated tasks. This acquired botnet can then be used for follow-on activities including large-scale phishing, distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks, or to establish proxy networks for command and control."
    },
