        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "未知"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "asyncrat",
        "uncertain_mes": "The State of SSL/TLS Certificate Usage in\nMalware C&C Communications\nMohamad Mokbel  \nIntroduction\nThe nature of malware communications with its C&C server(s) has advanced over time, from using plain\nnon-encrypted channels to using custom and standard symmetric (such as Blowfish, TEA, RC4, AES,\nDES, 3DES, XOR, ChaCha20, Salsa20) and asymmetric (such as RSA, DSA, ECC, DH) encryption",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.551017952138887,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]AsyncRAT is a malware family with aliases including Backdoor.MSIL.ASYNCRAT.AZ. It is attributed to unknown groups. Its key technical behaviors include abusing msiexec.exe (T1218.007) for defense evasion and privilege escalation, bypassing Mark-of-the-Web (T1553.005) via container files, capturing GUI input (T1056.002) for credential access, and potentially launching network denial-of-service attacks (T1498). It primarily targets Windows platforms and has utilized command-and-control infrastructure such as warpower.dynuddns.net, ferujoludo.online, and oierhjuhbi3i3.top, with activity linked to locations including Panama, Colombia, Chile, Lebanon, and Jordan.[/profile]"
    },
