            "6587de22729bf3dd6f3632d67881fbc75275b9fd6d88597c7f04462ec1b2bcdf",
            "7a413732944fe4101f589e9ae49cd1b48c42c1287606b6badf4ce582cd8dedb5",
            "e6e69e85962a402a35cbc5b75571dab3739c0b2f3861ba5853dbd140bae4e4da",
            "9b0d0284c48659a7d81399fc9e9174ba373363116e66a21ecf84246fa2591b8b",
            "b1d2f8d627af261a1556ba289a6d171e9eda3b9d9d28e330262dd9559e5a1da1",
            "75fed14fd61067a1c0c2a10d0eefcc349308e1f4a1993a075a9f0c768affab13"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "未知"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "purecrypter",
        "uncertain_mes": "Key points\n- PureCrypter is a fully-featured loader being sold since at least March 2021\n- The malware has been observed distributing a variety of remote access trojans and information stealers\n- The loader is a .NET executable obfuscated with SmartAssembly and makes use of compression, encryption and obfuscation to evade antivirus software products",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.808972635482924,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]PureCrypter, also known as purecrypter, is a fully-featured malware loader sold since at least March 2021. Its key technical behaviors include using a .NET executable obfuscated with SmartAssembly, employing compression and encryption to evade antivirus detection, and performing process hollowing (T1055.012) to inject malicious code. It utilizes data obfuscation (T1001) and traffic signaling (T1205) for command and control, and stages capabilities (T1608) on infrastructure like Virtual Private Servers (VPS). It has been observed distributing remote access trojans and information stealers, and its associated infrastructure includes domains like newstaticfreepoint24.ddns-ip.net and donato.con-ip.com.[/profile]"
    },
    "19251": {
        "semantic": 1,
