        "uncertain_id": "t1583.001",
        "uncertain_name": "domains",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may acquire domains that can be used during targeting. Domain names are the human readable names used to represent one or more IP addresses. They can be purchased or, in some cases, acquired for free.\n\nAdversaries may use acquired domains for a variety of purposes, including for [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566), [Drive-by Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1189), and Command and Control.(Citation: CISA MSS Sep 2020) Adversaries may choose domains that are similar to legitimate domains, including through use of homoglyphs or use of a different top-level domain (TLD).(Citation: FireEye APT28)(Citation: PaypalScam) Typosquatting may be used to aid in delivery of payloads via [Drive-by Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1189). Adversaries may also use internationalized domain names (IDNs) and different character sets (e.g. Cyrillic, Greek, etc.) to execute \"IDN homograph attacks,\" creating visually similar lookalike domains used to deliver malware to victim machines.(Citation: CISA IDN ST05-016)(Citation: tt_httrack_fake_domains)(Citation: tt_obliqueRAT)(Citation: httrack_unhcr)(Citation: lazgroup_idn_phishing)\n\nDifferent URIs/URLs may also be dynamically generated to uniquely serve malicious content to victims (including one-time, single use domain names).(Citation: iOS URL Scheme)(Citation: URI)(Citation: URI Use)(Citation: URI Unique)\n\nAdversaries may also acquire and repurpose expired domains, which may be potentially already allowlisted/trusted by defenders based on an existing reputation/history.(Citation: Categorisation_not_boundary)(Citation: Domain_Steal_CC)(Citation: Redirectors_Domain_Fronting)(Citation: bypass_webproxy_filtering)\n\nDomain registrars each maintain a publicly viewable database that displays contact information for every registered domain. Private WHOIS services display alternative information, such as their own company data, rather than the owner of the domain. Adversaries may use such private WHOIS services to obscure information about who owns a purchased domain. Adversaries may further interrupt efforts to track their infrastructure by using varied registration information and purchasing domains with different domain registrars.(Citation: Mandiant APT1)\n\nIn addition to legitimately purchasing a domain, an adversary may register a new domain in a compromised environment. For example, in AWS environments, adversaries may leverage the Route53 domain service to register a domain and create hosted zones pointing to resources of the threat actor’s choosing.(Citation: Invictus IR DangerDev 2024)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 8.961199819658427,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Acquire Infrastructure: Domains, identified as T1583.001, is a Resource Development tactic on the PRE platform. Adversaries purchase or acquire domains to establish operational infrastructure for subsequent attacks like phishing, drive-by compromise, and command and control. They employ techniques such as registering lookalike domains using typosquatting, homoglyphs, different TLDs, or internationalized domain names (IDNs) for homograph attacks. They may also repurpose expired domains for their trusted reputation, use private WHOIS services to obscure ownership, and register domains through compromised cloud services like AWS Route53. This technique is widely used by numerous threat groups including APT28, Lazarus Group, and FIN7.[/profile]"
    },
    "19313": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "credential-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "02ca11a6-35ed-45d4-99e1-119b7befd7a6",
        "aliases": [
            "Steal Web Session Cookie"
        ],
        "name": "Steal Web Session Cookie",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "An adversary may steal web application or service session cookies and use them to gain access to web applications or Internet services as an authenticated user without needing credentials. Web applications and services often use session cookies as an authentication token after a user has authenticated to a website.\n\nCookies are often valid for an extended period of time, even if the web application is not actively used. Cookies can be found on disk, in the process memory of the browser, and in network traffic to remote systems. Additionally, other applications on the targets machine might store sensitive authentication cookies in memory (e.g. apps which authenticate to cloud services). Session cookies can be used to bypasses some multi-factor authentication protocols.(Citation: Pass The Cookie)\n\nThere are several examples of malware targeting cookies from web browsers on the local system.(Citation: Kaspersky TajMahal April 2019)(Citation: Unit 42 Mac Crypto Cookies January 2019) Adversaries may also steal cookies by injecting malicious JavaScript content into websites or relying on [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204) by tricking victims into running malicious JavaScript in their browser.(Citation: Talos Roblox Scam 2023)(Citation: Krebs Discord Bookmarks 2023)\n\nThere are also open source frameworks such as `Evilginx2` and `Muraena` that can gather session cookies through a malicious proxy (e.g., [Adversary-in-the-Middle](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557)) that can be set up by an adversary and used in phishing campaigns.(Citation: Github evilginx2)(Citation: GitHub Mauraena)\n\nAfter an adversary acquires a valid cookie, they can then perform a [Web Session Cookie](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1550/004) technique to login to the corresponding web application.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Office Suite",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1539",
        "standard_attck_name": "Steal Web Session Cookie",
        "gid": 3196,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "kimsuky",
            "star blizzard",
            "evilnum",
            "scattered spider",
            "sandworm team",
            "luminousmoth",
            "lotus blossom",
            "apt42"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1539",
        "uncertain_name": "steal web session cookie",
        "uncertain_mes": "An adversary may steal web application or service session cookies and use them to gain access to web applications or Internet services as an authenticated user without needing credentials. Web applications and services often use session cookies as an authentication token after a user has authenticated to a website.\n\nCookies are often valid for an extended period of time, even if the web application is not actively used. Cookies can be found on disk, in the process memory of the browser, and in network traffic to remote systems. Additionally, other applications on the targets machine might store sensitive authentication cookies in memory (e.g. apps which authenticate to cloud services). Session cookies can be used to bypasses some multi-factor authentication protocols.(Citation: Pass The Cookie)\n\nThere are several examples of malware targeting cookies from web browsers on the local system.(Citation: Kaspersky TajMahal April 2019)(Citation: Unit 42 Mac Crypto Cookies January 2019) Adversaries may also steal cookies by injecting malicious JavaScript content into websites or relying on [User Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1204) by tricking victims into running malicious JavaScript in their browser.(Citation: Talos Roblox Scam 2023)(Citation: Krebs Discord Bookmarks 2023)\n\nThere are also open source frameworks such as `Evilginx2` and `Muraena` that can gather session cookies through a malicious proxy (e.g., [Adversary-in-the-Middle](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557)) that can be set up by an adversary and used in phishing campaigns.(Citation: Github evilginx2)(Citation: GitHub Mauraena)\n\nAfter an adversary acquires a valid cookie, they can then perform a [Web Session Cookie](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1550/004) technique to login to the corresponding web application.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.631414402917924,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Steal Web Session Cookie is MITRE ATT&CK technique T1539 under the Credential Access tactic, applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, Office Suite, and SaaS platforms. It involves stealing web session cookies from disk, browser process memory, or network traffic to impersonate authenticated users, bypassing multi-factor authentication. Specific methods include using malware to extract browser cookies, injecting malicious JavaScript, or employing adversary-in-the-middle proxy frameworks like Evilginx2. The stolen cookie is then used in a follow-on Web Session Cookie (T1550.004) technique for unauthorized access to web applications and services.[/profile]"
    },
    "19314": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "command-and-control"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Steganography"
        ],
        "unique_id": "78cd5b7a-caf0-47d8-9355-b47aa1776020",
        "name": "Steganography",
        "description": "Adversaries may use steganographic techniques to hide command and control traffic to make detection efforts more difficult. Steganographic techniques can be used to hide data in digital messages that are transferred between systems. This hidden information can be used for command and control of compromised systems. In some cases, the passing of files embedded using steganography, such as image or document files, can be used for command and control.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1406.001",
        "standard_attck_name": "Steganography",
        "gid": 3197,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "earth lusca",
            "apt37",
            "muddywater",
            "tropic trooper",
            "leviathan",
            "bronze butler",
            "ta551",
            "andariel"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1027.003",
        "uncertain_name": "steganography",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use steganography techniques in order to prevent the detection of hidden information. Steganographic techniques can be used to hide data in digital media such as images, audio tracks, video clips, or text files.\n\n[Duqu](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0038) was an early example of malware that used steganography. It encrypted the gathered information from a victim's system and hid it within an image before exfiltrating the image to a C2 server.(Citation: Wikipedia Duqu) \n\nBy the end of 2017, a threat group used <code>Invoke-PSImage</code> to hide [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001) commands in an image file (.png) and execute the code on a victim's system. In this particular case the [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001) code downloaded another obfuscated script to gather intelligence from the victim's machine and communicate it back to the adversary.(Citation: McAfee Malicious Doc Targets Pyeongchang Olympics)  ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 11.863230746373102,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Steganography is a technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework, identified as T1001.002, under the Command and Control tactic. It operates across Windows, Linux, macOS, and ESXi platforms. The technique involves hiding data or command and control traffic within digital media like images, audio, video, or text files to evade detection. For instance, malware such as Duqu has hidden exfiltrated data in images, and tools like Invoke-PSImage have been used to embed and execute PowerShell code from image files. It is a sub-technique of Data Obfuscation (T1001)."
    },
    "19315": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "collection",
            "credential-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "173bf53e-cca4-4c6a-b201-70554150a8fa",
        "aliases": [
            "GUI Input Capture"
        ],
        "name": "GUI Input Capture",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may mimic common operating system GUI components to prompt users for credentials with a seemingly legitimate prompt. When programs are executed that need additional privileges than are present in the current user context, it is common for the operating system to prompt the user for proper credentials to authorize the elevated privileges for the task (ex: [Bypass User Account Control](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/002)).\n\nAdversaries may mimic this functionality to prompt users for credentials with a seemingly legitimate prompt for a number of reasons that mimic normal usage, such as a fake installer requiring additional access or a fake malware removal suite.(Citation: OSX Malware Exploits MacKeeper) This type of prompt can be used to collect credentials via various languages such as [AppleScript](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/002)(Citation: LogRhythm Do You Trust Oct 2014)(Citation: OSX Keydnap malware)(Citation: Spoofing credential dialogs) and [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001).(Citation: LogRhythm Do You Trust Oct 2014)(Citation: Enigma Phishing for Credentials Jan 2015)(Citation: Spoofing credential dialogs) On Linux systems adversaries may launch dialog boxes prompting users for credentials from malicious shell scripts or the command line (i.e. [Unix Shell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/004)).(Citation: Spoofing credential dialogs)\n\nAdversaries may also mimic common software authentication requests, such as those from browsers or email clients. This may also be paired with user activity monitoring (i.e., [Browser Information Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1217) and/or [Application Window Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1010)) to spoof prompts when users are naturally accessing sensitive sites/data.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1417.002",
        "standard_attck_name": "GUI Input Capture",
        "gid": 3198,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "redcurl",
            "fin4"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1056.002",
        "uncertain_name": "gui input capture",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may mimic common operating system GUI components to prompt users for credentials with a seemingly legitimate prompt. When programs are executed that need additional privileges than are present in the current user context, it is common for the operating system to prompt the user for proper credentials to authorize the elevated privileges for the task (ex: [Bypass User Account Control](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/002)).\n\nAdversaries may mimic this functionality to prompt users for credentials with a seemingly legitimate prompt for a number of reasons that mimic normal usage, such as a fake installer requiring additional access or a fake malware removal suite.(Citation: OSX Malware Exploits MacKeeper) This type of prompt can be used to collect credentials via various languages such as [AppleScript](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/002)(Citation: LogRhythm Do You Trust Oct 2014)(Citation: OSX Keydnap malware)(Citation: Spoofing credential dialogs) and [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001).(Citation: LogRhythm Do You Trust Oct 2014)(Citation: Enigma Phishing for Credentials Jan 2015)(Citation: Spoofing credential dialogs) On Linux systems adversaries may launch dialog boxes prompting users for credentials from malicious shell scripts or the command line (i.e. [Unix Shell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/004)).(Citation: Spoofing credential dialogs)\n\nAdversaries may also mimic common software authentication requests, such as those from browsers or email clients. This may also be paired with user activity monitoring (i.e., [Browser Information Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1217) and/or [Application Window Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1010)) to spoof prompts when users are naturally accessing sensitive sites/data.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 14.049875760123529,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]GUI Input Capture is a technique under MITRE ATT&CK ID T1056.002, falling within the Credential Access and Collection tactics and applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. It involves adversaries mimicking legitimate operating system or application GUI prompts, such as privilege elevation requests or software authentication dialogs, to deceive users into entering their credentials. This deception is often implemented using scripting languages like AppleScript, PowerShell, or Unix shell scripts to generate the fake input boxes. The technique has been associated with threat groups like Redcurl and FIN4."
    },
    "19316": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "resource-development"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Email Accounts"
        ],
        "unique_id": "464ed015-5729-4f4d-9543-2a3170b58c4f",
        "name": "T1586.002",
        "description": "Adversaries may compromise email accounts that can be used during targeting. Adversaries can use compromised email accounts to further their operations, such as leveraging them to conduct [Phishing for Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598), [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566), or large-scale spam email campaigns. Utilizing an existing persona with a compromised email account may engender a level of trust in a potential victim if they have a relationship with, or knowledge of, the compromised persona. Compromised email accounts can also be used in the acquisition of infrastructure (ex: [Domains](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/001)).\n\nA variety of methods exist for compromising email accounts, such as gathering credentials via [Phishing for Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598), purchasing credentials from third-party sites, brute forcing credentials (ex: password reuse from breach credential dumps), or paying employees, suppliers or business partners for access to credentials.(Citation: AnonHBGary)(Citation: Microsoft DEV-0537) Prior to compromising email accounts, adversaries may conduct Reconnaissance to inform decisions about which accounts to compromise to further their operation. Adversaries may target compromising well-known email accounts or domains from which malicious spam or [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566) emails may evade reputation-based email filtering rules.\n\nAdversaries can use a compromised email account to hijack existing email threads with targets of interest.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "PRE"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "ta577",
            "kimsuky",
            "star blizzard",
            "magic hound",
            "mustang panda",
            "leviathan",
            "lapsus$",
            "hexane",
            "apt28",
            "oilrig",
            "apt29",
            "indigozebra"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1586.002",
        "uncertain_name": "email accounts",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may compromise email accounts that can be used during targeting. Adversaries can use compromised email accounts to further their operations, such as leveraging them to conduct [Phishing for Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598), [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566), or large-scale spam email campaigns. Utilizing an existing persona with a compromised email account may engender a level of trust in a potential victim if they have a relationship with, or knowledge of, the compromised persona. Compromised email accounts can also be used in the acquisition of infrastructure (ex: [Domains](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/001)).\n\nA variety of methods exist for compromising email accounts, such as gathering credentials via [Phishing for Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598), purchasing credentials from third-party sites, brute forcing credentials (ex: password reuse from breach credential dumps), or paying employees, suppliers or business partners for access to credentials.(Citation: AnonHBGary)(Citation: Microsoft DEV-0537) Prior to compromising email accounts, adversaries may conduct Reconnaissance to inform decisions about which accounts to compromise to further their operation. Adversaries may target compromising well-known email accounts or domains from which malicious spam or [Phishing](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566) emails may evade reputation-based email filtering rules.\n\nAdversaries can use a compromised email account to hijack existing email threads with targets of interest.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.952330030671298,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1586.002, also known as Compromise Email Accounts, is a resource development technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework applicable to the PRE platform. Adversaries compromise email accounts through methods like credential phishing, purchasing credentials, or brute-forcing reused passwords from breaches. These compromised accounts are then leveraged to conduct further operations, including spear-phishing campaigns, hijacking existing email threads to build trust, and acquiring infrastructure such as domains."
    },
    "19317": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
