        "uncertain_id": "t1071.004",
        "uncertain_name": "dns",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may communicate using the Domain Name System (DNS) application layer protocol to avoid detection/network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to the remote system, and often the results of those commands, will be embedded within the protocol traffic between the client and server. \n\nThe DNS protocol serves an administrative function in computer networking and thus may be very common in environments. DNS traffic may also be allowed even before network authentication is completed. DNS packets contain many fields and headers in which data can be concealed. Often known as DNS tunneling, adversaries may abuse DNS to communicate with systems under their control within a victim network while also mimicking normal, expected traffic.(Citation: PAN DNS Tunneling)(Citation: Medium DnsTunneling)\n\nDNS beaconing may be used to send commands to remote systems via DNS queries. A DNS beacon is created by tunneling DNS traffic (i.e. [Protocol Tunneling](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1572)). The commands may be embedded into different DNS records, for example, TXT or A records.(Citation: OilRig Uses Updated BONDUPDATER to Target Middle Eastern Government) DNS beacons may be difficult to detect because the beacons infrequently communicate with infected devices.(Citation: DNS Beacons) Infrequent communication conceals the malicious DNS traffic with normal DNS traffic. ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 11.946947941342966,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity \"DNS\" is a command and control technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework, specifically identified as sub-technique T1071.004 under the Application Layer Protocol technique (T1071). It operates on platforms including Windows, Linux, and macOS. This technique involves adversaries using the Domain Name System (DNS) protocol to covertly communicate with compromised systems, often embedding commands and data within DNS query and response fields like TXT or A records to blend with normal network traffic and bypass security controls. This method, known as DNS tunneling or beaconing, provides a stealthy channel for remote command execution."
    },
    "19414": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "command-and-control"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Asymmetric Cryptography"
        ],
        "unique_id": "5637b068-2226-4248-abb1-c779db4dec85",
        "name": "T1573.002",
        "description": "Adversaries may employ a known asymmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Asymmetric cryptography, also known as public key cryptography, uses a keypair per party: one public that can be freely distributed, and one private. Due to how the keys are generated, the sender encrypts data with the receiver’s public key and the receiver decrypts the data with their private key. This ensures that only the intended recipient can read the encrypted data. Common public key encryption algorithms include RSA and ElGamal.\n\nFor efficiency, many protocols (including SSL/TLS) use symmetric cryptography once a connection is established, but use asymmetric cryptography to establish or transmit a key. As such, these protocols are classified as [Asymmetric Cryptography](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1573/002).",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "fin6",
            "redecho",
            "tropic trooper",
            "medusa group",
            "velvet ant",
            "redcurl",
            "cobalt group",
            "apt42",
            "fin8",
            "oilrig",
            "ta2541"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1573.002",
        "uncertain_name": "asymmetric cryptography",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may employ a known asymmetric encryption algorithm to conceal command and control traffic rather than relying on any inherent protections provided by a communication protocol. Asymmetric cryptography, also known as public key cryptography, uses a keypair per party: one public that can be freely distributed, and one private. Due to how the keys are generated, the sender encrypts data with the receiver’s public key and the receiver decrypts the data with their private key. This ensures that only the intended recipient can read the encrypted data. Common public key encryption algorithms include RSA and ElGamal.\n\nFor efficiency, many protocols (including SSL/TLS) use symmetric cryptography once a connection is established, but use asymmetric cryptography to establish or transmit a key. As such, these protocols are classified as [Asymmetric Cryptography](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1573/002).",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.13200086479719,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1573.002, Asymmetric Cryptography, is a command-and-control technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, ESXi, and Network Devices. It involves adversaries using public key encryption algorithms like RSA or ElGamal to conceal C2 traffic, where data is encrypted with a recipient's public key and decrypted with their private key to ensure only the intended target can read it. This method is often used to establish secure channels for subsequent symmetric encryption. Associated threat groups include FIN6, OilRig, and APT42.[/profile]"
    },
    "19415": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "6e16a61e-3fcd-4a82-88aa-305baf2e37ac",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "AnvilEcho",
        "is_family": false,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [
            "dc5c963f1428db051ff7aa4d43967a4087f9540a9d331dea616ca5013c6d67ce",
            "dcb072061defd12f12deb659c66f40473a76d51c911040b8109ba32bb36504e3",
            "8a47fd166059e7e3c0c1740ea8997205f9e12fc87b1ffe064d0ed4b0bf7c2ce1",
            "5dca88f08b586a51677ff6d900234a1568f4474bbbfef258d59d73ca4532dcaf",
            "5aee738121093866404827e1db43c8e1a7882291afedfe90314ec90b198afb36",
            "d033db88065bd4f548ed13287021ac899d8c3215ebc46fdd33f46a671bba731c",
            "574fc53ba2e9684938d87fc486392568f8db0b92fb15028e441ffe26c920b4c5",
            "258d9d67e14506b70359daabebd41978c7699d6ce75533955736cdd2b8192c1a"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.66637530844932,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]AnvilEcho is a malware that uses the T1095 (Non-Application Layer Protocol) technique for command-and-control communication, potentially leveraging protocols like ICMP or UDP to evade network monitoring. It employs T1021 (Remote Services) for lateral movement, using valid accounts to access remote services such as SSH or RDP. For persistence and execution, it utilizes T1204 (User Execution) by tricking users into opening malicious files, and T1555 (Credentials from Password Stores) to harvest credentials from password managers or browsers. The malware also implements Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion (T1497) to detect analysis environments and alter its behavior. Its known infrastructure includes the domains deepspaceocean.info and understandingthewar.org.[/profile]"
    },
