        ],
        "unique_id": "0fa0a6da-ac16-4165-ad40-c4f2fcd35771",
        "name": "T1011.001",
        "description": "Adversaries may attempt to exfiltrate data over Bluetooth rather than the command and control channel. If the command and control network is a wired Internet connection, an adversary may opt to exfiltrate data using a Bluetooth communication channel.\n\nAdversaries may choose to do this if they have sufficient access and proximity. Bluetooth connections might not be secured or defended as well as the primary Internet-connected channel because it is not routed through the same enterprise network.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1011.001",
        "standard_attck_name": "Exfiltration Over Bluetooth",
        "gid": 3464,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1011.001",
        "uncertain_name": "exfiltration over bluetooth",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may attempt to exfiltrate data over Bluetooth rather than the command and control channel. If the command and control network is a wired Internet connection, an adversary may opt to exfiltrate data using a Bluetooth communication channel.\n\nAdversaries may choose to do this if they have sufficient access and proximity. Bluetooth connections might not be secured or defended as well as the primary Internet-connected channel because it is not routed through the same enterprise network.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 7.44779377854116,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1011.001, known as Exfiltration Over Bluetooth, is a technique within the Exfiltration tactic. It targets macOS, Linux, and Windows platforms. This method involves adversaries exfiltrating data via a Bluetooth communication channel instead of the primary command and control network, often because Bluetooth connections may be less monitored or secured than the main enterprise network. This approach requires the adversary to have sufficient access and physical proximity to the target device.[profile]"
    },
    "19582": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "persistence",
            "privilege-escalation"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts"
        ],
        "unique_id": "81eeb269-16ba-4614-93b8-0038428ec9c0",
        "name": "T1037",
        "description": "Adversaries may use scripts automatically executed at boot or logon initialization to establish persistence.(Citation: Mandiant APT29 Eye Spy Email Nov 22)(Citation: Anomali Rocke March 2019) Initialization scripts can be used to perform administrative functions, which may often execute other programs or send information to an internal logging server. These scripts can vary based on operating system and whether applied locally or remotely.  \n\nAdversaries may use these scripts to maintain persistence on a single system. Depending on the access configuration of the logon scripts, either local credentials or an administrator account may be necessary. \n\nAn adversary may also be able to escalate their privileges since some boot or logon initialization scripts run with higher privileges.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1037.003",
        "standard_attck_name": "Network Logon Script",
        "gid": 3465,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "unc3886",
            "apt41",
            "rocke",
            "apt29"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1037",
        "uncertain_name": "boot or logon initialization scripts",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use scripts automatically executed at boot or logon initialization to establish persistence.(Citation: Mandiant APT29 Eye Spy Email Nov 22)(Citation: Anomali Rocke March 2019) Initialization scripts can be used to perform administrative functions, which may often execute other programs or send information to an internal logging server. These scripts can vary based on operating system and whether applied locally or remotely.  \n\nAdversaries may use these scripts to maintain persistence on a single system. Depending on the access configuration of the logon scripts, either local credentials or an administrator account may be necessary. \n\nAn adversary may also be able to escalate their privileges since some boot or logon initialization scripts run with higher privileges.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 14.048130399741916,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The attack pattern is MITRE ATT&CK technique T1037, Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts, used for Persistence and Privilege Escalation on platforms including Windows, Linux, and macOS. It works by adversaries placing scripts that are automatically executed during system boot or user logon to establish persistence, with the scripts often running with higher privileges to enable privilege escalation. This technique is used by groups like APT29 and APT41, and its implementation can require modifying file permissions to access protected script directories."
    },
