    "19693": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "collection"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Stored Application Data"
        ],
        "unique_id": "0c04baad-c052-4544-bb5f-12eb5eb588c4",
        "name": "T1409",
        "description": "Adversaries may try to access and collect application data resident on the device. Adversaries often target popular applications, such as Facebook, WeChat, and Gmail.(Citation: SWB Exodus March 2019) \n\n \n\nDue to mobile OS sandboxing, this technique is only possible in three scenarios: \n\n \n\n* An application stores files in unprotected external storage \n* An application stores files in its internal storage directory with insecure permissions (e.g. 777) \n* The adversary gains root permissions on the device",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-mobile-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "android",
            "iOS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1409",
        "standard_attck_name": "Stored Application Data",
        "gid": 3577,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 7.447305862541213,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1409, also known as Stored Application Data, is a collection technique within the MITRE Mobile-ATT&CK framework targeting Android and iOS platforms. It involves adversaries accessing and exfiltrating sensitive data stored by applications on the device, such as cached files, databases, or configuration data."
    },
    "19694": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "collection"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "6868b1d1-9be6-4dfd-a3a0-17605ddbb505",
        "aliases": [
            "Stored Application Data"
        ],
        "name": "Stored Application Data",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-mobile-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may try to access and collect application data resident on the device. Adversaries often target popular applications, such as Facebook, WeChat, and Gmail.(Citation: SWB Exodus March 2019) \n\n \n\nDue to mobile OS sandboxing, this technique is only possible in three scenarios: \n\n \n\n* An application stores files in unprotected external storage \n* An application stores files in its internal storage directory with insecure permissions (e.g. 777) \n* The adversary gains root permissions on the device",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "android",
            "iOS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1409",
        "standard_attck_name": "Stored Application Data",
        "gid": 3578,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "apt38"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1565.001",
        "uncertain_name": "stored data manipulation",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may insert, delete, or manipulate data at rest in order to influence external outcomes or hide activity, thus threatening the integrity of the data.(Citation: FireEye APT38 Oct 2018)(Citation: DOJ Lazarus Sony 2018) By manipulating stored data, adversaries may attempt to affect a business process, organizational understanding, and decision making.\n\nStored data could include a variety of file formats, such as Office files, databases, stored emails, and custom file formats. The type of modification and the impact it will have depends on the type of data as well as the goals and objectives of the adversary. For complex systems, an adversary would likely need special expertise and possibly access to specialized software related to the system that would typically be gained through a prolonged information gathering campaign in order to have the desired impact.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 15.028265548120324,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Stored Application Data is a mobile attack technique within the Collection tactic, targeting Android and iOS platforms. It involves adversaries accessing and collecting sensitive data stored locally within mobile applications, such as databases, configuration files, or cached credentials, prior to exfiltration. This technique is a specific form of local data staging for mobile environments."
    },
