            "58e9cd249d947f829a6021cf6ab16c2ca8e83317dbe07a294e2035bb904d0cf3",
            "bcae978c17bcddc0bf6419ae978e3471197801c36f73cff2fc88cecbe3d88d1a",
            "56f1014eb2d145c957f9bc0843f4e506735d7821e16355bcfbb6150b1b5f39db",
            "9f873c29a38dd265decb6517a2a1f3b5d4f90ccd42eb61039086ea0b5e74827e",
            "6005dcbe15d60293c556f05e98ed9a46d398a82e5ca4d00c91ebec68a209ea84",
            "a6b0847cf31ccc3f76538333498f8fef79d444a9d4ecfca0592861cf731ae6cb"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "akira"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "akira",
        "uncertain_mes": "[Akira](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1129) ransomware, written in C++, is most prominently (but not exclusively) associated with the ransomware-as-a-service entity [Akira](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1024). [Akira](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1129) ransomware has been used in attacks across North America, Europe, and Australia, with a focus on critical infrastructure sectors including manufacturing, education, and IT services. [Akira](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1129) ransomware employs hybrid encryption and threading to increase the speed and efficiency of encryption and runtime arguments for tailored attacks. Notable variants include Rust-based [Megazord](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1191) for targeting Windows and [Akira _v2](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1194) for targeting VMware ESXi servers.(Citation: Kersten Akira 2023)(Citation: CISA Akira Ransomware APR 2024)(Citation: Cisco Akira Ransomware OCT 2024)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.402020329371293,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Akira - S1129, also known as Akira ransomware, is a C++-based ransomware associated with the Akira ransomware-as-a-service group. It employs hybrid encryption and threading for efficient encryption, uses runtime arguments for tailored attacks, and targets Windows and VMware ESXi platforms, with notable variants like Rust-based Megazord for Windows and Akira_v2 for ESXi. Its technical behaviors include Kerberoasting for credential access, stopping services, inhibiting system recovery, and dumping the NTDS database. It has been used in attacks across North America, Europe, and Australia, focusing on sectors like manufacturing, education, and IT services.[/profile]"
    },
