        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1021.001",
        "uncertain_name": "remote desktop protocol",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) to log into a computer using the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP). The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user.\n\nRemote desktop is a common feature in operating systems. It allows a user to log into an interactive session with a system desktop graphical user interface on a remote system. Microsoft refers to its implementation of the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) as Remote Desktop Services (RDS).(Citation: TechNet Remote Desktop Services) \n\nAdversaries may connect to a remote system over RDP/RDS to expand access if the service is enabled and allows access to accounts with known credentials. Adversaries will likely use Credential Access techniques to acquire credentials to use with RDP. Adversaries may also use RDP in conjunction with the [Accessibility Features](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/008) or [Terminal Services DLL](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505/005) for Persistence.(Citation: Alperovitch Malware)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 9.970213656264274,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1021.001 - Remote Desktop Protocol is a Lateral Movement technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework, targeting Windows platforms. It involves adversaries using valid accounts to log into remote systems via RDP to gain interactive graphical access, enabling them to perform actions as the logged-on user. This technique is commonly enabled through Remote Desktop Services and is often paired with credential access for initial access and other techniques like Accessibility Features for persistence. It is widely used by numerous threat groups, including FIN7, APT41, and Volt Typhoon."
    },
