        "uncertain_id": "t1021.001",
        "uncertain_name": "remote desktop protocol",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) to log into a computer using the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP). The adversary may then perform actions as the logged-on user.\n\nRemote desktop is a common feature in operating systems. It allows a user to log into an interactive session with a system desktop graphical user interface on a remote system. Microsoft refers to its implementation of the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) as Remote Desktop Services (RDS).(Citation: TechNet Remote Desktop Services) \n\nAdversaries may connect to a remote system over RDP/RDS to expand access if the service is enabled and allows access to accounts with known credentials. Adversaries will likely use Credential Access techniques to acquire credentials to use with RDP. Adversaries may also use RDP in conjunction with the [Accessibility Features](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/008) or [Terminal Services DLL](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505/005) for Persistence.(Citation: Alperovitch Malware)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 9.266004255133279,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Remote Desktop Protocol is MITRE ATT&CK technique T1021.001, a Lateral Movement tactic for Windows platforms. It involves using valid accounts to log into remote systems via Microsoft's Remote Desktop Services (RDS) to expand access. A specific technical mechanic for session hijacking uses the native Windows command `tscon.exe` with system permissions to steal an active or disconnected RDP session without user prompts, a feature also present in tools like RedSnarf."
    },
    "19772": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "impact"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Service Stop"
        ],
        "unique_id": "5eec4c3c-77fc-4cbc-8b0d-c739299e353d",
        "name": "Service Stop",
        "description": "Adversaries may stop or disable services on a system to render those services unavailable to legitimate users. Stopping critical services or processes can inhibit or stop response to an incident or aid in the adversary's overall objectives to cause damage to the environment.(Citation: Talos Olympic Destroyer 2018)(Citation: Novetta Blockbuster) \n\nAdversaries may accomplish this by disabling individual services of high importance to an organization, such as `MSExchangeIS`, which will make Exchange content inaccessible.(Citation: Novetta Blockbuster) In some cases, adversaries may stop or disable many or all services to render systems unusable.(Citation: Talos Olympic Destroyer 2018) Services or processes may not allow for modification of their data stores while running. Adversaries may stop services or processes in order to conduct [Data Destruction](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1485) or [Data Encrypted for Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1486) on the data stores of services like Exchange and SQL Server, or on virtual machines hosted on ESXi infrastructure.(Citation: SecureWorks WannaCry Analysis)(Citation: Crowdstrike Hypervisor Jackpotting Pt 2 2021)\n\nThreat actors may also disable or stop service in cloud environments. For example, by leveraging the `DisableAPIServiceAccess` API in AWS, a threat actor may prevent the service from creating service-linked roles on new accounts in the AWS Organization.(Citation: Datadog Security Labs Cloud Persistence 2025)(Citation: AWS DisableAWSServiceAccess)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi",
            "IaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1489",
        "standard_attck_name": "Service Stop",
        "gid": 3656,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "indrik spider",
            "medusa group",
            "sandworm team",
            "lapsus$",
            "lazarus group",
            "wizard spider"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1489",
        "uncertain_name": "service stop",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may stop or disable services on a system to render those services unavailable to legitimate users. Stopping critical services or processes can inhibit or stop response to an incident or aid in the adversary's overall objectives to cause damage to the environment.(Citation: Talos Olympic Destroyer 2018)(Citation: Novetta Blockbuster) \n\nAdversaries may accomplish this by disabling individual services of high importance to an organization, such as <code>MSExchangeIS</code>, which will make Exchange content inaccessible.(Citation: Novetta Blockbuster) In some cases, adversaries may stop or disable many or all services to render systems unusable.(Citation: Talos Olympic Destroyer 2018) Services or processes may not allow for modification of their data stores while running. Adversaries may stop services or processes in order to conduct [Data Destruction](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1485) or [Data Encrypted for Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1486) on the data stores of services like Exchange and SQL Server, or on virtual machines hosted on ESXi infrastructure.(Citation: SecureWorks WannaCry Analysis)(Citation: Crowdstrike Hypervisor Jackpotting Pt 2 2021)\n\nThreat actors may also disable or stop service in cloud environments. For example, by leveraging the `DisableAPIServiceAccess` API in AWS, a threat actor may prevent the service from creating service-linked roles on new accounts in the AWS Organization.(Citation: Datadog Security Labs Cloud Persistence 2025)(Citation: AWS DisableAWSServiceAccess)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.247438084116213,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Service Stop is identified as MITRE ATT&CK technique T1489 under the Impact tactic, targeting platforms including Windows, Linux, macOS, ESXi, and IaaS. Adversaries stop or disable services to render them unavailable, often targeting critical services like MSExchangeIS to inhibit incident response or enable follow-on actions like data destruction. This can be achieved using commands like `systemctl stop` on Linux or `sc stop` on Windows, and in cloud environments via APIs such as AWS's `DisableAPIServiceAccess`."
    },
