    "19801": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "6ab98eb2-6a77-4ee3-9312-64a2ecde35b9",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "Akira",
        "is_family": true,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [
            "3298d203c2acb68c474e5fdad8379181890b4403d6491c523c13730129be3f75",
            "bd1f381e5a3db22e88776b7873d4d2835e9a1ec620571d2b1da0c58f81c84a56",
            "16f83f056177c4ec24c7e99d01ca9d9d6713bd0497eeedb777a3ffefa99c97f0"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "akira"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "akira",
        "uncertain_mes": "[Akira](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1129) ransomware, written in C++, is most prominently (but not exclusively) associated with the ransomware-as-a-service entity [Akira](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1024). [Akira](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1129) ransomware has been used in attacks across North America, Europe, and Australia, with a focus on critical infrastructure sectors including manufacturing, education, and IT services. [Akira](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1129) ransomware employs hybrid encryption and threading to increase the speed and efficiency of encryption and runtime arguments for tailored attacks. Notable variants include Rust-based [Megazord](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1191) for targeting Windows and [Akira _v2](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1194) for targeting VMware ESXi servers.(Citation: Kersten Akira 2023)(Citation: CISA Akira Ransomware APR 2024)(Citation: Cisco Akira Ransomware OCT 2024)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 7.699500340161678,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Akira is a ransomware family, also known as Akira_v2 and Megazord, and is associated with the ransomware-as-a-service group Akira (G1024). It employs techniques including T1129 (Shared Modules) for execution, abuses Domain Accounts (T1078.002) and Default Accounts (T1078.001) for persistence and privilege escalation, and uses T1489 (Service Stop) for impact. The malware is written in C++ and Rust, uses hybrid encryption, and targets Windows and VMware ESXi platforms across critical infrastructure sectors in North America, Europe, and Australia. Blockchain analysis has indicated connections to the Conti ransomware operation.[/profile]"
    },
    "19802": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "execution"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "2ea9be68-1983-4c1c-ba31-00b2eff72360",
        "aliases": [
            "System Services"
        ],
        "name": "System Services",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may abuse system services or daemons to execute commands or programs. Adversaries can execute malicious content by interacting with or creating services either locally or remotely. Many services are set to run at boot, which can aid in achieving persistence ([Create or Modify System Process](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543)), but adversaries can also abuse services for one-time or temporary execution.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1569",
        "standard_attck_name": "System Services",
        "gid": 3686,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1569",
        "uncertain_name": "system services",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may abuse system services or daemons to execute commands or programs. Adversaries can execute malicious content by interacting with or creating services either locally or remotely. Many services are set to run at boot, which can aid in achieving persistence ([Create or Modify System Process](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1543)), but adversaries can also abuse services for one-time or temporary execution.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 4.1351503311890525,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]System Services is identified as MITRE ATT&CK technique T1569 under the Execution tactic, applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. This technique involves adversaries abusing system services or daemons to execute commands or programs, either by interacting with existing services or creating new ones locally or remotely. While often used for one-time execution, it can also facilitate persistence, as services configured to run at boot are linked to techniques like Create or Modify System Process (T1543). The method is associated with several CVEs, including CVE-2020-14883 and CVE-2024-3400.[/profile]"
    },
    "19803": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "execution"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Shared Modules"
        ],
        "unique_id": "293bff9c-99e6-41e6-81e4-9f420223ab47",
        "name": "T1129",
        "description": "Adversaries may execute malicious payloads via loading shared modules. Shared modules are executable files that are loaded into processes to provide access to reusable code, such as specific custom functions or invoking OS API functions (i.e., [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106)).\n\nAdversaries may use this functionality as a way to execute arbitrary payloads on a victim system. For example, adversaries can modularize functionality of their malware into shared objects that perform various functions such as managing C2 network communications or execution of specific actions on objective.\n\nThe Linux & macOS module loader can load and execute shared objects from arbitrary local paths. This functionality resides in `dlfcn.h` in functions such as `dlopen` and `dlsym`. Although macOS can execute `.so` files, common practice uses `.dylib` files.(Citation: Apple Dev Dynamic Libraries)(Citation: Linux Shared Libraries)(Citation: RotaJakiro 2021 netlab360 analysis)(Citation: Unit42 OceanLotus 2017)\n\nThe Windows module loader can be instructed to load DLLs from arbitrary local paths and arbitrary Universal Naming Convention (UNC) network paths. This functionality resides in `NTDLL.dll` and is part of the Windows [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106) which is called from functions like `LoadLibrary` at run time.(Citation: Microsoft DLL)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1129",
        "standard_attck_name": "Shared Modules",
        "gid": 3687,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "mustang panda"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1129",
        "uncertain_name": "shared modules",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may execute malicious payloads via loading shared modules. Shared modules are executable files that are loaded into processes to provide access to reusable code, such as specific custom functions or invoking OS API functions (i.e., [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106)).\n\nAdversaries may use this functionality as a way to execute arbitrary payloads on a victim system. For example, adversaries can modularize functionality of their malware into shared objects that perform various functions such as managing C2 network communications or execution of specific actions on objective.\n\nThe Linux & macOS module loader can load and execute shared objects from arbitrary local paths. This functionality resides in `dlfcn.h` in functions such as `dlopen` and `dlsym`. Although macOS can execute `.so` files, common practice uses `.dylib` files.(Citation: Apple Dev Dynamic Libraries)(Citation: Linux Shared Libraries)(Citation: RotaJakiro 2021 netlab360 analysis)(Citation: Unit42 OceanLotus 2017)\n\nThe Windows module loader can be instructed to load DLLs from arbitrary local paths and arbitrary Universal Naming Convention (UNC) network paths. This functionality resides in `NTDLL.dll` and is part of the Windows [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106) which is called from functions like `LoadLibrary` at run time.(Citation: Microsoft DLL)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 15.43214962823117,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1129, also known as Shared Modules, is an execution technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves adversaries loading and executing malicious payloads via shared modules like DLLs, .so, or .dylib files to run arbitrary code. On Windows, modules can be loaded from local or UNC paths using APIs like LoadLibrary, while on Linux/macOS, functions like dlopen and dlsym are used from dlfcn.h. This method allows adversaries to modularize malware functionality, such as command and control, directly within a process's memory."
    },
    "19804": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "execution"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Shared Modules"
        ],
        "unique_id": "6bdc39e6-19e1-44f5-b49d-48dee95f6c2e",
        "name": "Shared Modules",
        "description": "Adversaries may execute malicious payloads via loading shared modules. Shared modules are executable files that are loaded into processes to provide access to reusable code, such as specific custom functions or invoking OS API functions (i.e., [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106)).\n\nAdversaries may use this functionality as a way to execute arbitrary payloads on a victim system. For example, adversaries can modularize functionality of their malware into shared objects that perform various functions such as managing C2 network communications or execution of specific actions on objective.\n\nThe Linux & macOS module loader can load and execute shared objects from arbitrary local paths. This functionality resides in `dlfcn.h` in functions such as `dlopen` and `dlsym`. Although macOS can execute `.so` files, common practice uses `.dylib` files.(Citation: Apple Dev Dynamic Libraries)(Citation: Linux Shared Libraries)(Citation: RotaJakiro 2021 netlab360 analysis)(Citation: Unit42 OceanLotus 2017)\n\nThe Windows module loader can be instructed to load DLLs from arbitrary local paths and arbitrary Universal Naming Convention (UNC) network paths. This functionality resides in `NTDLL.dll` and is part of the Windows [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106) which is called from functions like `LoadLibrary` at run time.(Citation: Microsoft DLL)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1129",
        "standard_attck_name": "Shared Modules",
        "gid": 3688,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "mustang panda"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1129",
        "uncertain_name": "shared modules",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may execute malicious payloads via loading shared modules. Shared modules are executable files that are loaded into processes to provide access to reusable code, such as specific custom functions or invoking OS API functions (i.e., [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106)).\n\nAdversaries may use this functionality as a way to execute arbitrary payloads on a victim system. For example, adversaries can modularize functionality of their malware into shared objects that perform various functions such as managing C2 network communications or execution of specific actions on objective.\n\nThe Linux & macOS module loader can load and execute shared objects from arbitrary local paths. This functionality resides in `dlfcn.h` in functions such as `dlopen` and `dlsym`. Although macOS can execute `.so` files, common practice uses `.dylib` files.(Citation: Apple Dev Dynamic Libraries)(Citation: Linux Shared Libraries)(Citation: RotaJakiro 2021 netlab360 analysis)(Citation: Unit42 OceanLotus 2017)\n\nThe Windows module loader can be instructed to load DLLs from arbitrary local paths and arbitrary Universal Naming Convention (UNC) network paths. This functionality resides in `NTDLL.dll` and is part of the Windows [Native API](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1106) which is called from functions like `LoadLibrary` at run time.(Citation: Microsoft DLL)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 14.742162007961591,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Shared Modules is a MITRE ATT&CK technique identified as T1129 under the Execution tactic, applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. It involves adversaries executing malicious payloads by loading shared modules like DLLs (Windows) or shared objects (Linux/macOS) into processes. This is achieved using standard OS functions such as `LoadLibrary` on Windows or `dlopen` and `dlsym` from `dlfcn.h` on Unix-like systems, allowing code to be loaded from arbitrary local or network paths. The technique enables modular malware design for functions like command and control and has been associated with groups like Mustang Panda."
    },
    "19805": {
