        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1072",
        "uncertain_name": "software deployment tools",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may gain access to and use centralized software suites installed within an enterprise to execute commands and move laterally through the network. Configuration management and software deployment applications may be used in an enterprise network or cloud environment for routine administration purposes. These systems may also be integrated into CI/CD pipelines. Examples of such solutions include: SCCM, HBSS, Altiris, AWS Systems Manager, Microsoft Intune, Azure Arc, and GCP Deployment Manager.  \n\nAccess to network-wide or enterprise-wide endpoint management software may enable an adversary to achieve remote code execution on all connected systems. The access may be used to laterally move to other systems, gather information, or cause a specific effect, such as wiping the hard drives on all endpoints.\n\nSaaS-based configuration management services may allow for broad [Cloud Administration Command](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1651) on cloud-hosted instances, as well as the execution of arbitrary commands on on-premises endpoints. For example, Microsoft Configuration Manager allows Global or Intune Administrators to run scripts as SYSTEM on on-premises devices joined to Entra ID.(Citation: SpecterOps Lateral Movement from Azure to On-Prem AD 2020) Such services may also utilize [Web Protocols](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071/001) to communicate back to adversary owned infrastructure.(Citation: Mitiga Security Advisory: SSM Agent as Remote Access Trojan)\n\nNetwork infrastructure devices may also have configuration management tools that can be similarly abused by adversaries.(Citation: Fortinet Zero-Day and Custom Malware Used by Suspected Chinese Actor in Espionage Operation)\n\nThe permissions required for this action vary by system configuration; local credentials may be sufficient with direct access to the third-party system, or specific domain credentials may be required. However, the system may require an administrative account to log in or to access specific functionality.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.238821583879725,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1072, known as Software Deployment Tools, is a technique within the Execution and Lateral Movement tactics applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, Network Devices, and SaaS platforms. It involves adversaries abusing centralized enterprise software suites like SCCM, HBSS, Altiris, AWS Systems Manager, Microsoft Intune, Azure Arc, and GCP Deployment Manager to achieve remote code execution across connected systems. Access to these configuration management and deployment tools, which may be integrated into CI/CD pipelines, allows for broad command execution to move laterally, gather information, or cause specific effects like mass data wiping, often using administrative or specific domain credentials."
    },
    "19822": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "lateral-movement"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Exploitation of Remote Services"
        ],
        "unique_id": "0eb6b7f1-0697-4759-af42-46dc1c13c81b",
        "name": "T1210",
        "description": "Adversaries may exploit remote services to gain unauthorized access to internal systems once inside of a network. Exploitation of a software vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. A common goal for post-compromise exploitation of remote services is for lateral movement to enable access to a remote system.\n\nAn adversary may need to determine if the remote system is in a vulnerable state, which may be done through [Network Service Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1046) or other Discovery methods looking for common, vulnerable software that may be deployed in the network, the lack of certain patches that may indicate vulnerabilities,  or security software that may be used to detect or contain remote exploitation. Servers are likely a high value target for lateral movement exploitation, but endpoint systems may also be at risk if they provide an advantage or access to additional resources.\n\nThere are several well-known vulnerabilities that exist in common services such as SMB(Citation: CIS Multiple SMB Vulnerabilities) and RDP(Citation: NVD CVE-2017-0176) as well as applications that may be used within internal networks such as MySQL(Citation: NVD CVE-2016-6662) and web server services.(Citation: NVD CVE-2014-7169)(Citation: Ars Technica VMWare Code Execution Vulnerability 2021) Additionally, there have been a number of vulnerabilities in VMware vCenter installations, which may enable threat actors to move laterally from the compromised vCenter server to virtual machines or even to ESXi hypervisors.(Citation: Broadcom VMSA-2024-0019)\n\nDepending on the permissions level of the vulnerable remote service an adversary may achieve [Exploitation for Privilege Escalation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1068) as a result of lateral movement exploitation as well.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "earth lusca",
            "dragonfly",
            "muddywater",
            "fin7",
            "fox kitten",
            "menupass",
            "apt28",
            "tonto team",
            "threat group-3390",
            "ember bear",
            "wizard spider"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1210",
        "uncertain_name": "exploitation of remote services",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may exploit remote services to gain unauthorized access to internal systems once inside of a network. Exploitation of a software vulnerability occurs when an adversary takes advantage of a programming error in a program, service, or within the operating system software or kernel itself to execute adversary-controlled code. A common goal for post-compromise exploitation of remote services is for lateral movement to enable access to a remote system.\n\nAn adversary may need to determine if the remote system is in a vulnerable state, which may be done through [Network Service Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1046) or other Discovery methods looking for common, vulnerable software that may be deployed in the network, the lack of certain patches that may indicate vulnerabilities,  or security software that may be used to detect or contain remote exploitation. Servers are likely a high value target for lateral movement exploitation, but endpoint systems may also be at risk if they provide an advantage or access to additional resources.\n\nThere are several well-known vulnerabilities that exist in common services such as SMB(Citation: CIS Multiple SMB Vulnerabilities) and RDP(Citation: NVD CVE-2017-0176) as well as applications that may be used within internal networks such as MySQL(Citation: NVD CVE-2016-6662) and web server services.(Citation: NVD CVE-2014-7169)(Citation: Ars Technica VMWare Code Execution Vulnerability 2021) Additionally, there have been a number of vulnerabilities in VMware vCenter installations, which may enable threat actors to move laterally from the compromised vCenter server to virtual machines or even to ESXi hypervisors.(Citation: Broadcom VMSA-2024-0019)\n\nDepending on the permissions level of the vulnerable remote service an adversary may achieve [Exploitation for Privilege Escalation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1068) as a result of lateral movement exploitation as well.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.348892395958455,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1210, Exploitation of Remote Services, is a Lateral Movement technique targeting Windows, Linux, macOS, and ESXi platforms. Adversaries exploit programming errors in remote services, such as SMB, RDP, MySQL, or web servers, to execute their own code and gain unauthorized access to internal systems. This often follows network discovery to identify vulnerable targets, with servers being high-value objectives, and can also lead to privilege escalation. Threat actors like APT28, MuddyWater, and Wizard Spider have employed this technique."
    },
    "19823": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "discovery"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "9debdacf-9a6c-4dba-8778-1013159c6990",
        "aliases": [
            "Network Service Discovery"
        ],
        "name": "Network Service Discovery",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of services running on remote hosts and local network infrastructure devices, including those that may be vulnerable to remote software exploitation. Common methods to acquire this information include port, vulnerability, and/or wordlist scans using tools that are brought onto a system.(Citation: CISA AR21-126A FIVEHANDS May 2021)   \n\nWithin cloud environments, adversaries may attempt to discover services running on other cloud hosts. Additionally, if the cloud environment is connected to a on-premises environment, adversaries may be able to identify services running on non-cloud systems as well.\n\nWithin macOS environments, adversaries may use the native Bonjour application to discover services running on other macOS hosts within a network. The Bonjour mDNSResponder daemon automatically registers and advertises a host’s registered services on the network. For example, adversaries can use a mDNS query (such as `dns-sd -B _ssh._tcp .`) to find other systems broadcasting the ssh service.(Citation: apple doco bonjour description)(Citation: macOS APT Activity Bradley)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "Containers",
            "IaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1046",
        "standard_attck_name": "Network Service Discovery",
        "gid": 3707,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "fin13",
            "fin6",
            "inc ransom",
            "darkvishnya",
            "agrius",
            "mustang panda",
            "redcurl",
            "cobalt group",
            "leafminer",
            "blacktech",
            "suckfly",
            "blackbyte",
            "magic hound",
            "medusa group",
            "apt41",
            "fox kitten",
            "threat group-3390",
            "volt typhoon",
            "chimera",
            "teamtnt",
            "apt32",
            "lotus blossom",
            "rocke",
            "naikon",
            "lazarus group",
            "oilrig",
            "ember bear",
            "backdoordiplomacy",
            "tropic trooper",
            "menupass",
            "apt39"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1046",
        "uncertain_name": "network service discovery",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may attempt to get a listing of services running on remote hosts and local network infrastructure devices, including those that may be vulnerable to remote software exploitation. Common methods to acquire this information include port, vulnerability, and/or wordlist scans using tools that are brought onto a system.(Citation: CISA AR21-126A FIVEHANDS May 2021)   \n\nWithin cloud environments, adversaries may attempt to discover services running on other cloud hosts. Additionally, if the cloud environment is connected to a on-premises environment, adversaries may be able to identify services running on non-cloud systems as well.\n\nWithin macOS environments, adversaries may use the native Bonjour application to discover services running on other macOS hosts within a network. The Bonjour mDNSResponder daemon automatically registers and advertises a host’s registered services on the network. For example, adversaries can use a mDNS query (such as <code>dns-sd -B _ssh._tcp .</code>) to find other systems broadcasting the ssh service.(Citation: apple doco bonjour description)(Citation: macOS APT Activity Bradley)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 9.901428679699183,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Network Service Discovery, identified as T1046 within the MITRE ATT&CK framework, is a Discovery tactic used across platforms including Windows, Linux, macOS, Network Devices, Containers, and IaaS. The technique involves enumerating services on remote hosts and network devices to identify potential targets for exploitation, using methods such as port, vulnerability, and wordlist scans. In cloud environments, adversaries extend discovery to other cloud hosts and connected on-premises systems, while on macOS, they can leverage the native Bonjour mDNSResponder daemon with commands like `dns-sd -B _ssh._tcp .` to find services. This activity is commonly associated with threat groups such as Volt Typhoon, Lazarus Group, and APT41."
    },
