            "5bb45f372fb4df6a9c6a5460fa1845f5e96af53aa41939eb251cbe989a5cac6c",
            "d27eeb48b1a74efd8710ef4ce62ee8469dd2352b0079c5b1c82e8da43fe932a2",
            "bbcdffd6fa3b1370dfc091bfd3bfca38be013f72f94af7ef29466d911c9604d8",
            "bb89a6bbddc5dda36542a5fef230b8fa9d98fbdb0ec4fa1794b8c28a0b5a3af4",
            "d15af7984ed9b33093d7d5725c84ab24edf7c4ff02af3ac0a6c3aa9d5f7e12f4",
            "b2e26c7ce901296822085164ede73557a10badfdf99d1aa30f338446d0beb2d7",
            "https://tip.neiki.dev/file/64d8f887e33781bb814eaefa98dd64368da9a8d38bd9da4a76f04a23b6eb9de5/content.",
            "0af1b8cd042b6e2972c8ef43d98c0a0642047ec89493d315909629bcf185dffd"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 0.0,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]This intrusion set, likely the cryptojacking-focused group TeamTNT, is also known as backdoordiplomacy, backdip, quarian, and cloudcomputating. Its key malware includes Tsunami, Sliver, XMRig, and Platypus, and it employs TTPs such as Container and Resource Discovery (T1613), persistence via Cron (T1053.003) and Systemd Service (T1543.002), credential access from Unsecured Credentials (T1552), and command and control using DNS (T1071.004). The group is attributed to or shows overlaps with Russian-affiliated actors and primarily targets Linux-based cloud and container platforms, such as Docker, Apache Hadoop, Redis, and Confluence, with infrastructure involving domains like b.9-9-8.com. Its core motivation is resource hijacking for cryptomining.[/profile]"
    },
