            "5d74d439bbb0be789e23bdaafd8cff938e6e686af7c8e215dc945cacc88d131c",
            "46b8e68f5e85935349d0bfc555b9786f7adbac9ec9a9fa174ba0c4f89baa098f",
            "57e76a7af5bafb4ff06f5f44dcf1182ea5c6a8682651c260f555c52fd441b412",
            "148cd318aec19451b9ad17e58e0d97ebaffd46b56d3528608de20b95dd429c45",
            "15899e250892c2cc6b38d7cdcd2a3934a49c5dca954889564a98d15a52bf3b7c",
            "2776c052d11f52501871c4cb5a051a1970f002c3f099969040945fb94a158d9a",
            "27f482377777a1b8e1e679863685f64121f28e1e6e2bba832397269d1763e118"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.705955571269423,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Caiman is an information-stealing malware. It is also known as a variant tracked alongside Culebra Variant, Salve, and Kiron. It is attributed to LATAM-based eCrime actors and is available for purchase on underground forums. Its key behaviors include using Data Obfuscation and Data Encoding for C2 traffic, employing Automated Exfiltration for data transfer, and utilizing Application Layer Protocols like HTTP(S) for C2 communication. It targets users via User Execution, such as opening malicious documents. Its known infrastructure includes domains like lovecollege.hosthampster.com, massgrave.site, adjunto.pdfxml.store, api.cacher.io, and contpt.top, with targeting focused on Latin American countries including Costa Rica, Mexico, Chile, Argentina, and Peru.[/profile]"
    },
