            "d7a918b29b4423b2a4be151f1b37c28abc081068c13a04ad8fd70dbd725d659b",
            "ba4e715fe25aeaaf186e8395c2f13ca580457ab4e8ec1c037fd13821d97a6848",
            "0f035dced631ac58cfae510cfc61bb1dbef119331a8aea8d5c724a5ddca0f8c5",
            "fc258ef827620184253ba37d94efc0043745c29cf3c9f21a6c730f7727d6d076",
            "129971e378991d14c444db7a7f4c9a16ece750dd6498261d2f35c85baa9bfd07",
            "5f6c0ba669db489bc2ff186af312bfe7616f9e4a12706e195225da7168e10db0",
            "aec68d256d8d2caf2d94c5944279806dd4da36d125c7a7d1485c89f718d0db15",
            "60b32e40ec0a5e59081fa9816a26346892899175ce97c811761423c3533e0651",
            "b23aabe16db5f6ccdd061b457d01b94647ed5b5852806624dca277b43d63e188"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 0.0,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]This intrusion set, likely the Latin American eCrime group known as Malteiro or Scully Spider, primarily uses malware families such as Astaroth (Guildma), Mispadu (URSA), Culebra, Salve, and Kiron. Its key TTPs include Automated Exfiltration (T1020) for data theft, and Data Obfuscation (T1001), Data Encoding (T1132), and Encrypted Channels (T1573) for command and control, with initial compromise often relying on User Execution (T1204) via phishing. The group is attributed to Latin America-based cybercriminal actors and targets entities in Costa Rica, Mexico, Chile, Argentina, and Peru, utilizing infrastructure like massgrave.site, contpt.top, and api.cacher.io. Its motivation is financial gain through information and banking theft."
    },
