            "9d96e4816a59475768d461a71cecf20fd99215ce289ecae8c865cf45feeb8802",
            "cfc814a16547dd4e92607bd42d2722cc567492e88d2830d7d28a0cc20bf3950c",
            "ee8e8471fbe1b7fc85508e549444893bdea7579c5032c2626abcb1356129787e",
            "41d9b6d8cf0fff85bf35327d4b94db629cd9f754c487672911b7f701fe8c5539",
            "09a4adef9a7374616851e5e2a7d9539e1b9808e153538af94ad1d6d73a3a1232",
            "b0c0f38551a119d0b633c1b65e61b493574ed7083e3e003c83aa19dff7a0524b",
            "f1b4eb84e77e39803a0463b49b66600adb19347512354d0481568a8411b75b24",
            "2b6928101efa6ededc7da18e7894866710c10794b8cbaf43b48c721e9731c41a"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 0.0,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]This intrusion set is most likely APT37 (also known as ScarCruft, Group123, and Reaper). Its key malware includes ROKRAT, Konni RAT, and KONNI. The group employs sophisticated defense evasion techniques such as Process Doppelgänging and NTFS File Attributes for fileless execution and data hiding, and uses Windows Remote Management for lateral movement. It is attributed to North Korean state-sponsored actors and has historically targeted sectors and regions including Japan and South Korea, with motivations aligned with espionage and data theft.[/profile]"
    },
