            "magic hound",
            "fox kitten",
            "apt41",
            "aquatic panda",
            "storm-0501",
            "unc3886",
            "apt-c-36",
            "fin7",
            "apt32",
            "lazarus group",
            "naikon",
            "promethium",
            "bitter",
            "kimsuky",
            "backdoordiplomacy",
            "higaisa",
            "wizard spider"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1036.004",
        "uncertain_name": "masquerade task or service",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may attempt to manipulate the name of a task or service to make it appear legitimate or benign. Tasks/services executed by the Task Scheduler or systemd will typically be given a name and/or description.(Citation: TechNet Schtasks)(Citation: Systemd Service Units) Windows services will have a service name as well as a display name. Many benign tasks and services exist that have commonly associated names. Adversaries may give tasks or services names that are similar or identical to those of legitimate ones.\n\nTasks or services contain other fields, such as a description, that adversaries may attempt to make appear legitimate.(Citation: Palo Alto Shamoon Nov 2016)(Citation: Fysbis Dr Web Analysis)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 11.442005491496758,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity is MITRE ATT&CK technique T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service, a Defense Evasion tactic applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves adversaries manipulating the name or description of a scheduled task (e.g., via Task Scheduler or systemd) or a Windows service to appear legitimate, often by mimicking the names of benign system tasks. This technique is commonly used by groups including FIN7, Lazarus Group, and APT41."
    },
    "20103": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Rename Legitimate Utilities"
        ],
        "unique_id": "a21d4e78-d2af-4a67-837d-7b95fa5bb6ed",
        "name": "T1036.003",
        "description": "Adversaries may rename legitimate / system utilities to try to evade security mechanisms concerning the usage of those utilities. Security monitoring and control mechanisms may be in place for legitimate utilities adversaries are capable of abusing, including both built-in binaries and tools such as PSExec, AutoHotKey, and IronPython.(Citation: LOLBAS Main Site)(Citation: Huntress Python Malware 2025)(Citation: The DFIR Report AutoHotKey 2023)(Citation: Splunk Detect Renamed PSExec) It may be possible to bypass those security mechanisms by renaming the utility prior to utilization (ex: rename `rundll32.exe`).(Citation: Elastic Masquerade Ball) An alternative case occurs when a legitimate utility is copied or moved to a different directory and renamed to avoid detections based on these utilities executing from non-standard paths.(Citation: F-Secure CozyDuke)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1036.003",
        "standard_attck_name": "Rename Legitimate Utilities",
        "gid": 3989,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "apt38",
            "gallium",
            "apt32",
            "daggerfly",
            "menupass",
            "lazarus group"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1036.003",
        "uncertain_name": "rename legitimate utilities",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may rename legitimate / system utilities to try to evade security mechanisms concerning the usage of those utilities. Security monitoring and control mechanisms may be in place for legitimate utilities adversaries are capable of abusing, including both built-in binaries and tools such as PSExec, AutoHotKey, and IronPython.(Citation: LOLBAS Main Site)(Citation: Huntress Python Malware 2025)(Citation: The DFIR Report AutoHotKey 2023)(Citation: Splunk Detect Renamed PSExec) It may be possible to bypass those security mechanisms by renaming the utility prior to utilization (ex: rename <code>rundll32.exe</code>).(Citation: Elastic Masquerade Ball) An alternative case occurs when a legitimate utility is copied or moved to a different directory and renamed to avoid detections based on these utilities executing from non-standard paths.(Citation: F-Secure CozyDuke)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 14.334757228944818,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity T1036.003, known as \"Rename Legitimate Utilities,\" is a sub-technique of T1036 (Masquerading) within the Defense Evasion tactic, applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. It involves adversaries renaming legitimate system utilities or tools (e.g., rundll32.exe) or moving them to non-standard directories to bypass security controls and monitoring that are triggered by the original filenames or execution paths. This technique has been associated with threat groups including APT38, Gallium, and Lazarus Group."
    },
    "20104": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "collection"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "7d14a624-47c9-4aee-83c9-9a5a1a8bccee",
        "aliases": [
            "Archive via Utility"
        ],
        "name": "Archive via Utility",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may use utilities to compress and/or encrypt collected data prior to exfiltration. Many utilities include functionalities to compress, encrypt, or otherwise package data into a format that is easier/more secure to transport.\n\nAdversaries may abuse various utilities to compress or encrypt data before exfiltration. Some third party utilities may be preinstalled, such as `tar` on Linux and macOS or `zip` on Windows systems. \n\nOn Windows, `diantz` or ` makecab` may be used to package collected files into a cabinet (.cab) file. `diantz` may also be used to download and compress files from remote locations (i.e. [Remote Data Staging](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1074/002)).(Citation: diantz.exe_lolbas) `xcopy` on Windows can copy files and directories with a variety of options. Additionally, adversaries may use [certutil](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0160) to Base64 encode collected data before exfiltration. \n\nAdversaries may use also third party utilities, such as 7-Zip, WinRAR, and WinZip, to perform similar activities.(Citation: 7zip Homepage)(Citation: WinRAR Homepage)(Citation: WinZip Homepage)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1560.001",
        "standard_attck_name": "Archive via Utility",
        "gid": 3990,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
