    "20165": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "command-and-control"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Fallback Channels"
        ],
        "unique_id": "38c0f046-f70d-48df-bbfc-361d18b43f44",
        "name": "T1008",
        "description": "Adversaries may use fallback or alternate communication channels if the primary channel is compromised or inaccessible in order to maintain reliable command and control and to avoid data transfer thresholds.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1008",
        "standard_attck_name": "Fallback Channels",
        "gid": 4051,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "fin7",
            "apt41",
            "lazarus group",
            "oilrig",
            "unc3886"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1008",
        "uncertain_name": "fallback channels",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use fallback or alternate communication channels if the primary channel is compromised or inaccessible in order to maintain reliable command and control and to avoid data transfer thresholds.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.420680468402628,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1008, known as Fallback Channels, is a command and control technique within the MITRE ATT&CK framework applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, and ESXi platforms. It involves adversaries establishing backup communication channels to maintain C2 connectivity if the primary channel is lost or blocked. This technique is often implemented in conjunction with others, such as Multi-Stage Channels, where a loader may have backup callbacks, or with Dynamic Resolution to algorithmically reestablish contact. It has been associated with threat groups including FIN7, APT41, and Lazarus Group."
    },
    "20166": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "VBA Stomping"
        ],
        "unique_id": "ea92d7f0-bdf3-4300-b677-8f24a1fc2b02",
        "name": "T1564.007",
        "description": "Adversaries may hide malicious Visual Basic for Applications (VBA) payloads embedded within MS Office documents by replacing the VBA source code with benign data.(Citation: FireEye VBA stomp Feb 2020)\n\nMS Office documents with embedded VBA content store source code inside of module streams. Each module stream has a `PerformanceCache` that stores a separate compiled version of the VBA source code known as p-code. The p-code is executed when the MS Office version specified in the `_VBA_PROJECT` stream (which contains the version-dependent description of the VBA project) matches the version of the host MS Office application.(Citation: Evil Clippy May 2019)(Citation: Microsoft _VBA_PROJECT Stream)\n\nAn adversary may hide malicious VBA code by overwriting the VBA source code location with zero’s, benign code, or random bytes while leaving the previously compiled malicious p-code. Tools that scan for malicious VBA source code may be bypassed as the unwanted code is hidden in the compiled p-code. If the VBA source code is removed, some tools might even think that there are no macros present. If there is a version match between the `_VBA_PROJECT` stream and host MS Office application, the p-code will be executed, otherwise the benign VBA source code will be decompressed and recompiled to p-code, thus removing malicious p-code and potentially bypassing dynamic analysis.(Citation: Walmart Roberts Oct 2018)(Citation: FireEye VBA stomp Feb 2020)(Citation: pcodedmp Bontchev)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1564.007",
        "standard_attck_name": "VBA Stomping",
        "gid": 4052,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1564.007",
        "uncertain_name": "vba stomping",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may hide malicious Visual Basic for Applications (VBA) payloads embedded within MS Office documents by replacing the VBA source code with benign data.(Citation: FireEye VBA stomp Feb 2020)\n\nMS Office documents with embedded VBA content store source code inside of module streams. Each module stream has a <code>PerformanceCache</code> that stores a separate compiled version of the VBA source code known as p-code. The p-code is executed when the MS Office version specified in the <code>_VBA_PROJECT</code> stream (which contains the version-dependent description of the VBA project) matches the version of the host MS Office application.(Citation: Evil Clippy May 2019)(Citation: Microsoft _VBA_PROJECT Stream)\n\nAn adversary may hide malicious VBA code by overwriting the VBA source code location with zero’s, benign code, or random bytes while leaving the previously compiled malicious p-code. Tools that scan for malicious VBA source code may be bypassed as the unwanted code is hidden in the compiled p-code. If the VBA source code is removed, some tools might even think that there are no macros present. If there is a version match between the <code>_VBA_PROJECT</code> stream and host MS Office application, the p-code will be executed, otherwise the benign VBA source code will be decompressed and recompiled to p-code, thus removing malicious p-code and potentially bypassing dynamic analysis.(Citation: Walmart Roberts Oct 2018)(Citation: FireEye VBA stomp Feb 2020)(Citation: pcodedmp Bontchev)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 7.44779377854116,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1564.007, also known as VBA Stomping, is a Defense Evasion technique for Windows, macOS, and Linux platforms. It hides malicious Visual Basic for Applications (VBA) payloads in MS Office documents by overwriting the readable VBA source code module with benign or null data while leaving the compiled malicious p-code intact. This allows the malicious p-code to execute if the Office version matches the document's specification, bypassing static analysis tools that scan source code and potentially evading dynamic analysis if a version mismatch causes recompilation of the benign source. It is a sub-technique of T1564 (Hide Artifacts)."
    },
