    "20503": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "exfiltration"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Scheduled Transfer"
        ],
        "unique_id": "8d775eb0-1b01-4a3f-b649-ed56aab1d9c0",
        "name": "T1029",
        "description": "Adversaries may schedule data exfiltration to be performed only at certain times of day or at certain intervals. This could be done to blend traffic patterns with normal activity or availability.\n\nWhen scheduled exfiltration is used, other exfiltration techniques likely apply as well to transfer the information out of the network, such as [Exfiltration Over C2 Channel](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1041) or [Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1048).",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1029",
        "standard_attck_name": "Scheduled Transfer",
        "gid": 4389,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "higaisa"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1029",
        "uncertain_name": "scheduled transfer",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may schedule data exfiltration to be performed only at certain times of day or at certain intervals. This could be done to blend traffic patterns with normal activity or availability.\n\nWhen scheduled exfiltration is used, other exfiltration techniques likely apply as well to transfer the information out of the network, such as [Exfiltration Over C2 Channel](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1041) or [Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1048).",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 15.43214962823117,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1029, Scheduled Transfer, is a MITRE ATT&CK exfiltration technique for macOS, Linux, and Windows. It involves adversaries scheduling data exfiltration to occur at specific times or intervals to blend traffic with normal network activity. This scheduling is typically used in conjunction with other exfiltration methods, such as Exfiltration Over C2 Channel or Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol, to transfer the data out of the network."
    },
    "20504": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "lateral-movement"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "098cfb0b-f1ce-41a4-8610-309bb9aa5790",
        "aliases": [
            "Internal Spearphishing"
        ],
        "name": "Internal Spearphishing",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "After they already have access to accounts or systems within the environment, adversaries may use internal spearphishing to gain access to additional information or compromise other users within the same organization. Internal spearphishing is multi-staged campaign where a legitimate account is initially compromised either by controlling the user's device or by compromising the account credentials of the user. Adversaries may then attempt to take advantage of the trusted internal account to increase the likelihood of tricking more victims into falling for phish attempts, often incorporating [Impersonation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1656).(Citation: Trend Micro - Int SP)\n\nFor example, adversaries may leverage [Spearphishing Attachment](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/001) or [Spearphishing Link](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/002) as part of internal spearphishing to deliver a payload or redirect to an external site to capture credentials through [Input Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1056) on sites that mimic login interfaces.\n\nAdversaries may also leverage internal chat apps, such as Microsoft Teams, to spread malicious content or engage users in attempts to capture sensitive information and/or credentials.(Citation: Int SP - chat apps)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Office Suite",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1534",
        "standard_attck_name": "Internal Spearphishing",
        "gid": 4390,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "gamaredon group",
            "hexane",
            "leviathan",
            "kimsuky"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1534",
        "uncertain_name": "internal spearphishing",
        "uncertain_mes": "After they already have access to accounts or systems within the environment, adversaries may use internal spearphishing to gain access to additional information or compromise other users within the same organization. Internal spearphishing is multi-staged campaign where a legitimate account is initially compromised either by controlling the user's device or by compromising the account credentials of the user. Adversaries may then attempt to take advantage of the trusted internal account to increase the likelihood of tricking more victims into falling for phish attempts, often incorporating [Impersonation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1656).(Citation: Trend Micro - Int SP)\n\nFor example, adversaries may leverage [Spearphishing Attachment](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/001) or [Spearphishing Link](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/002) as part of internal spearphishing to deliver a payload or redirect to an external site to capture credentials through [Input Capture](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1056) on sites that mimic login interfaces.\n\nAdversaries may also leverage internal chat apps, such as Microsoft Teams, to spread malicious content or engage users in attempts to capture sensitive information and/or credentials.(Citation: Int SP - chat apps)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.643361133281065,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Internal Spearphishing, identified as MITRE ATT&CK T1534, is a Lateral Movement technique used on Windows, Linux, macOS, and SaaS platforms. It involves adversaries using a previously compromised internal account to launch spearphishing campaigns against other users within the same organization, leveraging the trusted position to increase success. This multi-stage attack often employs Spearphishing Attachments or Links to deliver malware or credential-harvesting sites and can utilize internal communication tools like Microsoft Teams to distribute malicious content."
    },
    "20505": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "exfiltration"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "0e686b0e-6fa2-4260-947c-a013649626f6",
        "aliases": [
            "Scheduled Transfer"
        ],
        "name": "Scheduled Transfer",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may schedule data exfiltration to be performed only at certain times of day or at certain intervals. This could be done to blend traffic patterns with normal activity or availability.\n\nWhen scheduled exfiltration is used, other exfiltration techniques likely apply as well to transfer the information out of the network, such as [Exfiltration Over C2 Channel](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1041) or [Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1048).",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1029",
        "standard_attck_name": "Scheduled Transfer",
        "gid": 4391,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "higaisa"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1029",
        "uncertain_name": "scheduled transfer",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may schedule data exfiltration to be performed only at certain times of day or at certain intervals. This could be done to blend traffic patterns with normal activity or availability.\n\nWhen scheduled exfiltration is used, other exfiltration techniques likely apply as well to transfer the information out of the network, such as [Exfiltration Over C2 Channel](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1041) or [Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1048).",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 15.43214962823117,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Scheduled Transfer is a data exfiltration technique (ID: T1029) within the Exfiltration tactic. It operates on Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms by scheduling data transfers to occur at specific times or intervals, such as certain times of day, to blend traffic patterns with normal network activity. This technique is typically used in conjunction with other exfiltration methods like Exfiltration Over C2 Channel or Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol to actually move the data."
    },
