    "20546": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "2b486f6a-6506-4e50-bfcc-db38822b63be",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "EDRSandBlast",
        "is_family": false,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.621503373172831,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]EDRSandBlast, also known as EDRKillShifter, is a tool attributed to APT29 (SVR) and Cicada3301. Its key technical behavior is exploiting signed, vulnerable drivers to disable security protections, specifically removing Protected Process Light (PPL) protection and disabling Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) and Local Security Authority Subsystem Service (LSASS) protections using kernel and user-level evasion techniques. It is used to inject code into antivirus and EDR processes, primarily targeting Windows systems.[/profile]"
    },
    "20547": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "3b41ed91-dddd-4bab-8852-e041e2d026f7",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "TCESB",
        "is_family": false,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.621408774710123,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]TCESB is a malware entity that employs the Exploitation for Defense Evasion (T1211) and Hijack Execution Flow (T1574) techniques to evade security mechanisms and establish persistence. It is associated with the Attor spyware campaign, which has targeted Russian diplomats and government agencies. The malware creates a registry key to ensure automatic execution on system startup."
    },
    "20548": {
        "created_time": "2025-08-14T07:17:38.661Z",
        "semantic": 1,
        "aka_name": [],
        "entity_type": "intrusion-set",
        "unique_id": "8c5ab161-26c5-4a40-bffa-633d9ee60386",
        "modified_time": "2025-08-21T08:58:58.776Z",
        "group_name": "ToddyCat",
        "name": "unkown_apt_group",
        "description": "",
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [
            "370d3b2ac96306a83cc49f1c5929a0badbeb2459d966046d88bc38709fb0245f",
            "ff7c79649da193cf16f5100a4b924b1e28ea684a5012a229ed65ca4900613bd7",
            "a1a26778656c5215ab1d939ebd9088827f69923ecf53a503b04bb504fdb97752"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 0.0,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]This unknown APT group is likely associated with or overlaps with the cluster tracked as Backdoordiplomacy (also known as Backdip, Quarian, or Cloudcomputating). Its key malware includes WAExp, TomBerBil, and Cuthead, which are notable for using the 'Archive via Library' technique (T1560.002) to compress data and for stealing credentials from web browsers (T1555.003). The group's infrastructure involves domains such as 1.tcp.ap.ngrok.io, ha.bbmouseme.com, www.netportal.or.kr, and etracking.nso.go.th. Its TTPs focus on credential access and data archiving for exfiltration, with tools also showing capability for lateral movement via remote services (T1021). While a definitive origin is not specified, its activities and tooling suggest a focus on cyber-espionage.[/profile]"
    },
    "20549": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "e88ce5f0-a126-41e1-88e5-594a4abd2ceb",
        "entity_type": "indicator",
        "valid_until": "2025-08-05T16:09:20.000Z",
        "name": "www.netportal.or.kr",
        "valid_from": "2024-04-22T16:09:20.000Z",
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 6.799976558321452
    },
    "20550": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "cf38ef0e-4659-4f3f-9197-ef99f74ed7b5",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "TomBerBil",
        "is_family": false,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [
            "ff7c79649da193cf16f5100a4b924b1e28ea684a5012a229ed65ca4900613bd7",
            "a1a26778656c5215ab1d939ebd9088827f69923ecf53a503b04bb504fdb97752",
            "370d3b2ac96306a83cc49f1c5929a0badbeb2459d966046d88bc38709fb0245f"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.660125323955093,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]TomBerBil is a malware associated with the criminal group TWISTED SPIDER. It uses the credential-access technique T1555.003 to steal credentials from web browsers and employs the defense evasion technique T1036.005 by matching legitimate resource names or locations. For data collection and exfiltration, it utilizes archiving techniques T1560 and T1560.002 (Archive via Library) to compress or encrypt data. It leverages remote services (T1021) for lateral movement. Its known command-and-control infrastructure includes domains like etracking.nso.go.th, ha.bbmouseme.com, 1.tcp.ap.ngrok.io, and www.netportal.or.kr.[/profile]"
    },
    "20551": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "886ac2ca-e153-43fa-9fd4-f5d2200727ea",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "cuthead",
        "is_family": false,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [
            "370d3b2ac96306a83cc49f1c5929a0badbeb2459d966046d88bc38709fb0245f",
            "ff7c79649da193cf16f5100a4b924b1e28ea684a5012a229ed65ca4900613bd7",
            "a1a26778656c5215ab1d939ebd9088827f69923ecf53a503b04bb504fdb97752"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.65962093287683,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]CutHead is a malware that uses the T1560.002 technique to archive collected data via libraries like Python's rarfile, libzip, or zlib for exfiltration. It employs T1555.003 to extract credentials from web browsers and T1555 to harvest credentials from password stores. For lateral movement, it utilizes T1021 Remote Services, such as SSH and RDP. Associated infrastructure indicators include domains like www.netportal.or.kr, etracking.nso.go.th, 1.tcp.ap.ngrok.io, and ha.bbmouseme.com. Its target platforms are Windows, Linux, and macOS.[/profile]"
    },
    "20552": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "bea3004c-f35e-4c56-bec0-28c6ecf036a6",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "WAExp",
        "is_family": false,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [
            "370d3b2ac96306a83cc49f1c5929a0badbeb2459d966046d88bc38709fb0245f",
            "ff7c79649da193cf16f5100a4b924b1e28ea684a5012a229ed65ca4900613bd7",
            "a1a26778656c5215ab1d939ebd9088827f69923ecf53a503b04bb504fdb97752"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.660247070449365,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]WAExp is a malware that uses the 'Archive via Library' technique (T1560.002) to compress or encrypt collected data prior to exfiltration by linking against third-party libraries. It also employs lateral movement via SMB/Windows Admin Shares (T1021.002) and Remote Services (T1021), and steals credentials from web browsers (T1555.003). Associated infrastructure includes domains like 1.tcp.ap.ngrok.io, ha.bbmouseme.com, www.netportal.or.kr, and etracking.nso.go.th.[/profile]"
    },
