            "3f9db7bf1c9d897d46f669854e7ecc945778024f04cac9cd1585140d0d73a34f",
            "a6b1de8184a7e560cea461b0e05d4136d0068b35c12c0889c4036d177e331a83",
            "ff2ae62ba88e7068fa142bbe67d7b9398e8ae737a43cf36ace1fcf809776c909",
            "2722e289767ae391e3c3773b8640a8b9f6eb24c6a9d6e541f29c8765f7a8944b",
            "165a80f6856487b3b4f41225ac60eed99c3d603f5a35febab8235757a273d1fd",
            "7e7292b5029882602fe31f15e25b5c59e01277abaab86b29843ded4aa0dcbdd1"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "muddywater"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "phonyc2",
        "uncertain_mes": "Welcome:\nLogin\n|\nSign Up\n|\nAbout CircleID\nFollow:\n|\n|\n|\n- August 24, 2023\n- Views: 18,275\n|\nCyber espionage group MuddyWater’s or Mercury’s first major campaign was seen as early as 2012. But as things always go in the cybersecurity realm, threat groups, especially those that gain infamy, don’t necessarily just come and go.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.38122505837158,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]PhonyC2 is a command and control (C2) framework attributed to the MuddyWater (also known as Mercury) cyber espionage group. It is similar to the group's previous MuddyC3 framework. Its technical behaviors include using encrypted channels for C2, employing data encoding to obfuscate traffic, and leveraging cloud accounts for infrastructure. It has been observed in campaigns targeting the United States of America, with identified server versions including \"apiv4\" and \"apiy7\"."
    },
