    "20984": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "initial-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Trusted Relationship"
        ],
        "unique_id": "dbbeea6f-43a4-47c4-a5e0-900393d800ad",
        "name": "T1199",
        "description": "Adversaries may breach or otherwise leverage organizations who have access to intended victims. Access through trusted third party relationship abuses an existing connection that may not be protected or receives less scrutiny than standard mechanisms of gaining access to a network.\n\nOrganizations often grant elevated access to second or third-party external providers in order to allow them to manage internal systems as well as cloud-based environments. Some examples of these relationships include IT services contractors, managed security providers, infrastructure contractors (e.g. HVAC, elevators, physical security). The third-party provider's access may be intended to be limited to the infrastructure being maintained, but may exist on the same network as the rest of the enterprise. As such, [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) used by the other party for access to internal network systems may be compromised and used.(Citation: CISA IT Service Providers)\n\nIn Office 365 environments, organizations may grant Microsoft partners or resellers delegated administrator permissions. By compromising a partner or reseller account, an adversary may be able to leverage existing delegated administrator relationships or send new delegated administrator offers to clients in order to gain administrative control over the victim tenant.(Citation: Office 365 Delegated Administration)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "IaaS",
            "Office Suite",
            "Identity Provider",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1199",
        "standard_attck_name": "Trusted Relationship",
        "gid": 4871,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "hafnium",
            "polonium",
            "sea turtle",
            "gold southfield",
            "redcurl",
            "sandworm team",
            "lapsus$",
            "menupass",
            "apt28",
            "threat group-3390",
            "apt29"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1199",
        "uncertain_name": "trusted relationship",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may breach or otherwise leverage organizations who have access to intended victims. Access through trusted third party relationship abuses an existing connection that may not be protected or receives less scrutiny than standard mechanisms of gaining access to a network.\n\nOrganizations often grant elevated access to second or third-party external providers in order to allow them to manage internal systems as well as cloud-based environments. Some examples of these relationships include IT services contractors, managed security providers, infrastructure contractors (e.g. HVAC, elevators, physical security). The third-party provider's access may be intended to be limited to the infrastructure being maintained, but may exist on the same network as the rest of the enterprise. As such, [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) used by the other party for access to internal network systems may be compromised and used.(Citation: CISA IT Service Providers)\n\nIn Office 365 environments, organizations may grant Microsoft partners or resellers delegated administrator permissions. By compromising a partner or reseller account, an adversary may be able to leverage existing delegated administrator relationships or send new delegated administrator offers to clients in order to gain administrative control over the victim tenant.(Citation: Office 365 Delegated Administration)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 11.666418787479383,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity is the MITRE ATT&CK technique T1199 \"Trusted Relationship\", categorized under the Initial Access tactic. It operates across platforms including Windows, Linux, macOS, and cloud services like IaaS, SaaS, and Identity Providers. This technique involves an adversary compromising or abusing an organization that has existing, trusted access to a victim's network, such as IT service providers, managed security providers, or Microsoft partners with delegated administrator permissions in Office 365. Attackers leverage these relationships to gain access using the provider's potentially less-scrutinized credentials or administrative pathways, as seen in campaigns by groups like APT29, APT28, and Hafnium."
    },
    "20985": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "initial-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "e2b62ed8-eec3-4d01-b95a-6a2bc45a8882",
        "aliases": [
            "Trusted Relationship"
        ],
        "name": "Trusted Relationship",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may breach or otherwise leverage organizations who have access to intended victims. Access through trusted third party relationship abuses an existing connection that may not be protected or receives less scrutiny than standard mechanisms of gaining access to a network.\n\nOrganizations often grant elevated access to second or third-party external providers in order to allow them to manage internal systems as well as cloud-based environments. Some examples of these relationships include IT services contractors, managed security providers, infrastructure contractors (e.g. HVAC, elevators, physical security). The third-party provider's access may be intended to be limited to the infrastructure being maintained, but may exist on the same network as the rest of the enterprise. As such, [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) used by the other party for access to internal network systems may be compromised and used.(Citation: CISA IT Service Providers)\n\nIn Office 365 environments, organizations may grant Microsoft partners or resellers delegated administrator permissions. By compromising a partner or reseller account, an adversary may be able to leverage existing delegated administrator relationships or send new delegated administrator offers to clients in order to gain administrative control over the victim tenant.(Citation: Office 365 Delegated Administration)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "IaaS",
            "Office Suite",
            "Identity Provider",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1199",
        "standard_attck_name": "Trusted Relationship",
        "gid": 4872,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "hafnium",
            "polonium",
            "sea turtle",
            "gold southfield",
            "redcurl",
            "sandworm team",
            "lapsus$",
            "menupass",
            "apt28",
            "threat group-3390",
            "apt29"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1199",
        "uncertain_name": "trusted relationship",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may breach or otherwise leverage organizations who have access to intended victims. Access through trusted third party relationship abuses an existing connection that may not be protected or receives less scrutiny than standard mechanisms of gaining access to a network.\n\nOrganizations often grant elevated access to second or third-party external providers in order to allow them to manage internal systems as well as cloud-based environments. Some examples of these relationships include IT services contractors, managed security providers, infrastructure contractors (e.g. HVAC, elevators, physical security). The third-party provider's access may be intended to be limited to the infrastructure being maintained, but may exist on the same network as the rest of the enterprise. As such, [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078) used by the other party for access to internal network systems may be compromised and used.(Citation: CISA IT Service Providers)\n\nIn Office 365 environments, organizations may grant Microsoft partners or resellers delegated administrator permissions. By compromising a partner or reseller account, an adversary may be able to leverage existing delegated administrator relationships or send new delegated administrator offers to clients in order to gain administrative control over the victim tenant.(Citation: Office 365 Delegated Administration)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 10.596515958437495,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Trusted Relationship is MITRE ATT&CK technique T1199 under the Initial Access tactic, applicable across Windows, Linux, macOS, and cloud platforms like IaaS and SaaS. It involves adversaries compromising or abusing the access granted to third-party organizations—such as IT service providers, managed security firms, or Microsoft partners with delegated administrator permissions—to gain a foothold in a victim's network. This method exploits existing, often less-scrutinized connections, allowing attackers to leverage compromised valid accounts from these trusted entities for initial access."
    },
