    "21219": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "persistence",
            "privilege-escalation"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Event Triggered Execution"
        ],
        "unique_id": "7e00608e-11ce-4a96-bf45-193513003b55",
        "name": "T1546",
        "description": "Adversaries may establish persistence and/or elevate privileges using system mechanisms that trigger execution based on specific events. Various operating systems have means to monitor and subscribe to events such as logons or other user activity such as running specific applications/binaries. Cloud environments may also support various functions and services that monitor and can be invoked in response to specific cloud events.(Citation: Backdooring an AWS account)(Citation: Varonis Power Automate Data Exfiltration)(Citation: Microsoft DART Case Report 001)\n\nAdversaries may abuse these mechanisms as a means of maintaining persistent access to a victim via repeatedly executing malicious code. After gaining access to a victim system, adversaries may create/modify event triggers to point to malicious content that will be executed whenever the event trigger is invoked.(Citation: FireEye WMI 2015)(Citation: Malware Persistence on OS X)(Citation: amnesia malware)\n\nSince the execution can be proxied by an account with higher permissions, such as SYSTEM or service accounts, an adversary may be able to abuse these triggered execution mechanisms to escalate their privileges.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "IaaS",
            "Office Suite",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1546",
        "uncertain_name": "event triggered execution",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may establish persistence and/or elevate privileges using system mechanisms that trigger execution based on specific events. Various operating systems have means to monitor and subscribe to events such as logons or other user activity such as running specific applications/binaries. Cloud environments may also support various functions and services that monitor and can be invoked in response to specific cloud events.(Citation: Backdooring an AWS account)(Citation: Varonis Power Automate Data Exfiltration)(Citation: Microsoft DART Case Report 001)\n\nAdversaries may abuse these mechanisms as a means of maintaining persistent access to a victim via repeatedly executing malicious code. After gaining access to a victim system, adversaries may create/modify event triggers to point to malicious content that will be executed whenever the event trigger is invoked.(Citation: FireEye WMI 2015)(Citation: Malware Persistence on OS X)(Citation: amnesia malware)\n\nSince the execution can be proxied by an account with higher permissions, such as SYSTEM or service accounts, an adversary may be able to abuse these triggered execution mechanisms to escalate their privileges. ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.159896981574477,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Event Triggered Execution (T1546) is a persistence and privilege-escalation technique applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, IaaS, Office Suite, and SaaS platforms. Adversaries abuse system mechanisms that monitor and subscribe to specific events, such as logons or application execution, to establish persistence. After initial access, they create or modify event triggers to point to malicious content, which is then repeatedly executed whenever the trigger is invoked. Execution can be proxied by high-permission accounts like SYSTEM, enabling privilege escalation. This technique is foundational for sub-techniques like Unix Shell Configuration Modification (T1546.004).[/profile]"
    },
    "21220": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "persistence",
            "privilege-escalation"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "435f8f6a-0b30-4241-96c6-53637cc6ba31",
        "aliases": [
            "Event Triggered Execution"
        ],
        "name": "Event Triggered Execution",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may establish persistence and/or elevate privileges using system mechanisms that trigger execution based on specific events. Various operating systems have means to monitor and subscribe to events such as logons or other user activity such as running specific applications/binaries. Cloud environments may also support various functions and services that monitor and can be invoked in response to specific cloud events.(Citation: Backdooring an AWS account)(Citation: Varonis Power Automate Data Exfiltration)(Citation: Microsoft DART Case Report 001)\n\nAdversaries may abuse these mechanisms as a means of maintaining persistent access to a victim via repeatedly executing malicious code. After gaining access to a victim system, adversaries may create/modify event triggers to point to malicious content that will be executed whenever the event trigger is invoked.(Citation: FireEye WMI 2015)(Citation: Malware Persistence on OS X)(Citation: amnesia malware)\n\nSince the execution can be proxied by an account with higher permissions, such as SYSTEM or service accounts, an adversary may be able to abuse these triggered execution mechanisms to escalate their privileges.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "IaaS",
            "Office Suite",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1624",
        "standard_attck_name": "Event Triggered Execution",
        "gid": 5107,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1546",
        "uncertain_name": "event triggered execution",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may establish persistence and/or elevate privileges using system mechanisms that trigger execution based on specific events. Various operating systems have means to monitor and subscribe to events such as logons or other user activity such as running specific applications/binaries. Cloud environments may also support various functions and services that monitor and can be invoked in response to specific cloud events.(Citation: Backdooring an AWS account)(Citation: Varonis Power Automate Data Exfiltration)(Citation: Microsoft DART Case Report 001)\n\nAdversaries may abuse these mechanisms as a means of maintaining persistent access to a victim via repeatedly executing malicious code. After gaining access to a victim system, adversaries may create/modify event triggers to point to malicious content that will be executed whenever the event trigger is invoked.(Citation: FireEye WMI 2015)(Citation: Malware Persistence on OS X)(Citation: amnesia malware)\n\nSince the execution can be proxied by an account with higher permissions, such as SYSTEM or service accounts, an adversary may be able to abuse these triggered execution mechanisms to escalate their privileges. ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.2626798770413155,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Event Triggered Execution is identified as MITRE ATT&CK technique T1546, falling under the Persistence and Privilege Escalation tactics. It operates across Windows, Linux, macOS, IaaS, SaaS, and Office Suite platforms. This technique involves adversaries abusing system or cloud mechanisms that monitor and execute code in response to specific events—such as logons, application runs, or cloud service triggers—to maintain persistent access. Execution is often proxied through high-privilege accounts like SYSTEM or service accounts, enabling privilege escalation. A common sub-technique is Windows Management Instrumentation (WMI) Event Subscription (T1546.003), where malicious filters and consumers are installed to run code when subscribed events occur."
    },
