            "{'SHA-1': 'f5ba545d4a16836756989a3ab32f3f6c5d5ad8ff'}",
            "64.176.56.26",
            "ab65c08da16a45565dba930069b5fc5a56806a4c",
            "e5b99572581df7a5116511be3f03b9f1a90611235b8288d9f59141876adb1ef1",
            "68b72da59467b1bb477d0c1c5107cee8d9078e7e",
            "63e813b5bf94bdec9ce35c9d7311f76c3a35728d158ade0a6487fc99c73dcf31",
            "{'SHA-1': 'e3da9467d0c89a9312ea199ecc83cddf3607d8b1'}",
            "a347e1efbfca3722c9e8cc86eba3b288f7e4fae9d386f2a8969faffb125a74c5"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 0.0,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]This intrusion set is attributed to the APT group known as menuPass (also known as APT10). Its key malware includes the UPPERCUT backdoor, which employs Compile After Delivery (T1027.004) and Bypass User Account Control (T1548.002), and is associated with tools like ROAMINGMOUSE and ANELLDR. The group's TTPs involve Defense Evasion techniques such as Debugger Evasion, Double File Extension, Fileless Storage, and Dynamic API Resolution, as well as C2 obfuscation via Junk Data (T1001.001). It is believed to be of Chinese origin and has primarily targeted entities in Japan and Taiwan, with motivations likely aligned with cyber espionage.[/profile]"
    },
