            "1e54b2e6558e2c92df73da65cd90b462dcafa1e6dcc311336b1543c68d3e82bc",
            "03c84ae3bdd28341bdb9ef24918c3cad6c9ed27c768d351f23e6d37bf048f7a4",
            "d63060e61c98074c58926a6239185e8128fd0fbc2a45ccf60f3c831bb18ffc93",
            "d4960f3c7cc891ff2bafd0a080451e42e0a23ba4db54ae2d7d355497a3b3d81a"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "fin7",
            "carbanak"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "carbanak",
        "uncertain_mes": "[Carbanak](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0030) is a full-featured, remote backdoor used by a group of the same name ([Carbanak](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0008)). It is intended for espionage, data exfiltration, and providing remote access to infected machines. (Citation: Kaspersky Carbanak) (Citation: FireEye CARBANAK June 2017)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 7.007583928987866,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Carbanak, also known as Carberp, is a full-featured remote backdoor malware family used for espionage, data exfiltration, and remote access, primarily attributed to the criminal group FIN7 (also known as Carbanak or Anunak). Its key technical behaviors include credential harvesting via Web Portal Capture (T1056.003) on externally facing portals like VPN login pages, and modifying Windows File and Directory Permissions (T1222.001) using commands like `icacls` to evade access controls. It targets Windows platforms and has utilized infrastructure domains such as myscannappo.info, ipscanneronline.com, and theipscanner.com for command and control. The malware is staged via techniques like Link Target (T1608.005) on typosquatted domains and is associated with downloaders like Bateleur and Tinymet.[/profile]"
    },
