        "uncertain_id": "t1222.001",
        "uncertain_name": "windows file and directory permissions modification",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may modify file or directory permissions/attributes to evade access control lists (ACLs) and access protected files.(Citation: Hybrid Analysis Icacls1 June 2018)(Citation: Hybrid Analysis Icacls2 May 2018) File and directory permissions are commonly managed by ACLs configured by the file or directory owner, or users with the appropriate permissions. File and directory ACL implementations vary by platform, but generally explicitly designate which users or groups can perform which actions (read, write, execute, etc.).\n\nWindows implements file and directory ACLs as Discretionary Access Control Lists (DACLs).(Citation: Microsoft DACL May 2018) Similar to a standard ACL, DACLs identifies the accounts that are allowed or denied access to a securable object. When an attempt is made to access a securable object, the system checks the access control entries in the DACL in order. If a matching entry is found, access to the object is granted. Otherwise, access is denied.(Citation: Microsoft Access Control Lists May 2018)\n\nAdversaries can interact with the DACLs using built-in Windows commands, such as `icacls`, `cacls`, `takeown`, and `attrib`, which can grant adversaries higher permissions on specific files and folders. Further, [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001) provides cmdlets that can be used to retrieve or modify file and directory DACLs. Specific file and directory modifications may be a required step for many techniques, such as establishing Persistence via [Accessibility Features](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1546/008), [Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1037), or tainting/hijacking other instrumental binary/configuration files via [Hijack Execution Flow](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1574).",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 14.335998044576836,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Windows File and Directory Permissions Modification, identified as T1222.001, is a Defense Evasion technique specific to the Windows platform. It involves adversaries modifying Discretionary Access Control Lists (DACLs) to evade access controls and access protected files. This is commonly executed using built-in Windows commands like `icacls`, `cacls`, `takeown`, and `attrib`, or via PowerShell cmdlets, to grant higher permissions on specific files and folders, often as a prerequisite step for establishing persistence or hijacking execution flow.[/profile]"
    },
    "22361": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "discovery"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Domain Groups"
        ],
        "unique_id": "d0c00e8d-849a-48a8-9113-d3acf57db606",
        "name": "T1069.002",
        "description": "Adversaries may attempt to find domain-level groups and permission settings. The knowledge of domain-level permission groups can help adversaries determine which groups exist and which users belong to a particular group. Adversaries may use this information to determine which users have elevated permissions, such as domain administrators.\n\nCommands such as `net group /domain` of the [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039) utility,  `dscacheutil -q group` on macOS, and `ldapsearch` on Linux can list domain-level groups.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1069.002",
        "standard_attck_name": "Domain Groups",
        "gid": 6251,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "dragonfly",
            "fin7",
            "ke3chang",
            "inc ransom",
            "medusa group",
            "mustang panda",
            "inception",
            "scattered spider",
            "turla",
            "lapsus$",
            "toddycat",
            "oilrig",
            "volt typhoon"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1069.002",
        "uncertain_name": "domain groups",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may attempt to find domain-level groups and permission settings. The knowledge of domain-level permission groups can help adversaries determine which groups exist and which users belong to a particular group. Adversaries may use this information to determine which users have elevated permissions, such as domain administrators.\n\nCommands such as <code>net group /domain</code> of the [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039) utility,  <code>dscacheutil -q group</code> on macOS, and <code>ldapsearch</code> on Linux can list domain-level groups.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.469280568431184,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1069.002, also known as Domain Groups, is a discovery technique (T1069) within the MITRE ATT&CK framework applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves adversaries enumerating domain-level groups and their members to identify users with elevated permissions, such as domain administrators. Specific commands for this include `net group /domain` on Windows, `dscacheutil -q group` on macOS, and `ldapsearch` on Linux. This technique has been associated with groups including Dragonfly, FIN7, and Volt Typhoon."
    },
    "22362": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Disable or Modify System Firewall"
        ],
        "unique_id": "4b1549e0-7832-49d2-a093-1647adb2dbd2",
        "name": "T1562.004",
        "description": "Adversaries may disable or modify system firewalls in order to bypass controls limiting network usage. Changes could be disabling the entire mechanism as well as adding, deleting, or modifying particular rules. This can be done numerous ways depending on the operating system, including via command-line, editing Windows Registry keys, and Windows Control Panel.\n\nModifying or disabling a system firewall may enable adversary C2 communications, lateral movement, and/or data exfiltration that would otherwise not be allowed. For example, adversaries may add a new firewall rule for a well-known protocol (such as RDP) using a non-traditional and potentially less securitized port (i.e. [Non-Standard Port](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1571)).(Citation: change_rdp_port_conti)\n\nAdversaries may also modify host networking settings that indirectly manipulate system firewalls, such as interface bandwidth or network connection request thresholds.(Citation: Huntress BlackCat) Settings related to enabling abuse of various [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021) may also indirectly modify firewall rules.\n\nIn ESXi, firewall rules may be modified directly via the esxcli command line interface (e.g., via `esxcli network firewall set`) or via the vCenter user interface.(Citation: Trellix Rnasomhouse 2024)(Citation: Broadcom ESXi Firewall)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1562.004",
        "standard_attck_name": "Disable or Modify System Firewall",
        "gid": 6252,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "dragonfly",
            "apt38",
            "fin7",
            "lazarus group",
            "teamtnt",
            "kimsuky",
            "blackbyte",
            "moses staff",
            "medusa group",
            "magic hound",
            "velvet ant",
            "salt typhoon",
            "rocke",
            "oilrig",
            "toddycat",
            "carbanak",
            "unc3886"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1562.004",
        "uncertain_name": "disable or modify system firewall",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may disable or modify system firewalls in order to bypass controls limiting network usage. Changes could be disabling the entire mechanism as well as adding, deleting, or modifying particular rules. This can be done numerous ways depending on the operating system, including via command-line, editing Windows Registry keys, and Windows Control Panel.\n\nModifying or disabling a system firewall may enable adversary C2 communications, lateral movement, and/or data exfiltration that would otherwise not be allowed. For example, adversaries may add a new firewall rule for a well-known protocol (such as RDP) using a non-traditional and potentially less securitized port (i.e. [Non-Standard Port](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1571)).(Citation: change_rdp_port_conti)\n\nAdversaries may also modify host networking settings that indirectly manipulate system firewalls, such as interface bandwidth or network connection request thresholds.(Citation: Huntress BlackCat) Settings related to enabling abuse of various [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021) may also indirectly modify firewall rules.\n\nIn ESXi, firewall rules may be modified directly via the esxcli command line interface (e.g., via `esxcli network firewall set`) or via the vCenter user interface.(Citation: Trellix Rnasomhouse 2024)(Citation: Broadcom ESXi Firewall)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.20594052696033,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1562.004, known as Disable or Modify System Firewall, is a Defense Evasion technique applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, ESXi, and Network Devices. It involves adversaries disabling a firewall entirely or adding, deleting, and modifying specific rules to bypass network controls, which can enable command and control, lateral movement, and data exfiltration. Methods vary by OS and include command-line actions, editing Windows Registry keys, and using interfaces like the Windows Control Panel or the ESXi `esxcli network firewall` command. This technique is a sub-technique of T1562 (Impair Defenses) and has been used by groups including APT38, FIN7, and Lazarus Group.[/profile]"
    },
