        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1055.004",
        "uncertain_name": "asynchronous procedure call",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may inject malicious code into processes via the asynchronous procedure call (APC) queue in order to evade process-based defenses as well as possibly elevate privileges. APC injection is a method of executing arbitrary code in the address space of a separate live process. \n\nAPC injection is commonly performed by attaching malicious code to the APC Queue (Citation: Microsoft APC) of a process's thread. Queued APC functions are executed when the thread enters an alterable state.(Citation: Microsoft APC) A handle to an existing victim process is first created with native Windows API calls such as <code>OpenThread</code>. At this point <code>QueueUserAPC</code> can be used to invoke a function (such as <code>LoadLibrayA</code> pointing to a malicious DLL). \n\nA variation of APC injection, dubbed \"Early Bird injection\", involves creating a suspended process in which malicious code can be written and executed before the process' entry point (and potentially subsequent anti-malware hooks) via an APC. (Citation: CyberBit Early Bird Apr 2018) AtomBombing (Citation: ENSIL AtomBombing Oct 2016) is another variation that utilizes APCs to invoke malicious code previously written to the global atom table.(Citation: Microsoft Atom Table)\n\nRunning code in the context of another process may allow access to the process's memory, system/network resources, and possibly elevated privileges. Execution via APC injection may also evade detection from security products since the execution is masked under a legitimate process. ",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 14.526234720226995,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Asynchronous Procedure Call, identified as MITRE ATT&CK T1055.004, is a Windows-specific technique used for Defense Evasion and Privilege Escalation. It works by injecting malicious code into the APC queue of a target process's thread using API calls like OpenThread and QueueUserAPC, which executes when the thread enters an alterable state. Variations include Early Bird injection, which runs code in a suspended process before its entry point, and AtomBombing, which uses the global atom table. This execution within a legitimate process's context can evade detection and potentially grant elevated privileges or access to system resources."
    },
    "22722": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion",
            "discovery"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Time Based Checks",
            "Time Based Evasion"
        ],
        "unique_id": "91ba4afc-4ce1-47a5-9e21-d248ec4ea1dd",
        "name": "T1497.003",
        "description": "Adversaries may employ various time-based methods to detect virtualization and analysis environments, particularly those that attempt to manipulate time mechanisms to simulate longer elapses of time. This may include enumerating time-based properties, such as uptime or the system clock. \n\nAdversaries may use calls like `GetTickCount` and `GetSystemTimeAsFileTime` to discover if they are operating within a virtual machine or sandbox, or may be able to identify a sandbox accelerating time by sampling and calculating the expected value for an environment's timestamp before and after execution of a sleep function.(Citation: ISACA Malware Tricks)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1497.003",
        "standard_attck_name": "Time Based Checks",
        "gid": 6614,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1497.003",
        "uncertain_name": "time based checks",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may employ various time-based methods to detect virtualization and analysis environments, particularly those that attempt to manipulate time mechanisms to simulate longer elapses of time. This may include enumerating time-based properties, such as uptime or the system clock. \n\nAdversaries may use calls like `GetTickCount` and `GetSystemTimeAsFileTime` to discover if they are operating within a virtual machine or sandbox, or may be able to identify a sandbox accelerating time by sampling and calculating the expected value for an environment's timestamp before and after execution of a sleep function.(Citation: ISACA Malware Tricks)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 7.450817812604741,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1497.003, also known as Time Based Evasion, is a Defense Evasion and Discovery technique for Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves adversaries using time-based checks to detect virtualization or sandbox environments by sampling system timestamps, such as through calls like `GetTickCount` or `GetSystemTimeAsFileTime`, to identify discrepancies like accelerated time that indicate an analysis environment. It is a sub-technique of T1497.[/profile]"
    },
