    "22842": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "credential-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Golden Ticket"
        ],
        "unique_id": "cf7f0979-f098-4db5-b18c-d13312e048af",
        "name": "T1558.001",
        "description": "Adversaries who have the KRBTGT account password hash may forge Kerberos ticket-granting tickets (TGT), also known as a golden ticket.(Citation: AdSecurity Kerberos GT Aug 2015) Golden tickets enable adversaries to generate authentication material for any account in Active Directory.(Citation: CERT-EU Golden Ticket Protection) \n\nUsing a golden ticket, adversaries are then able to request ticket granting service (TGS) tickets, which enable access to specific resources. Golden tickets require adversaries to interact with the Key Distribution Center (KDC) in order to obtain TGS.(Citation: ADSecurity Detecting Forged Tickets)\n\nThe KDC service runs all on domain controllers that are part of an Active Directory domain. KRBTGT is the Kerberos Key Distribution Center (KDC) service account and is responsible for encrypting and signing all Kerberos tickets.(Citation: ADSecurity Kerberos and KRBTGT) The KRBTGT password hash may be obtained using [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003) and privileged access to a domain controller.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1558.001",
        "standard_attck_name": "Golden Ticket",
        "gid": 6734,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "ke3chang"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1558.001",
        "uncertain_name": "golden ticket",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries who have the KRBTGT account password hash may forge Kerberos ticket-granting tickets (TGT), also known as a golden ticket.(Citation: AdSecurity Kerberos GT Aug 2015) Golden tickets enable adversaries to generate authentication material for any account in Active Directory.(Citation: CERT-EU Golden Ticket Protection) \n\nUsing a golden ticket, adversaries are then able to request ticket granting service (TGS) tickets, which enable access to specific resources. Golden tickets require adversaries to interact with the Key Distribution Center (KDC) in order to obtain TGS.(Citation: ADSecurity Detecting Forged Tickets)\n\nThe KDC service runs all on domain controllers that are part of an Active Directory domain. KRBTGT is the Kerberos Key Distribution Center (KDC) service account and is responsible for encrypting and signing all Kerberos tickets.(Citation: ADSecurity Kerberos and KRBTGT) The KRBTGT password hash may be obtained using [OS Credential Dumping](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1003) and privileged access to a domain controller.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 15.432592235904114,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1558.001, also known as Golden Ticket, is a credential-access technique for Windows platforms. It involves forging Kerberos ticket-granting tickets (TGTs) using the stolen KRBTGT account password hash, which adversaries obtain via methods like OS Credential Dumping. This forged golden ticket enables the generation of authentication material for any Active Directory account, allowing subsequent requests for ticket-granting service (TGS) tickets to access specific resources by interacting with the domain controller's Key Distribution Center (KDC)."
    },
    "22843": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [],
        "unique_id": "17d6c3a6-33b2-4b4b-90c6-ab43f7fc19d1",
        "name": "T1038",
        "description": "",
        "kill_chain_names": [],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1038",
        "standard_attck_name": "DLL Search Order Hijacking",
        "gid": 6735,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1038",
        "uncertain_name": "dll search order hijacking",
        "uncertain_mes": "Windows systems use a common method to look for required DLLs to load into a program. (Citation: Microsoft DLL Search) Adversaries may take advantage of the Windows DLL search order and programs that ambiguously specify DLLs to gain privilege escalation and persistence. \n\nAdversaries may perform DLL preloading, also called binary planting attacks, (Citation: OWASP Binary Planting) by placing a malicious DLL with the same name as an ambiguously specified DLL in a location that Windows searches before the legitimate DLL. Often this location is the current working directory of the program. Remote DLL preloading attacks occur when a program sets its current directory to a remote location such as a Web share before loading a DLL. (Citation: Microsoft 2269637) Adversaries may use this behavior to cause the program to load a malicious DLL. \n\nAdversaries may also directly modify the way a program loads DLLs by replacing an existing DLL or modifying a .manifest or .local redirection file, directory, or junction to cause the program to load a different DLL to maintain persistence or privilege escalation. (Citation: Microsoft DLL Redirection) (Citation: Microsoft Manifests) (Citation: Mandiant Search Order)\n\nIf a search order-vulnerable program is configured to run at a higher privilege level, then the adversary-controlled DLL that is loaded will also be executed at the higher level. In this case, the technique could be used for privilege escalation from user to administrator or SYSTEM or from administrator to SYSTEM, depending on the program.\n\nPrograms that fall victim to path hijacking may appear to behave normally because malicious DLLs may be configured to also load the legitimate DLLs they were meant to replace.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.378896741664954,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1038, known as DLL Search Order Hijacking, is a Privilege Escalation and Persistence technique for Windows platforms. It exploits the standard Windows DLL search order by placing a malicious DLL with the same name as a legitimate, ambiguously referenced DLL in a directory that is searched earlier, such as the program's current working directory. This causes the vulnerable application to load and execute the adversary's code. The technique can also be achieved by modifying .manifest or .local redirection files or directory junctions to redirect loads. If the vulnerable program runs with elevated privileges, the malicious DLL executes at that higher level."
    },
    "22844": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "reconnaissance"
        ],
        "aliases": [
            "Gather Victim Network Information"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "49f2a65a-f9b3-4853-a97c-55eaec68bb06",
        "name": "Gather Victim Network Information",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's networks that can be used during targeting. Information about networks may include a variety of details, including administrative data (ex: IP ranges, domain names, etc.) as well as specifics regarding its topology and operations.\n\nAdversaries may gather this information in various ways, such as direct collection actions via [Active Scanning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1595) or [Phishing for Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598). Information about networks may also be exposed to adversaries via online or other accessible data sets (ex: [Search Open Technical Databases](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1596)).(Citation: WHOIS)(Citation: DNS Dumpster)(Citation: Circl Passive DNS) Gathering this information may reveal opportunities for other forms of reconnaissance (ex: [Active Scanning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1595) or [Search Open Websites/Domains](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1593)), establishing operational resources (ex: [Acquire Infrastructure](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583) or [Compromise Infrastructure](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584)), and/or initial access (ex: [Trusted Relationship](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1199)).",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "PRE"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1590",
        "standard_attck_name": "Gather Victim Network Information",
        "gid": 6736,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "hafnium",
            "indrik spider",
            "volt typhoon"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1590",
        "uncertain_name": "gather victim network information",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may gather information about the victim's networks that can be used during targeting. Information about networks may include a variety of details, including administrative data (ex: IP ranges, domain names, etc.) as well as specifics regarding its topology and operations.\n\nAdversaries may gather this information in various ways, such as direct collection actions via [Active Scanning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1595) or [Phishing for Information](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598). Information about networks may also be exposed to adversaries via online or other accessible data sets (ex: [Search Open Technical Databases](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1596)).(Citation: WHOIS)(Citation: DNS Dumpster)(Citation: Circl Passive DNS) Gathering this information may reveal opportunities for other forms of reconnaissance (ex: [Active Scanning](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1595) or [Search Open Websites/Domains](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1593)), establishing operational resources (ex: [Acquire Infrastructure](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583) or [Compromise Infrastructure](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584)), and/or initial access (ex: [Trusted Relationship](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1199)).",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.967965030996229,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Gather Victim Network Information is technique T1590 under the Reconnaissance tactic, applicable to the PRE platform. It involves collecting details about a victim's administrative data like IP ranges and domain names, as well as network topology and operations. This information is gathered through methods such as Active Scanning, Phishing for Information, or querying open technical databases like WHOIS and DNS Dumpster, which can reveal opportunities for further reconnaissance or initial access."
    },
