            "fe2046e479289b1013eb394f5b3d7a49a419cb98015add3ead0fa87614fe6e38"
        ],
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 0.0,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]This intrusion set is most likely the APT group known as Lotus Blossom, also referred to as Lotus Blossom. It is associated with the Evora malware, which uses C2 servers such as jf.doyourbestyet.com, zg.poorgoddaay.com, and davaotour.net. Key techniques include Data Encoding for C2 obfuscation, Impair Defenses, Account Discovery, User Execution via phishing, and Software Discovery. The group has targeted entities in Hong Kong, the Philippines, and Taiwan, and its malware has exploited vulnerabilities like CVE-2024-4577. Its motivation appears to be cyber espionage, and it shows potential links to other clusters like BackdoorDiplomacy.[/profile]"
    },
    "24063": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "persistence"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "5d1eb622-fd67-436b-9c92-bd7f0f83d2e5",
        "aliases": [
            "Server Software Component"
        ],
        "name": "Server Software Component",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may abuse legitimate extensible development features of servers to establish persistent access to systems. Enterprise server applications may include features that allow developers to write and install software or scripts to extend the functionality of the main application. Adversaries may install malicious components to extend and abuse server applications.(Citation: volexity_0day_sophos_FW)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1505",
        "standard_attck_name": "Server Software Component",
        "gid": 7955,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1505",
        "uncertain_name": "server software component",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may abuse legitimate extensible development features of servers to establish persistent access to systems. Enterprise server applications may include features that allow developers to write and install software or scripts to extend the functionality of the main application. Adversaries may install malicious components to extend and abuse server applications.(Citation: volexity_0day_sophos_FW)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.844187086458591,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Server Software Component, identified as T1505, is a Persistence technique targeting platforms including Windows, Linux, macOS, Network Devices, and ESXi. Adversaries abuse legitimate extensible development features of enterprise server applications by installing malicious software components or scripts to extend and abuse the server's functionality, thereby establishing persistent access to the compromised system."
    },
    "24064": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion",
            "lateral-movement"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Use Alternate Authentication Material"
        ],
        "unique_id": "3d1cbc00-28a1-4626-af6f-8e551ee3df49",
        "name": "Use Alternate Authentication Material",
        "description": "Adversaries may use alternate authentication material, such as password hashes, Kerberos tickets, and application access tokens, in order to move laterally within an environment and bypass normal system access controls. \n\nAuthentication processes generally require a valid identity (e.g., username) along with one or more authentication factors (e.g., password, pin, physical smart card, token generator, etc.). Alternate authentication material is legitimately generated by systems after a user or application successfully authenticates by providing a valid identity and the required authentication factor(s). Alternate authentication material may also be generated during the identity creation process.(Citation: NIST Authentication)(Citation: NIST MFA)\n\nCaching alternate authentication material allows the system to verify an identity has successfully authenticated without asking the user to reenter authentication factor(s). Because the alternate authentication must be maintained by the system—either in memory or on disk—it may be at risk of being stolen through [Credential Access](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0006) techniques. By stealing alternate authentication material, adversaries are able to bypass system access controls and authenticate to systems without knowing the plaintext password or any additional authentication factors.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Containers",
            "IaaS",
            "Office Suite",
            "Identity Provider",
            "SaaS"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1550",
        "standard_attck_name": "Use Alternate Authentication Material",
        "gid": 7956,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1550",
        "uncertain_name": "use alternate authentication material",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use alternate authentication material, such as password hashes, Kerberos tickets, and application access tokens, in order to move laterally within an environment and bypass normal system access controls. \n\nAuthentication processes generally require a valid identity (e.g., username) along with one or more authentication factors (e.g., password, pin, physical smart card, token generator, etc.). Alternate authentication material is legitimately generated by systems after a user or application successfully authenticates by providing a valid identity and the required authentication factor(s). Alternate authentication material may also be generated during the identity creation process.(Citation: NIST Authentication)(Citation: NIST MFA)\n\nCaching alternate authentication material allows the system to verify an identity has successfully authenticated without asking the user to reenter authentication factor(s). Because the alternate authentication must be maintained by the system—either in memory or on disk—it may be at risk of being stolen through [Credential Access](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0006) techniques. By stealing alternate authentication material, adversaries are able to bypass system access controls and authenticate to systems without knowing the plaintext password or any additional authentication factors.\n",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.065134184122299,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Use Alternate Authentication Material, identified as T1550, is a defense evasion and lateral movement technique applicable to Windows, Linux, and cloud platforms. Adversaries use stolen or forged authentication material like password hashes, Kerberos tickets, or application tokens to authenticate to systems without needing plaintext credentials, thereby bypassing normal access controls and multi-factor authentication. This material is cached by systems after legitimate logons and can be acquired through credential access techniques."
    },
