        "uncertain_name": "cobalt strike",
        "uncertain_mes": "[Cobalt Strike](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0154) is a commercial, full-featured, remote access tool that bills itself as “adversary simulation software designed to execute targeted attacks and emulate the post-exploitation actions of advanced threat actors”. Cobalt Strike’s interactive post-exploit capabilities cover the full range of ATT&CK tactics, all executed within a single, integrated system.(Citation: cobaltstrike manual)\n\nIn addition to its own capabilities, [Cobalt Strike](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0154) leverages the capabilities of other well-known tools such as Metasploit and [Mimikatz](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0002).(Citation: cobaltstrike manual)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 9.555260760480476,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Cobalt Strike - S0154, also known as cobalt strike, is a commercial remote access tool used for adversary simulation and post-exploitation activities, associated with numerous threat groups including APT37, Mustard Tempest, FIN6, Storm-1811, Indrik Spider, Mustang Panda, CopyKittens, Cobalt Group, Cinnamon Tempest, APT19, TA505, BlackByte, PLAY, DarkHydrus, APT41, Wizard Spider, Threat Group-3390, ToddyCat, Storm-0501, APT29, Earth Lusca, Chimera, FIN7, Leviathan, APT32, Sandworm Team, LuminousMoth, MenuPass, and Aquatic Panda. Its key technical behaviors include One-Way Communication for command-and-control, Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism for privilege escalation, Traffic Signaling for triggering hidden responses, Data Encoding (T1132) for obfuscating C2 traffic, and Boot or Logon Autostart Execution for persistence. It targets multiple platforms including Windows, Linux, macOS, and ESXi, and utilizes infrastructure such as C2 servers at domains like taskandpurpose.icu, kitchengardenseeds.icu, and sweetgeorgiayarns.online.[/profile]"
    },
