        "unique_id": "3712300f-48e2-4294-aa2b-3729738528f6",
        "name": "Shell History",
        "description": "Adversaries may search the command history on compromised systems for insecurely stored credentials.\n\nOn Linux and macOS systems, shells such as Bash and Zsh keep track of the commands users type on the command-line with the \"history\" utility. Once a user logs out, the history is flushed to the user's history file. For each user, this file resides at the same location: for example, `~/.bash_history` or `~/.zsh_history`. Typically, these files keeps track of the user's last 1000 commands.\n\nOn Windows, PowerShell has both a command history that is wiped after the session ends, and one that contains commands used in all sessions and is persistent. The default location for persistent history can be found in `%userprofile%\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\PowerShell\\PSReadline\\ConsoleHost_history.txt`, but command history can also be accessed with `Get-History`. Command Prompt (CMD) on Windows does not have persistent history.(Citation: Microsoft about_History)(Citation: Medium)\n\nUsers often type usernames and passwords on the command-line as parameters to programs, which then get saved to this file when they log out. Adversaries can abuse this by looking through the file for potential credentials.(Citation: External to DA, the OS X Way)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1552.003",
        "standard_attck_name": "Shell History",
        "gid": 8139,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1552.003",
        "uncertain_name": "shell history",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may search the command history on compromised systems for insecurely stored credentials.\n\nOn Linux and macOS systems, shells such as Bash and Zsh keep track of the commands users type on the command-line with the \"history\" utility. Once a user logs out, the history is flushed to the user's history file. For each user, this file resides at the same location: for example, `~/.bash_history` or `~/.zsh_history`. Typically, these files keeps track of the user's last 1000 commands.\n\nOn Windows, PowerShell has both a command history that is wiped after the session ends, and one that contains commands used in all sessions and is persistent. The default location for persistent history can be found in `%userprofile%\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\PowerShell\\PSReadline\\ConsoleHost_history.txt`, but command history can also be accessed with `Get-History`. Command Prompt (CMD) on Windows does not have persistent history.(Citation: Microsoft about_History)(Citation: Medium)\n\nUsers often type usernames and passwords on the command-line as parameters to programs, which then get saved to this file when they log out. Adversaries can abuse this by looking through the file for potential credentials.(Citation: External to DA, the OS X Way)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 6.756184821259857,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Shell History is a credential access technique where adversaries search shell command history files for insecurely stored credentials. On Linux and macOS, shells like Bash and Zsh flush command history to user-specific files such as `~/.bash_history` or `~/.zsh_history` upon logout, typically retaining the last 1000 commands. On Windows, persistent PowerShell command history is stored by default in `%userprofile%\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\PowerShell\\PSReadline\\ConsoleHost_history.txt` and can be accessed via `Get-History`. Adversaries abuse this by parsing these files to find credentials users may have typed as command-line parameters.[/profile]"
    },
    "24248": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "defense-evasion"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Indicator Removal from Tools"
        ],
        "unique_id": "f5b88786-fc1b-4fd5-bc49-2e49d3db63b7",
        "name": "T1027.005",
        "description": "Adversaries may remove indicators from tools if they believe their malicious tool was detected, quarantined, or otherwise curtailed. They can modify the tool by removing the indicator and using the updated version that is no longer detected by the target's defensive systems or subsequent targets that may use similar systems.\n\nA good example of this is when malware is detected with a file signature and quarantined by anti-virus software. An adversary who can determine that the malware was quarantined because of its file signature may modify the file to explicitly avoid that signature, and then re-use the malware.",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1027.005",
        "standard_attck_name": "Indicator Removal from Tools",
        "gid": 8140,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "patchwork",
            "apt3",
            "deep panda",
            "gallium",
            "turla",
            "oilrig",
            "unc3886"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1027.005",
        "uncertain_name": "indicator removal from tools",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may remove indicators from tools if they believe their malicious tool was detected, quarantined, or otherwise curtailed. They can modify the tool by removing the indicator and using the updated version that is no longer detected by the target's defensive systems or subsequent targets that may use similar systems.\n\nA good example of this is when malware is detected with a file signature and quarantined by anti-virus software. An adversary who can determine that the malware was quarantined because of its file signature may modify the file to explicitly avoid that signature, and then re-use the malware.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 14.181069007421973,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1027.005, named Indicator Removal from Tools, is a Defense Evasion technique applicable to Windows, Linux, and macOS. It involves adversaries modifying their malicious tools to remove specific forensic or detection indicators, such as file signatures, after those tools have been detected or quarantined by security systems. This allows the updated, \"cleaner\" version of the tool to evade the same defenses upon reuse. The technique is associated with threat groups including Patchwork, APT3, and OilRig."
    },
    "24249": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "persistence"
        ],
        "aliases": [
            "Compromise Host Software Binary"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "1a86a135-2be1-42b5-bbbb-80a192e219e5",
        "name": "Compromise Host Software Binary",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may modify host software binaries to establish persistent access to systems. Software binaries/executables provide a wide range of system commands or services, programs, and libraries. Common software binaries are SSH clients, FTP clients, email clients, web browsers, and many other user or server applications.\n\nAdversaries may establish persistence though modifications to host software binaries. For example, an adversary may replace or otherwise infect a legitimate application binary (or support files) with a backdoor. Since these binaries may be routinely executed by applications or the user, the adversary can leverage this for persistent access to the host. An adversary may also modify a software binary such as an SSH client in order to persistently collect credentials during logins (i.e., [Modify Authentication Process](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556)).(Citation: Google Cloud Mandiant UNC3886 2024)\n\nAn adversary may also modify an existing binary by patching in malicious functionality (e.g., IAT Hooking/Entry point patching)(Citation: Unit42 Banking Trojans Hooking 2022) prior to the binary’s legitimate execution. For example, an adversary may modify the entry point of a binary to point to malicious code patched in by the adversary before resuming normal execution flow.(Citation: ESET FontOnLake Analysis 2021)\n\nAfter modifying a binary, an adversary may attempt to [Impair Defenses](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562) by preventing it from updating (e.g., via the `yum-versionlock` command or `versionlock.list` file in Linux systems that use the yum package manager).(Citation: Google Cloud Mandiant UNC3886 2024)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1554",
        "standard_attck_name": "Compromise Host Software Binary",
        "gid": 8141,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "unc3886",
            "apt5"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1554",
        "uncertain_name": "compromise host software binary",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may modify host software binaries to establish persistent access to systems. Software binaries/executables provide a wide range of system commands or services, programs, and libraries. Common software binaries are SSH clients, FTP clients, email clients, web browsers, and many other user or server applications.\n\nAdversaries may establish persistence though modifications to host software binaries. For example, an adversary may replace or otherwise infect a legitimate application binary (or support files) with a backdoor. Since these binaries may be routinely executed by applications or the user, the adversary can leverage this for persistent access to the host. An adversary may also modify a software binary such as an SSH client in order to persistently collect credentials during logins (i.e., [Modify Authentication Process](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1556)).(Citation: Google Cloud Mandiant UNC3886 2024)\n\nAn adversary may also modify an existing binary by patching in malicious functionality (e.g., IAT Hooking/Entry point patching)(Citation: Unit42 Banking Trojans Hooking 2022) prior to the binary’s legitimate execution. For example, an adversary may modify the entry point of a binary to point to malicious code patched in by the adversary before resuming normal execution flow.(Citation: ESET FontOnLake Analysis 2021)\n\nAfter modifying a binary, an adversary may attempt to [Impair Defenses](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562) by preventing it from updating (e.g., via the `yum-versionlock` command or `versionlock.list` file in Linux systems that use the yum package manager).(Citation: Google Cloud Mandiant UNC3886 2024)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 14.739465977792426,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Compromise Host Software Binary, also known as T1554, is a Persistence tactic used on Windows, Linux, macOS, and ESXi platforms. It involves modifying legitimate host binaries like SSH or FTP clients to establish persistent access, such as by replacing or patching them with backdoors or credential-stealing code via methods like IAT hooking or entry-point patching. Adversaries may subsequently use commands like `yum-versionlock` to prevent the compromised binary from updating, thereby impairing defenses. This technique has been associated with threat groups like UNC3886 and APT5."
    },
    "24250": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "credential-access",
            "defense-evasion",
            "persistence"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "7c47fe62-b721-4426-84d6-0e30ee251b69",
        "aliases": [
            "Password Filter DLL"
        ],
        "name": "Password Filter DLL",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may register malicious password filter dynamic link libraries (DLLs) into the authentication process to acquire user credentials as they are validated. \n\nWindows password filters are password policy enforcement mechanisms for both domain and local accounts. Filters are implemented as DLLs containing a method to validate potential passwords against password policies. Filter DLLs can be positioned on local computers for local accounts and/or domain controllers for domain accounts. Before registering new passwords in the Security Accounts Manager (SAM), the Local Security Authority (LSA) requests validation from each registered filter. Any potential changes cannot take effect until every registered filter acknowledges validation. \n\nAdversaries can register malicious password filters to harvest credentials from local computers and/or entire domains. To perform proper validation, filters must receive plain-text credentials from the LSA. A malicious password filter would receive these plain-text credentials every time a password request is made.(Citation: Carnal Ownage Password Filters Sept 2013)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1174",
        "standard_attck_name": "Password Filter DLL",
        "gid": 8142,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "oilrig",
            "strider"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1556.002",
        "uncertain_name": "password filter dll",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may register malicious password filter dynamic link libraries (DLLs) into the authentication process to acquire user credentials as they are validated. \n\nWindows password filters are password policy enforcement mechanisms for both domain and local accounts. Filters are implemented as DLLs containing a method to validate potential passwords against password policies. Filter DLLs can be positioned on local computers for local accounts and/or domain controllers for domain accounts. Before registering new passwords in the Security Accounts Manager (SAM), the Local Security Authority (LSA) requests validation from each registered filter. Any potential changes cannot take effect until every registered filter acknowledges validation. \n\nAdversaries can register malicious password filters to harvest credentials from local computers and/or entire domains. To perform proper validation, filters must receive plain-text credentials from the LSA. A malicious password filter would receive these plain-text credentials every time a password request is made.(Citation: Carnal Ownage Password Filters Sept 2013)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.362620505870467,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Password Filter DLL, identified as T1556.002, is a Credential Access, Defense Evasion, and Persistence technique for Windows. It involves adversaries registering a malicious dynamic link library (DLL) as a password filter with the Local Security Authority (LSA). This malicious DLL receives plain-text user credentials from the LSA for validation during every password change or set request, enabling credential harvesting from local systems or entire domains. Threat groups like OilRig and Strider have been associated with this method."
    },
    "24251": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "credential-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets"
        ],
        "unique_id": "24ec0c29-8171-4e29-8ae5-0dd656144aa4",
        "name": "T1558",
        "description": "Adversaries may attempt to subvert Kerberos authentication by stealing or forging Kerberos tickets to enable [Pass the Ticket](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1550/003). Kerberos is an authentication protocol widely used in modern Windows domain environments. In Kerberos environments, referred to as “realms”, there are three basic participants: client, service, and Key Distribution Center (KDC).(Citation: ADSecurity Kerberos Ring Decoder) Clients request access to a service and through the exchange of Kerberos tickets, originating from KDC, they are granted access after having successfully authenticated. The KDC is responsible for both authentication and ticket granting.  Adversaries may attempt to abuse Kerberos by stealing tickets or forging tickets to enable unauthorized access.\n\nOn Windows, the built-in `klist` utility can be used to list and analyze cached Kerberos tickets.(Citation: Microsoft Klist)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1558",
        "standard_attck_name": "Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets",
        "gid": 8143,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "akira"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1558",
        "uncertain_name": "steal or forge kerberos tickets",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may attempt to subvert Kerberos authentication by stealing or forging Kerberos tickets to enable [Pass the Ticket](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1550/003). Kerberos is an authentication protocol widely used in modern Windows domain environments. In Kerberos environments, referred to as “realms”, there are three basic participants: client, service, and Key Distribution Center (KDC).(Citation: ADSecurity Kerberos Ring Decoder) Clients request access to a service and through the exchange of Kerberos tickets, originating from KDC, they are granted access after having successfully authenticated. The KDC is responsible for both authentication and ticket granting.  Adversaries may attempt to abuse Kerberos by stealing tickets or forging tickets to enable unauthorized access.\n\nOn Windows, the built-in <code>klist</code> utility can be used to list and analyze cached Kerberos tickets.(Citation: Microsoft Klist)\n",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.50631409548949,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets (T1558) is a Credential Access technique targeting Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. It involves subverting Kerberos authentication by stealing or forging tickets, such as Ticket Granting Tickets (TGTs) or service tickets, to enable unauthorized access via Pass the Ticket. Specific sub-techniques include Kerberoasting (T1558.003) to obtain service account hashes and forging Silver Tickets (T1558.002) for specific resources or Golden Tickets for domain-wide access using the KRBTGT account hash. The built-in Windows `klist` utility can be used to analyze cached tickets.[/profile]"
    },
