    },
    "24254": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "credential-access"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "aliases": [
            "Shell History",
            "Bash History"
        ],
        "unique_id": "f41b2c9a-71b9-4b2a-ad17-f09cdde8547f",
        "name": "T1552.003",
        "description": "Adversaries may search the command history on compromised systems for insecurely stored credentials.\n\nOn Linux and macOS systems, shells such as Bash and Zsh keep track of the commands users type on the command-line with the \"history\" utility. Once a user logs out, the history is flushed to the user's history file. For each user, this file resides at the same location: for example, `~/.bash_history` or `~/.zsh_history`. Typically, these files keeps track of the user's last 1000 commands.\n\nOn Windows, PowerShell has both a command history that is wiped after the session ends, and one that contains commands used in all sessions and is persistent. The default location for persistent history can be found in `%userprofile%\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\PowerShell\\PSReadline\\ConsoleHost_history.txt`, but command history can also be accessed with `Get-History`. Command Prompt (CMD) on Windows does not have persistent history.(Citation: Microsoft about_History)(Citation: Medium)\n\nUsers often type usernames and passwords on the command-line as parameters to programs, which then get saved to this file when they log out. Adversaries can abuse this by looking through the file for potential credentials.(Citation: External to DA, the OS X Way)",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1552.003",
        "standard_attck_name": "Shell History",
        "gid": 8146,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [],
        "uncertain_id": "t1552.003",
        "uncertain_name": "shell history",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may search the command history on compromised systems for insecurely stored credentials.\n\nOn Linux and macOS systems, shells such as Bash and Zsh keep track of the commands users type on the command-line with the \"history\" utility. Once a user logs out, the history is flushed to the user's history file. For each user, this file resides at the same location: for example, `~/.bash_history` or `~/.zsh_history`. Typically, these files keeps track of the user's last 1000 commands.\n\nOn Windows, PowerShell has both a command history that is wiped after the session ends, and one that contains commands used in all sessions and is persistent. The default location for persistent history can be found in `%userprofile%\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\PowerShell\\PSReadline\\ConsoleHost_history.txt`, but command history can also be accessed with `Get-History`. Command Prompt (CMD) on Windows does not have persistent history.(Citation: Microsoft about_History)(Citation: Medium)\n\nUsers often type usernames and passwords on the command-line as parameters to programs, which then get saved to this file when they log out. Adversaries can abuse this by looking through the file for potential credentials.(Citation: External to DA, the OS X Way)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 7.44779377854116,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]The entity is the MITRE ATT&CK technique T1552.003, named \"Unsecured Credentials: Bash History,\" which falls under the Credential Access tactic and operates on Linux, macOS, and Windows platforms. This technique involves adversaries searching shell command history files, such as `~/.bash_history` or `~/.zsh_history` on Linux/macOS or the PowerShell history file on Windows, to find insecurely stored credentials that users may have typed as command-line parameters. The retrieved information confirms the core technical mechanics of accessing these persistent history files to harvest credentials, while irrelevant data on command and control protocols, VPSs, and junk data has been discarded."
    },
