            "apt28",
            "ember bear",
            "apt29"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1090.003",
        "uncertain_name": "multi-hop proxy",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may chain together multiple proxies to disguise the source of malicious traffic. Typically, a defender will be able to identify the last proxy traffic traversed before it enters their network; the defender may or may not be able to identify any previous proxies before the last-hop proxy. This technique makes identifying the original source of the malicious traffic even more difficult by requiring the defender to trace malicious traffic through several proxies to identify its source.\n\nFor example, adversaries may construct or use onion routing networks – such as the publicly available [Tor](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0183) network – to transport encrypted C2 traffic through a compromised population, allowing communication with any device within the network.(Citation: Onion Routing) Adversaries may also use operational relay box (ORB) networks composed of virtual private servers (VPS), Internet of Things (IoT) devices, smart devices, and end-of-life routers to obfuscate their operations.(Citation: ORB Mandiant) \n\nIn the case of network infrastructure, it is possible for an adversary to leverage multiple compromised devices to create a multi-hop proxy chain (i.e., [Network Devices](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584/008)). By leveraging [Patch System Image](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1601/001) on routers, adversaries can add custom code to the affected network devices that will implement onion routing between those nodes. This method is dependent upon the [Network Boundary Bridging](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1599) method allowing the adversaries to cross the protected network boundary of the Internet perimeter and into the organization’s Wide-Area Network (WAN).  Protocols such as ICMP may be used as a transport.  \n\nSimilarly, adversaries may abuse peer-to-peer (P2P) and blockchain-oriented infrastructure to implement routing between a decentralized network of peers.(Citation: NGLite Trojan)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.039322623900976,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Multi-hop Proxy (T1090.003) is a Command and Control technique used across Windows, Linux, macOS, and network platforms to disguise malicious traffic by routing it through a chain of multiple proxies, such as Tor networks, operational relay boxes (ORBs) composed of VPS or IoT devices, or peer-to-peer infrastructure. This makes tracing the traffic's original source difficult, as defenders typically only identify the last proxy in the chain. The technique is related to the parent technique Proxy and has been associated with groups including Gamaredon Group, Volt Typhoon, and APT28.[/profile]"
    },
    "24289": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "command-and-control"
        ],
        "aliases": [
            "Domain Generation Algorithms"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "9b9a6ba0-6e3c-47f5-9fe1-e3fb77953869",
        "name": "Domain Generation Algorithms",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may make use of Domain Generation Algorithms (DGAs) to dynamically identify a destination domain for command and control traffic rather than relying on a list of static IP addresses or domains. This has the advantage of making it much harder for defenders to block, track, or take over the command and control channel, as there potentially could be thousands of domains that malware can check for instructions.(Citation: Cybereason Dissecting DGAs)(Citation: Cisco Umbrella DGA)(Citation: Unit 42 DGA Feb 2019)\n\nDGAs can take the form of apparently random or “gibberish” strings (ex: istgmxdejdnxuyla.ru) when they construct domain names by generating each letter. Alternatively, some DGAs employ whole words as the unit by concatenating words together instead of letters (ex: cityjulydish.net). Many DGAs are time-based, generating a different domain for each time period (hourly, daily, monthly, etc). Others incorporate a seed value as well to make predicting future domains more difficult for defenders.(Citation: Cybereason Dissecting DGAs)(Citation: Cisco Umbrella DGA)(Citation: Talos CCleanup 2017)(Citation: Akamai DGA Mitigation)\n\nAdversaries may use DGAs for the purpose of [Fallback Channels](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1008). When contact is lost with the primary command and control server malware may employ a DGA as a means to reestablishing command and control.(Citation: Talos CCleanup 2017)(Citation: FireEye POSHSPY April 2017)(Citation: ESET Sednit 2017 Activity)",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1637.001",
        "standard_attck_name": "Domain Generation Algorithms",
        "gid": 8181,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "apt41",
            "ta551"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1568.002",
        "uncertain_name": "domain generation algorithms",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may make use of Domain Generation Algorithms (DGAs) to dynamically identify a destination domain for command and control traffic rather than relying on a list of static IP addresses or domains. This has the advantage of making it much harder for defenders to block, track, or take over the command and control channel, as there potentially could be thousands of domains that malware can check for instructions.(Citation: Cybereason Dissecting DGAs)(Citation: Cisco Umbrella DGA)(Citation: Unit 42 DGA Feb 2019)\n\nDGAs can take the form of apparently random or “gibberish” strings (ex: istgmxdejdnxuyla.ru) when they construct domain names by generating each letter. Alternatively, some DGAs employ whole words as the unit by concatenating words together instead of letters (ex: cityjulydish.net). Many DGAs are time-based, generating a different domain for each time period (hourly, daily, monthly, etc). Others incorporate a seed value as well to make predicting future domains more difficult for defenders.(Citation: Cybereason Dissecting DGAs)(Citation: Cisco Umbrella DGA)(Citation: Talos CCleanup 2017)(Citation: Akamai DGA Mitigation)\n\nAdversaries may use DGAs for the purpose of [Fallback Channels](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1008). When contact is lost with the primary command and control server malware may employ a DGA as a means to reestablishing command and control.(Citation: Talos CCleanup 2017)(Citation: FireEye POSHSPY April 2017)(Citation: ESET Sednit 2017 Activity)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 14.73990858546537,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Domain Generation Algorithms (DGA) is a command-and-control technique (MITRE ID T1568.002) used for defense evasion and resilience. It operates on Windows, Linux, macOS, and ESXi platforms. The technique dynamically generates a large number of potential command-and-control domains using an algorithm, often based on time or a seed value, to create seemingly random or word-based domain strings (e.g., istgmxdejdnxuyla.ru). This makes it difficult for defenders to block all possible destinations and can serve as a fallback mechanism to re-establish lost C2 connections. It is distinct from and often used alongside techniques like Dynamic Resolution."
    },
