        "uncertain_id": "t1563",
        "uncertain_name": "remote service session hijacking",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may take control of preexisting sessions with remote services to move laterally in an environment. Users may use valid credentials to log into a service specifically designed to accept remote connections, such as telnet, SSH, and RDP. When a user logs into a service, a session will be established that will allow them to maintain a continuous interaction with that service.\n\nAdversaries may commandeer these sessions to carry out actions on remote systems. [Remote Service Session Hijacking](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1563) differs from use of [Remote Services](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021) because it hijacks an existing session rather than creating a new session using [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078).(Citation: RDP Hijacking Medium)(Citation: Breach Post-mortem SSH Hijack)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 5.844187086458591,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Remote Service Session Hijacking is MITRE ATT&CK technique T1563 under the Lateral Movement tactic, targeting Windows, Linux, and macOS platforms. It involves adversaries taking control of existing, authenticated sessions on remote services like SSH, RDP, or telnet to move laterally, rather than creating new sessions with stolen credentials. A specific method for RDP is using the native Windows tscon.exe command with system permissions to hijack a session without user notification. This technique allows adversaries to assume the privileges and access of the hijacked user session."
    },
    "24344": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "a7179c06-34ea-4de2-9deb-98c22faf27f9",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "DUSTTRAP",
        "is_family": true,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [
            "50124174a4ac0d65bf8b6fd66f538829d1589edc73aa7cf36502e57aa5513360",
            "469b534bec827be03c0823e72e7b4da0b84f53199040705da203986ef154406a",
            "151257e9dfda476cdafd9983266ad3255104d72a66f9265caa8417a5fe1df5d7",
            "3b88b3efbdc86383ee9738c92026b8931ce1c13cd75cd1cda2fa302791c2c4fb"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "apt41"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "dusttrap",
        "uncertain_mes": "[DUSTTRAP](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1159) is a multi-stage plugin framework associated with [APT41](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0096) operations with multiple components.(Citation: Google Cloud APT41 2024)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 7.699500340161678,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]DUSTTRAP is a multi-stage plugin framework malware associated with APT41 operations. Its key technical behaviors include using Process Hollowing (T1055.012) for defense evasion, employing Data Encoding (T1132) and Symmetric Cryptography (T1573.001) for obfuscating command-and-control traffic, and utilizing Binary Padding (T1027.001). It targets Windows platforms and has been observed using infrastructure like trycloudflare.com domains and suspected command-and-control via Google API, as well as using a stolen code signing certificate from CCR Inc.[/profile]"
    },
    "24345": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "e10e853d-25b3-45cc-b970-b5ece03e7d4b",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "Voldemort",
        "is_family": false,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [
            "469b534bec827be03c0823e72e7b4da0b84f53199040705da203986ef154406a",
            "151257e9dfda476cdafd9983266ad3255104d72a66f9265caa8417a5fe1df5d7",
            "50124174a4ac0d65bf8b6fd66f538829d1589edc73aa7cf36502e57aa5513360",
            "3b88b3efbdc86383ee9738c92026b8931ce1c13cd75cd1cda2fa302791c2c4fb"
        ],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "未知"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "voldemort",
        "uncertain_mes": "Key findings\n- Between March and June 2025, Proofpoint Threat Research observed three Chinese state-sponsored threat actors conduct targeted phishing campaigns against the Taiwanese semiconductor industry. In all cases, the motive was most likely espionage.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.363422102592143,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Voldemort is a malware loader, not a family, that uses Process Hollowing to inject malicious code into legitimate suspended processes for evasion. It employs Binary Padding to alter its file checksum and size, and uses Symmetric Cryptography like AES to encrypt its command and control traffic, which has been observed communicating with domains such as ways-sms-pmc-shareholders.trycloudflare.com. It is delivered via User Execution, such as phishing, and has been used as a loader for Cobalt Strike. It has been associated with Chinese state-sponsored threat actors targeting the Taiwanese semiconductor industry for espionage.[/profile]"
    },
