        "uncertain_id": "t1505.003",
        "uncertain_name": "web shell",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may backdoor web servers with web shells to establish persistent access to systems. A Web shell is a Web script that is placed on an openly accessible Web server to allow an adversary to access the Web server as a gateway into a network. A Web shell may provide a set of functions to execute or a command-line interface on the system that hosts the Web server.(Citation: volexity_0day_sophos_FW)\n\nIn addition to a server-side script, a Web shell may have a client interface program that is used to talk to the Web server (e.g. [China Chopper](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0020) Web shell client).(Citation: Lee 2013)",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 11.117689789708086,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]T1505.003, known as Web Shell, is a Server Software Component technique within the Persistence tactic, applicable to Windows, Linux, macOS, and Network Devices. It involves placing a malicious web script on a server to provide persistent backdoor access, often featuring a command-line interface or specific execution functions. This technique is commonly associated with groups like APT28, Hafnium, and Volt Typhoon, and has been linked to vulnerabilities such as CVE-2021-26857 and CVE-2023-32315.[/profile]"
    },
    "24355": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "phase_names": [
            "persistence",
            "privilege-escalation"
        ],
        "aliases": [
            "Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts"
        ],
        "entity_type": "attack-pattern",
        "unique_id": "b4a9586a-f0b6-42c8-a531-769c083692fd",
        "name": "Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts",
        "kill_chain_names": [
            "mitre-attack",
            "mitre-attack"
        ],
        "description": "Adversaries may use scripts automatically executed at boot or logon initialization to establish persistence.(Citation: Mandiant APT29 Eye Spy Email Nov 22)(Citation: Anomali Rocke March 2019) Initialization scripts can be used to perform administrative functions, which may often execute other programs or send information to an internal logging server. These scripts can vary based on operating system and whether applied locally or remotely.  \n\nAdversaries may use these scripts to maintain persistence on a single system. Depending on the access configuration of the logon scripts, either local credentials or an administrator account may be necessary. \n\nAn adversary may also be able to escalate their privileges since some boot or logon initialization scripts run with higher privileges.",
        "x_mitre_description": "",
        "platforms": [
            "macos",
            "linux",
            "windows",
            "Network Devices",
            "ESXi"
        ],
        "standard_attck_id": "T1398",
        "standard_attck_name": "Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts",
        "gid": 8247,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "unc3886",
            "apt41",
            "rocke",
            "apt29"
        ],
        "uncertain_id": "t1037",
        "uncertain_name": "boot or logon initialization scripts",
        "uncertain_mes": "Adversaries may use scripts automatically executed at boot or logon initialization to establish persistence.(Citation: Mandiant APT29 Eye Spy Email Nov 22)(Citation: Anomali Rocke March 2019) Initialization scripts can be used to perform administrative functions, which may often execute other programs or send information to an internal logging server. These scripts can vary based on operating system and whether applied locally or remotely.  \n\nAdversaries may use these scripts to maintain persistence on a single system. Depending on the access configuration of the logon scripts, either local credentials or an administrator account may be necessary. \n\nAn adversary may also be able to escalate their privileges since some boot or logon initialization scripts run with higher privileges.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.643361133281065,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts is MITRE ATT&CK technique T1037, used for Persistence and Privilege Escalation on platforms including Windows, Linux, macOS, and ESXi. Adversaries abuse scripts that execute automatically at system boot or user logon to maintain access, with execution often triggered by modifications to system-specific mechanisms like the Windows Registry (e.g., HKCU\\Environment\\UserInitMprLogonScript), Unix RC files (e.g., rc.local), or network logon scripts via Active Directory. Since these scripts frequently run with elevated privileges, they can also enable privilege escalation. Associated threat groups include APT29, APT41, and UNC3886."
    },
    "24356": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "704f3e3b-053c-4ec7-9ae7-7ce69f899d11",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "MS Edge password stealer",
        "is_family": false,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "mirage"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "ms exchange tool",
        "uncertain_mes": "Royal APT - APT15 - Related Information from NCC Group Cyber Defense Operations Research\nAmong the tools developed by the group for the victim, APT15 created a .net tool to enumerate the victim's sharepoint database. Below is an screen-shot from the decompiled binary.\nDecoder scripts for BS2005 and RoyalCLI samples found by NCC Group can be found in the scripts directory.\nbs_decoder.py",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.4058895995557,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]MS Edge password stealer, also known as GodRat, is a stealer malware attributed to APT41. It targets the Microsoft Edge browser, extracting saved passwords by accessing the SQLite database file at \"%LOCALAPPDATA%\\\\Google\\\\Chrome\\\\User Data\\\\Default\\\\Login Data\" and the encryption key from the \"Local State\" file. It stores collected data in a file named \"edge.txt\" and uses persistence mechanisms like Boot or Logon Autostart Execution. Its command-and-control traffic employs encryption (T1573) and data encoding (T1132), and it has been deployed against targets in locations including Lebanon, Jordan, Malaysia, Hong Kong, and the United Arab Emirates.[/profile]"
    },
    "24357": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "520477a4-b583-4b22-a630-fc3767254794",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "Chrome password stealer",
        "is_family": false,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "sweed",
            "the gorgon group",
            "cobalt"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "loki password stealer (pws)",
        "uncertain_mes": "Intro\nI’m going to make my first post an easy one. I’m currently in the middle of writing up my GREM Gold paper, which focuses on the reverse engineering of a Loki-Bot v1.8 sample. This post is going to focus on how Loki-Bot creates its mutex and the folders, files, and registry keys that are created as a result.\nPer PhishMe:",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 12.308111119792425,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]Chrome password stealer is a malware that steals stored credentials from the Google Chrome browser. Its key technical behaviors include data staging (T1074), clipboard data capture (T1115), data encoding for command and control (T1132), achieving persistence via boot or logon autostart execution (T1547), and input capture (T1056). It has been associated with groups like APT41 and has been observed targeting users in locations including Lebanon, Jordan, Malaysia, Hong Kong, and the United Arab Emirates.[/profile]"
    },
    "24358": {
        "semantic": 1,
        "unique_id": "b56d5838-5713-4c32-872a-7628f6d2787a",
        "entity_type": "malware",
        "name": "GodRAT",
        "is_family": false,
        "no_semantic_neighbors": [],
        "uncertain_related_groups": [
            "apt41"
        ],
        "uncertain_name": "godrat",
        "uncertain_mes": "In September 2024, we detected malicious activity targeting financial (trading and brokerage) firms through the distribution of malicious .scr (screen saver) files disguised as financial documents via Skype messenger. The threat actor deployed a newly identified Remote Access Trojan (RAT) named GodRAT, which is based on the Gh0st RAT codebase.",
        "processed": true,
        "hsage": 13.404315761362112,
        "profile_without_enhance_5": "[profile]GodRAT is a Remote Access Trojan (RAT) based on the Gh0st RAT codebase, attributed to the APT41 group. It is distributed via malicious .scr files disguised as financial documents through Skype messenger, primarily targeting financial firms in locations including Lebanon, Jordan, Malaysia, Hong Kong, and the United Arab Emirates. Its key TTPs include Boot or Logon Autostart Execution for persistence, Input Capture for credential theft, Clipboard Data collection, Data Staging prior to exfiltration, and Data Encoding for command-and-control traffic obfuscation. Associated tools include a dedicated builder, shellcode injectors, and plugins like a file manager and password stealers for Chrome and MSEdge.[/profile]"
    },
